/* Long-form help for rrg_guide.html — one entry per sidebar section, keyed like RRG_FEATS. */
window.RRG_GUIDE = {"brief":{"intro":"The Daily Brief reads your listings, deals, agreements, LOIs and tasks and writes a short, ranked list of what to do today.","overview":["Open <b>Daily Brief</b> from My Work in the left nav. The AI assistant pulls a live snapshot of your book — listings and their expiration dates, deals under contract or closing, commissions owed, agreements about to lapse, recent LOIs and your dated open tasks — and turns it into a headline, a <b>Do today</b> list, a <b>This week</b> heads-up and one <b>Play to win</b> line.","It only works from what is in the system: it is told never to invent a name, number or date. If your pipeline is quiet it says so and points you to prospecting. Each run is a metered AI call, so the page shows your last brief while it's still current and <b>↻ Refresh</b> runs a new one."],"feats":{"today":{"lead":"The top of the brief is a short list of the things that matter most right now.","body":["Under <b>Do today</b> you get three to seven actions, most urgent first. Each has a one-line <i>what</i>, a line of detail that names the business, party and number, and an urgency pill: <b>high</b> (red), <b>med</b> (gold) or <b>low</b> (grey).","Above the list is a one-line headline for the day, and a tag that says whose numbers you are looking at (the person's name followed by <b>· brief</b>, or <b>Firm-wide brief</b>) so a brief never reads as yours when it isn't."],"tip":"Treat the high items as your first three calls of the morning."},"reads":{"lead":"The brief is built from a live snapshot of your book, not a template.","body":["Each run collects: your listings with status, value and days to expiration; any deal on them with price, expected close and commission status; your open tasks that have a due date (overdue ones flagged), up to 25; agreements that expire within the next 60 days; and your 15 most recent LOIs.","It also counts firm-wide spaces (total, available, LOI out). Spaces have no owner, so that block is firm-wide in every view and the brief is told to say so if it cites it.","The AI is instructed to ground every line in that data and never invent a name, number or date. Tasks with no due date are not included, so date your tasks if you want them weighed."],"tip":"If a listing or deal looks missing, check that it is assigned to you — the brief only reads what you own."},"week":{"lead":"Below today's list the brief looks a few days out.","body":["<b>This week</b> lists up to five heads-up items — things that aren't urgent today but will be soon, like an agreement lapsing Friday or a closing next week.","<b>Play to win</b> is a single strategic line at the bottom: where the brief thinks your effort pays off most."]},"spaces":{"lead":"For tenant-rep work, the brief checks fresh inventory against your clients' criteria.","body":["Every run looks at spaces added (or flagged to the client) in the last 7 days and scores each against the site criteria on your tenant engagements. Anything that clears the fit threshold is handed to the brief with the tenant, the space and the top reasons it fits — up to 12 matches, best first.","Only engagements with criteria filled in are checked, so fill in the criteria on the tenant engagement if you want matches."]},"rerun":{"lead":"Opening the page shows a brief straight away — your last one if it's still current, otherwise a fresh run.","body":["The top of the page shows <b>Generated …</b> with the time of the brief you're reading, and a <span class=\"kbd\">↻ Refresh</span> button. Opening the page reuses your last brief for the same view when it was made today and the pipeline hasn't changed, or when it's under an hour old. If the pipeline has moved since, the line adds <b>pipeline has changed since — Refresh to update</b>.","<span class=\"kbd\">↻ Refresh</span> always runs a new brief. It asks you to confirm first, because each run is a metered AI call. Tick <b>Don't ask again for this</b> in that box to skip the prompt next time (remembered on this browser).","While a run works you see what it is reading (listings, deals, agreements, tasks) and a <span class=\"kbd\">Cancel</span> button. Cancel stops it and shows <b>Run the brief →</b> to start again. If you see <i>not configured</i>, an admin needs to add the Anthropic API key in Admin settings."],"tip":"One run in the morning is usually enough — come back later and the page shows it again without a new AI call."},"scope":{"lead":"People who can see every record get a switch above the brief to change whose book it reads.","body":["The bar shows <span class=\"kbd\">Mine</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Everyone</span> and a <b>Someone else…</b> picker. <b>Everyone</b> builds a firm-wide brief across every rep's listings and tasks; picking a person builds that rep's brief. A note under the bar says whose book you are looking at.","Your choice is remembered and put in the page address, so a link like <i>rrg_brief.html?scope=avery</i> opens that person's brief directly.","The switch appears for admins and for anyone whose role has <b>See all records</b>. Everyone else only ever gets their own brief, whatever the link says."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why didn't my overdue task show up?","a":"The brief only reads open tasks that have a due date, and only the first 25 by date. Undated tasks are skipped."},{"q":"Does it make things up?","a":"It is told to ground every line in your data and never invent a name, number or date. If something looks wrong, check the record it names."},{"q":"Does opening the page cost an AI call?","a":"Only when it runs a new brief. Opening the page reuses your last brief for the same view if it's from today and the pipeline hasn't changed, or if it's under an hour old. <span class=\"kbd\">↻ Refresh</span> always runs a new one, and each run is a metered AI call."}]},"inbox":{"intro":"Review is your confirm-before-write queue: the AI reads emails you label in Gmail and proposes new leads, contact and deal updates, and files to save — nothing is written until you confirm.","overview":["Open <b>Review</b> from My Work. Label an email in Gmail with your intake label (<b>Lavano</b> unless you've saved your own — the status bar shows which) and the AI reads it. A stranger becomes a <b>New lead</b> card, a contact you already have becomes an <b>Update</b> card, and PDF or Word attachments become a <b>File</b> card.","Every card shows the sender, subject and the start of the email so you can see what it read. Edit anything, then confirm or dismiss. The number on the Review item in the left nav is how many are waiting."],"feats":{"leads":{"lead":"The intake reads the email signature so you don't retype a business card.","body":["Put your intake label on an email in Gmail. The AI picks the one useful new contact — normally the sender, read from their signature; for a forwarded intro it takes the person being introduced. It ignores your own signature, list footers and disclaimers.","The <b>New lead</b> card shows first and last name, email, phone, title, company, an <b>Interest</b> guess and a one-line note, plus a <b>Read confidence</b> % for how sure the AI is of what it read. No phone or address is invented if it isn't in the email.","If no company is named but the sender uses a business domain, the company is inferred from the domain; free mail like Gmail or Yahoo never is. If the sender is already in your book you get an Update card instead (see <a href=\"#updates\">Updates to contacts you know</a>)."],"tip":"The status bar tells you the exact label name it watches — new users start on <b>Lavano</b>; a label you saved earlier keeps working as-is."},"confirm":{"lead":"Each card is a draft until you confirm it.","body":["Edit any field on the card, set <b>Interest</b>, and add to the <b>Note</b>. <span class=\"kbd\">Confirm & add</span> creates (or updates) the contact, finds or creates the company, logs the note on the contact and tags it <i>Inbox review</i>. If you picked a follow-up automation for Gmail intake, the contact is enrolled in it.","<span class=\"kbd\">Dismiss</span> drops the card; that email won't be proposed again.","If you leave <b>Interest</b> blank on a brand-new contact it stays blank — set it before confirming if you want the contact typed."]},"updates":{"lead":"When a known contact emails, the AI looks for news instead of making a duplicate.","body":["The AI reads the email against the contact and their open deals and proposes only real changes: contact <b>Title</b>, <b>Phone</b>, <b>Email</b> or <b>Interest</b>, and on a deal the <b>Deal status</b>, <b>Asking / price</b>, <b>Expected close</b> or <b>Terms</b>.","Each line shows the old value struck through, an arrow, and the new value in an editable box. Untick a line to skip it, change the value if needed, add a note, then <span class=\"kbd\">Confirm & apply</span>.","New terms are appended to the deal's existing terms rather than replacing them. A deal status only changes to a valid status."]},"files":{"lead":"LOIs, NDAs and flyers that arrive by email get filed without download-and-upload.","body":["Any PDF, DOC or DOCX on a labeled email becomes a <b>File</b> card. The type is guessed from the file name and subject — LOI, NDA, Agreement, Flyer, Lease, Financials or General — and you can retype it.","<b>File onto</b> defaults to the sender's deal when they have exactly one open deal, otherwise to the sender's contact; you can pick another of their deals, the sender's <b>Company</b>, or <b>Just Documents (no link)</b>. Untick any file you don't want, then <span class=\"kbd\">Confirm & file</span>.","The files are pulled from Gmail at that moment (25 MB max each); when you file onto the contact, a note is logged there too. Attachments always come through Review — they are never filed automatically."]},"scan":{"lead":"The status bar at the top tells you whether the inbox is being watched.","body":["A green dot and <b>Watching your inbox</b> means auto-intake is on and it checks your labeled mail on its own interval. <b>Inbox watching is off</b> or <b>Inbox not connected</b> tell you what to fix on your Account page.","<span class=\"kbd\">Scan now</span> checks right away and reports what it found — for example how many leads, updates and attachments are ready, or that every labeled email was already processed.","Each email is only read once. The first scan looks back 60 days, later scans 30."],"tip":"If Scan now says no emails were found, add the label to a few emails in Gmail first."},"autoadd":{"lead":"The <b>Confirm before adding</b> checkbox in the status bar controls whether leads wait for you.","body":["On (the default), every lead and update comes here first.","Off, new leads are written straight into your book — tagged, logged and enrolled in your intake follow-up. In that mode a known contact is simply refreshed rather than shown as an Update card. Attachments still wait here either way."]},"recent":{"lead":"Open <b>Recently handled</b> at the bottom of the page to see what you've already decided.","body":["Each row shows who it was, what happened (<b>Added</b>, <b>Updated</b>, <b>Filed</b> or <b>Dismissed</b>) and an <b>open</b> link to the contact when there is one. It keeps your last 30 decisions."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Can a teammate see my Review queue?","a":"No. The queue is per person — you only see items read from your own Gmail."},{"q":"I removed the label in Gmail — will it come back?","a":"Each email is only processed once, so removing or re-adding the label won't create a second card."},{"q":"Why didn't an email turn into a card?","a":"If the AI couldn't find a real person in it, or a known contact's email had nothing new to update, it is skipped."}]},"tasks":{"intro":"Tasks is your follow-up list: who you owe a call, an NDA or a CIM, when it's due, and which contact or listing it belongs to.","overview":["Open <b>Tasks</b> from My Work. The list shows your open tasks — ones assigned to you or that you created — with due date and time, priority, the linked contact and company, and the <b>Owner</b> it's assigned to. Overdue dates turn red.","The Tasks item in the left nav carries counts for open tasks, late ones and ones due today, so you can see your load from any page."],"feats":{"create":{"lead":"Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New task</span> to open the task form.","body":["Type what needs doing in <b>Task</b>. As you type it suggests titles — the ones you and the team use most, plus broker staples like <i>Send listing agreement to</i>, <i>Send CIM to</i>, <i>Request P&L from</i> and <i>Draft LOI for</i>.","Set <b>Due date</b> and <b>Due time</b> (time defaults to 9:00 AM), <b>Priority</b> (Low, Normal, High), an optional activity <b>Type</b>, and <b>Assign to</b> — yourself or any teammate.","Add <b>Notes</b> if needed and hit <span class=\"kbd\">Save task</span>. You can also start a task from the <b>+</b> create menu in the top bar on any page."],"steps":["Click <b>+ New task</b>.","Type the task and pick a due date and time.","Choose who it's assigned to and, if useful, <b>Link to</b> a contact or listing.","Click <b>Save task</b>."]},"link":{"lead":"Use <b>Link to</b> and <b>Which one</b> on the task form.","body":["Pick <b>Contact</b> or <b>Listing / Assignment</b> under <b>Link to</b>, then choose the record. A task linked to a contact shows that contact and their company; a task linked to a listing shows the listing's client and company.","The contact name in the list is a link straight to the contact's page, so you can call from there.","Linking matters beyond this page: when a task comes due, the Feed's Keep things moving rail shows it with the linked contact."]},"done":{"lead":"The checkbox at the start of each row marks the task complete.","body":["Ticking it stamps the <b>Completed</b> date; ticking a done task again reopens it. Use <span class=\"kbd\">Open</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Done</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">All</span> above the list to switch views — All shows open and completed tasks together.","The Tasks item in the left nav shows your open count, a late count and a due-today count, and they update as you check things off."]},"repeat":{"lead":"Set <b>Repeat</b> on the task form for anything you do on a schedule.","body":["Choices are <b>Daily</b>, <b>Weekly</b>, <b>Every 2 weeks</b>, <b>Monthly</b>, <b>Monthly — last day</b>, <b>Quarterly</b> and <b>Yearly</b>. Repeating tasks show a 🔁 tag in the list.","When you complete one, the next is created right away with its due date moved forward and the reminder moved with it. If you don't complete it, the next one still opens once the due date passes — a monthly check-in keeps coming whether or not last month's got done. Each task only ever spawns one successor."],"tip":"Use Monthly — last day for month-end reporting; it always lands on the month's last day, whether that's the 28th, 29th, 30th or 31st."},"remind":{"lead":"Pick a <b>Reminder</b> and how to <b>Send reminder via</b> on the task form.","body":["Reminder choices are <b>At due date/time</b>, <b>10 minutes before</b>, <b>30 minutes before</b>, <b>1 hour before</b>, <b>1 day before</b> or <b>Custom time…</b>.","Channels are <b>Pop-up</b> (a browser notification while the app is open — allow notifications when your browser asks), <b>Email</b> and <b>SMS</b>. Email and SMS are greyed out until an admin sets them up.","The reminder goes to the person the task is assigned to.","Due dates, reminder times and what counts as today or overdue all run on the firm's time zone, set by an admin in <b>Settings → Calendar</b> (Central time unless changed)."]},"inline":{"lead":"You rarely need to open the form to make a change.","body":["Click the <b>Task</b> or <b>Priority</b> cell on any row to edit it in place. <span class=\"kbd\">Enter</span> saves, <span class=\"kbd\">Esc</span> backs out.","<span class=\"kbd\">Edit</span> on a row opens the full form. While you're editing an existing task, changes save on their own a second after you stop typing."]},"bulk":{"lead":"Select rows with the list's row checkboxes to get the bulk bar.","body":["Choose <span class=\"kbd\">✓ Mark complete</span> or <span class=\"kbd\">Delete selected</span>. Bulk complete also opens the next occurrence of any repeating task.","You can only delete tasks you created (admins can delete any); others are skipped and the message tells you how many."]},"tools":{"lead":"The list has the same tools as Contacts and Companies.","body":["<b>Search tasks…</b> matches every word you type across the title, notes, priority, type, linked contact, company and assignee — so <i>nda smith</i> finds the NDA task for Smith.","<span class=\"kbd\">Filter</span> narrows by any column; <span class=\"kbd\">Saved</span> stores the current filters, columns and sort under a name (on this browser); <span class=\"kbd\">Columns</span> shows or hides columns such as <b>Assignee</b> and <b>Type</b>.","<span class=\"kbd\">Export</span> (CSV) and <span class=\"kbd\">Print</span> appear if your role is allowed to export."]},"cleanup":{"lead":"The <span class=\"kbd\">🧹 Clear completed imports</span> button (<b>🧹 Clear my completed imports</b> for reps) shows only when there's something to clear, with a count.","body":["It deletes completed tasks that have no contact or listing link. Open tasks and anything linked to a record are kept. You're asked to confirm, and it can't be undone.","Reps clear only completed imports they created; tasks other people created are kept. Admins clear everyone's."]},"everyone":{"lead":"Admins get an <span class=\"kbd\">Everyone</span> button next to <b>My tasks</b>.","body":["<b>Everyone</b> lists every task in the firm, so you can see who's behind and reassign work with <b>Assign to</b>. The <b>Owner</b> column shows who each task is assigned to (or who created it, if no one is).","Reps only ever see tasks assigned to them or that they created."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why don't my tasks show on the Calendar?","a":"Tasks are kept off the calendar by design. They live here, on the contact's Tasks card, in the Daily Brief and in the Feed's Keep things moving rail."},{"q":"I assigned a task to a teammate — can I still see it?","a":"Yes. My tasks shows tasks assigned to you and tasks you created."},{"q":"Why can't I delete a task?","a":"Only the person who created it, or an admin, can delete it."}]},"calendar":{"intro":"The Calendar is the team's shared schedule — tours, buyer calls, seller meetings and follow-ups — tied to your contacts, with real calendar invites, reminders and online booking.","overview":["Open <b>Calendar</b> from My Work. Every meeting booked here, from a contact's page or through your online booking page lands on the same calendar, colored by whose it is. Meetings linked to a contact also show on that contact's timeline.","The left rail has a mini month, the color key and working hours; the toolbar steps through time, switches views, picks whose calendar to show and syncs with Google."],"feats":{"schedule":{"lead":"Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New meeting</span>, or click a day in Month view, to open the meeting form.","body":["Give it a <b>Title</b>, link a <b>Contact</b> (start typing a name), pick the <b>Type</b>, <b>Date</b>, <b>Time</b> and <b>End</b>. <b>Location</b> searches places and addresses as you type — arrow keys and Enter pick one — or paste a video link or phone number.","Add <b>Notes</b> for the agenda. Once the meeting is saved, <span class=\"kbd\">📎 Attach file</span> keeps pre-reads and documents on it (20 MB each).","A meeting linked to a contact is logged on that contact's timeline as Scheduled or Updated."],"steps":["Click <b>+ New meeting</b>.","Enter a title and pick the contact.","Set date, time, end and location.","Click <b>Save meeting</b>."]},"views":{"lead":"Switch views with <span class=\"kbd\">Day</span> <span class=\"kbd\">Week</span> <span class=\"kbd\">Month</span> <span class=\"kbd\">Agenda</span> and move with <span class=\"kbd\">‹</span> <span class=\"kbd\">Today</span> <span class=\"kbd\">›</span>.","body":["Month shows up to four meetings a day with a <b>+N more</b> when it's busy; Agenda lists everything coming up, grouped by day.","The mini calendar in the left rail dots days that have something on them. Click a date there to jump to it — from Month view it opens that day.","Each rep has a fixed color; the <b>Calendars</b> key in the left rail says whose is whose. A struck-through meeting was cancelled."]},"invites":{"lead":"Use <b>Attendees</b> on the meeting form for people outside the group.","body":["Add each guest's name and email (<span class=\"kbd\">+ Add attendee</span> for more). Tick <b>Email a calendar invite to attendees now</b> before saving, or use <span class=\"kbd\">Send invite</span> on a saved meeting to send or re-send it.","The invite carries a calendar file, the meeting details and any Google Meet link, and replies go to you. The time is stamped with the firm's time zone, so it lands at the right hour for a guest in any time zone. The invite option only shows once an admin has set up email.","If the meeting has no contact linked, the first outside guest is added to your contacts automatically and linked to the meeting. Teammates are never added as contacts."]},"reminders":{"lead":"Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ Add a reminder</span> on the meeting form.","body":["Each reminder can be <b>At start time</b>, 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes, 1, 2 or 3 hours, 1 or 2 days, or <b>1 week before</b>. Add up to 8 — say a day before and 30 minutes before.","Under <b>Notify by</b> pick <b>Pop-up</b>, <b>Email</b> and/or <b>Text</b>. A pop-up appears for the meeting's organizer as a browser notification while the app is open (allow notifications when your browser asks); email is the reliable backstop. If you've sent the meeting invite, your guests get an email reminder too.","Reminder times run on the firm's time zone, which an admin sets under <b>Settings → Calendar</b> (Central time unless changed)."]},"recurring":{"lead":"Set <b>Repeat</b> and <b>Occurrences</b> when you create the meeting.","body":["Choose <b>Weekly</b>, <b>Every 2 weeks</b> or <b>Monthly</b> and how many times (up to 60, 12 by default). Each occurrence is a real meeting with its own reminders, so you can move one without touching the rest.","Repeat is only offered when creating. When you delete one in a series you're asked whether to remove only that one or that one and all future ones; past meetings stay on the record."]},"scope":{"lead":"Use the calendar picker in the toolbar.","body":["If you can see other users' calendars, the picker offers <b>Everyone's calendar</b>, <b>Just my meetings</b> or any one rep. Otherwise you see your own meetings plus any teammate who has shared their calendar with you, under <b>My + shared calendars</b>. Share yours from your Account page.","Pick a teammate and the left rail shows their working days, hours and location, so you can schedule around them. Your own card has an <b>Edit</b> link to set yours.","You can open anyone's meeting you can see, but only its owner can delete it."]},"meet":{"lead":"Click <span class=\"kbd\">🎥 Add Google Meet</span> on the meeting form.","body":["It creates the event on your Google Calendar with a Meet link and shows it on the meeting with a <span class=\"kbd\">Copy</span> button. If the meeting isn't saved yet it's saved first (it needs a title and date).","The button only appears when your Google account is connected with Calendar access on your Account page."]},"gsync":{"lead":"The <span class=\"kbd\">⟳ Google</span> button syncs both ways on demand.","body":["Click it and the bar under the toolbar reports how many events came in new, how many were updated from Google and how many were pushed out. A large first sync can take a moment.","A green dot on the button means you're ready to sync. An amber one means Google isn't connected or Calendar permission wasn't granted — the bar tells you to fix it on your Account page.","Sync runs when you click; it isn't continuous. The button is hidden if Google isn't set up or an admin has turned sync off."],"tip":"Sync before a busy week so meetings booked on your phone show up for the team."},"notetaker":{"lead":"Open a saved meeting and click <span class=\"kbd\">🎙 Record with Notetaker</span>.","body":["The Notetaker opens in a new tab, records the call or meeting, transcribes it and drafts a recap you can edit and save.","Once a meeting has a recording, the button becomes <b>Open Notetaker</b>, the form shows how many recordings are on file, and Day and Agenda views mark the meeting with a red <b>🎙 Recap</b>.","Tell everyone on the call they're being recorded before you start."]},"booking":{"lead":"Turn on your booking page on your Account page and share the link.","body":["Visitors pick a meeting type and an open time inside your working hours, and the meeting appears here automatically — red-edged with a 🔗 and a <b>Booked online</b> tag so it stands out.","The Calendar item in the left nav counts new bookings since you last opened the Calendar; opening the page clears it.","Buffers, minimum notice, per-type questions and guest reschedule/cancel are all set up with the booking page — see the full guide."]},"automation":{"lead":"Use <b>When scheduled, run automation (optional)</b> on the meeting form.","body":["Pick any active automation — for example a pre-tour reminder or a post-meeting thank-you sequence. When you save a new meeting, the linked contact (or the outside guest you added by email) is enrolled in it.","It runs on new meetings only; changing it on an existing meeting won't enroll anyone again."]},"emails":{"lead":"Blue 📧 chips are scheduled sends; green ✓📧 chips are campaigns that finished sending.","body":["Click either to see the subject, audience, sender, a preview of the message and — for sent ones — sent, opens, clicks and unsubscribes. Scheduled ones show the estimated number of recipients.","These are read-only here. Admins get an <b>Open in Email Studio</b> button to change or cancel a send."],"tip":"Check the week's sends before booking a big announcement so you don't stack two blasts on one morning."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I delete a meeting?","a":"Only the person who created it can delete it (admins included server-side). You can still open and read it."},{"q":"I don't see the Email a calendar invite option.","a":"It only shows once an admin has set up email sending."},{"q":"Can I book a meeting with someone who isn't a contact?","a":"Yes. Leave Contact blank and add them under Attendees — the first guest is added to your contacts automatically when you save."},{"q":"Do my tasks show on the calendar?","a":"No. Tasks are kept on the Tasks page and the contact's Tasks card."}]},"feed":{"intro":"The Feed is one stream of everything happening across your book — calls, tours, notes, emails, meetings, agreements and data-room visits — with a ranked list of what needs you today.","overview":["Open <b>Feed</b> from My Work. The center column is the activity stream, newest first, with buyer data-room visits and calendar meetings folded in. The right rail ranks what needs you; the left rail holds your saved views, this week's tallies, what's coming up and a pipeline snapshot.","Automated housekeeping is hidden by default so real deal movement stands out. Flip <b>System activity</b> on to see the full audit trail."],"feats":{"stream":{"lead":"The center column is every activity logged on your contacts, in one timeline.","body":["Each row shows the activity type (color-coded dot), the contact and company, who logged it and when, plus the note. Click a row to jump to the contact.","Deal milestones — agreements sent and signed, LOIs sent, received, countered and accepted, contacts added — land here as they happen.","The feed loads the 200 most recent items; filters work within those."]},"buyers":{"lead":"Buyer visits to your confidential data rooms show up in the stream as <b>Data Room</b> rows.","body":["Each row names the buyer and the business, how many documents they opened and how many they downloaded. Clicks within 45 minutes of each other count as one visit, so the stream stays readable.","If the visit touched the money — P&L, profit, financials, tax returns, the lease, rent roll, SDE, EBITDA, balance sheet, revenue, the CIM or offering — the row is tinted and tagged <b>● Hot</b>, and names the key files.","Click the row to open the data room and see the full breakdown."]},"moving":{"lead":"The right rail ranks up to 12 things that need you, most pressing first.","body":["<b>Overdue or due-today tasks</b> come first, then <b>Call</b> for a buyer who opened the financials, <b>Reply to</b> emails sitting in your inbox 2+ days, <b>Re-engage</b> a buyer who went quiet in a data room for 6–30 days, <b>Follow up with</b> someone who hasn't answered you, and <b>Reach out to</b> active buyers and sellers you haven't contacted in 12–150 days — only contacts you're allowed to see.","Each has a one-click action — <b>Open data room</b>, <b>Open seller</b>, <b>Reply</b> in Gmail, <b>Reach out</b> or <b>Open contact</b>.","The × hides a nudge for now; it comes back on reload if it still applies. Email nudges need Gmail connected on your Account page."]},"calendar":{"lead":"Calendar meetings sit in the same stream as your activity.","body":["Anything from about 60 days back to 21 days ahead shows, with future meetings tagged <b>Upcoming</b>. A meeting linked to a contact clicks through to that contact.","Because the stream is newest first, upcoming meetings sit at the very top."]},"glance":{"lead":"Three cards in the left rail give you the numbers without leaving the feed.","body":["<b>This week</b> counts the last 7 days: meetings, calls, agreements and LOIs, tours, data-room visits and distinct hot buyers.","<b>Coming up</b> lists the next five upcoming meetings in the feed, each linking to its contact.","<b>Pipeline</b> shows active listings, open offers, deals in diligence or under contract, and deals expected to close this month — for the listings you can see."]},"filters":{"lead":"The filter row sits above the stream.","body":["Date: <b>Today</b>, <b>Last 7 days</b>, <b>Last 30 days</b>, <b>This quarter</b> or <b>Custom…</b> with from/to dates. Type a name in <b>Any contact / company / listing</b> — it suggests names already in the stream. <b>All types</b> opens a checklist to pick several types at once.","If you can see the whole book you also get <span class=\"kbd\">All activity</span> / <span class=\"kbd\">My activity</span> and an <b>All users</b> picker to narrow to one teammate."]},"views":{"lead":"Set the filters, then click <span class=\"kbd\">＋ Save this view</span> in the left rail.","body":["Name it and press <span class=\"kbd\">Enter</span>. The view appears in the rail with a summary of what it filters; the one matching your current filters is highlighted. Click a view to apply it, × to delete it.","Views are kept on this browser, so they won't follow you to another computer."]},"quiet":{"lead":"The <span class=\"kbd\">System activity</span> button controls the audit trail.","body":["Off (the default), automated notes, to-dos and diligence entries and system events are hidden so you see human and deal activity first.","On, they come back — and back-to-back repeats of the same automated entry on the same contact collapse into a single row ending <b>· +N more</b>. Your choice is remembered on this browser."]},"suggested":{"lead":"<b>Add suggested people</b> in the right rail reads your recent Gmail.","body":["It shows up to five frequent correspondents from the last two months who aren't contacts yet, with how many emails you've traded. No-reply and notification addresses are skipped.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Add</span> to create the contact on the spot. Needs Gmail connected on your Account page."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why don't I see older activity when I pick This quarter?","a":"The feed loads the 200 most recent items and filters within them. On a busy book, open the contact or listing for the full history."},{"q":"I dismissed a nudge and it came back.","a":"The × only hides it until you reload. It returns while the condition still holds — finish the task or make the call to clear it."},{"q":"What makes a buyer Hot?","a":"They opened a money document in a data room: P&L, financials, tax returns, lease, rent roll, SDE/EBITDA, balance sheet, revenue, the CIM or offering."}]},"companies":{"intro":"Companies is the operator side of your book: each restaurant group or owner's business, the concepts it runs, every location, and the people and listings tied to it.","overview":["Find it under <b>Book of Business → Companies</b>. Each row is one operator. Click the name to open the company file, where its concepts, locations, contacts, listings, documents, files and activity all live on one screen.","Most companies are created for you as you work — starting a deal, importing a CRM file, or adding a contact with a company name. Use <span class=\"kbd\">+ New company</span> when you want to set one up by hand, and let the AI build out the concepts and locations."],"feats":{"newco":{"lead":"The New company window captures the company and its primary contact together, so you don't build the record in pieces.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New company</span>. Fill in <b>Company name</b>, <b>Type</b>, <b>Lead source</b>, <b>Owner</b> (defaults to you) and one or more <b>Major metros</b> — press Enter or a comma after each metro.","The <b>Primary contact</b> block is optional. If you enter a first and last name you must also pick at least one <b>Main interest</b>; the contact is created and linked to the company.","<b>Pipeline</b> starts the company as a lead on the board at Unqualified. Leave it on <b>None</b> unless the deal is real — adding a company doesn't have to start a listing. Your admin can make the pipeline required, in which case the hint under the field says so.","Clicking outside the window does not close it, so you won't lose what you typed. Use <span class=\"kbd\">Cancel</span> to back out."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New company</span>.","Type the company name and watch for a duplicate warning.","Add metros, the primary contact and any concept names.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Create company</span> — the company file opens."],"tip":"Arriving from Create New → Company opens this window immediately, before the list finishes loading."},"paste":{"lead":"Paste to fill reads any blob of contact text and fills the New company window for you.","body":["At the top of the New company window, paste an email signature, business card text or a line like \"John Rivera, GM, Blue Agave Cantina, john@blueagave.com, (512) 555-0134, Austin TX\" into <b>Paste to fill</b>, then click <span class=\"kbd\">Read & fill</span>.","It fills the company name, contact first and last name, title, phone and email, adds the city as a metro when it matches one of your markets, and ticks a main interest when it can tell. The duplicate check runs on the name it found.","Review what it filled before you create — the message under the button tells you if you still need to pick a main interest. If it can't find a clear contact it says so and you fill the fields by hand."],"tip":"Press Ctrl+Enter (⌘+Enter on Mac) in the paste box instead of clicking Read & fill."},"dupes":{"lead":"The name field checks your whole book while you type so you don't work the same operator twice.","body":["If the name matches a company already in your book, you see <b>Already in your book</b> with an <span class=\"kbd\">Open it →</span> link and <b>Create company</b> is disabled.","If it's close but not exact, you get <b>Possible matches — did you mean</b> with up to five links. The comparison ignores filler words like LLC, Inc, Group, Restaurant, Grill, Kitchen, Hospitality, so \"Blue Agave Restaurant Group LLC\" still catches \"Blue Agave\". If none of them is it, go ahead and create.","The server checks again on save, so a duplicate that slipped past still offers to open the existing company instead."]},"aibuild":{"lead":"Concept and location setup is the slow part of a new operator; the AI does the web legwork for you.","body":["In the New company window, list the concepts under <b>Concept name(s)</b>, one per line, and optionally the <b>Approx. # locations each</b>. When you click <span class=\"kbd\">Create company</span> the company file opens and the AI builds each concept — website, type, cuisine, price and logo — and finds its locations, with storefront photos when your firm has a Google Maps key.","If the type is <b>Restaurant Group</b>, tick <b>✨ Restaurant group — let AI find every concept</b> and skip typing them. It reads the group's website for every brand it operates, then builds them the same way.","On the company file the same tools sit behind the <span class=\"kbd\">+</span> on the concepts card: <b>Find them for me</b>, <b>I know the brand names</b> (with ✨ Build all), <b>I already have a list</b> (paste brands and addresses — nothing is searched or invented) and <b>Add one by hand</b>.","These are AI web searches and are metered; you're asked to confirm before each run and can cancel while it works. Concepts with no website found are listed so you can add a site and pull their locations."],"tip":"Adding metros helps the AI land on the right brand when names are common."},"hierarchy":{"lead":"The company file mirrors how restaurant groups actually work: one operator, several concepts, many locations.","body":["Each concept is a collapsible block showing its type, price point, markets, website and location count, with <span class=\"kbd\">+ Location</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Edit</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">Remove</span>.","A location record holds status (with a closed / went-dark date), address, phone, date opened, and lease details — site type, leased or owned, square feet, lease commencement and expiration. Mark a <b>★ Flagship</b>, or flag a <b>commissary / central kitchen</b> and choose which concepts it serves.","Locations not tied to a concept collect under <b>Unassigned</b>. File them one by one or use <b>Assign all</b> to move them under a concept in one step.","Once a location is added, its form saves itself as you type."]},"map":{"lead":"Know where an operator's units are and how they're doing without leaving the file.","body":["On the concepts card, the <span class=\"kbd\">Map</span> button opens every located unit on one map. Pins are colored by concept, units at the same address fan out so each is clickable, and the legend lets you show or hide a concept. The map tells you how many locations still lack coordinates.","Locations pick up Google data — rating and review count, operating status, price level and coordinates — shown in the <b>Google data</b> panel on the location with a link to view it on Maps.","Each location holds up to five photos. Upload your own, or click <span class=\"kbd\">⤓ Pull from Google</span> to fetch one; click <span class=\"kbd\">☆ Main</span> to choose the photo that leads. <span class=\"kbd\">⤓ Photos</span> on a concept pulls Google photos for all of that concept's locations at once."],"tip":"⤓ Pull from Google only appears once your firm has a Google Maps key set up."},"glance":{"lead":"Tell a revenue-producing operator from a cold name in one look.","body":["The top of the company file shows <b>Interactions</b>, <b>Last Contacted</b> and <b>Inactive Days</b>, then <b>Active Listings</b>, <b>Closed Deals</b> and <b>Commission</b> from closed deals — hover Commission to see what's still in the pipeline.","The list carries the same signal: <b>Concepts</b>, <b>Contacts</b>, <b>Locations</b>, <b>Listings</b>, <b>Interactions</b> and <b>Inactive days</b> columns you can sort to find the operators going cold."]},"activity":{"lead":"The company keeps its own timeline, and contact activity rolls up into it.","body":["Use <b>Log Activity</b> to record a call, email, tour, meeting or other touch with its date and time, or <b>Create Note</b> for a free-form note.","Choose <b>To-Do</b> as the type and an owner picker appears — the entry becomes a task for that rep.","The <b>Activity</b> card lists notes, emails, tasks and leads for the company and its contacts, newest first. Filter it by type with the dropdown in the card header. Company-level entries can be deleted with the ✕."]},"linked":{"lead":"Everything tied to the operator is one click from the company file.","body":["<b>Contacts</b>: add a new person with <span class=\"kbd\">+</span> or <span class=\"kbd\">🔗 Link</span> someone already in your book. Click the ☆ to make a person the main contact. When a company has only one contact, that person is treated as the main contact automatically.","<b>Listings</b>: every listing tied to the company, with a link to its data room — or <span class=\"kbd\">Set up data room →</span> if it doesn't have one yet.","<b>Documents</b> collects agreements, valuations and other records linked to the company; its <span class=\"kbd\">✍ Add agreement</span> button records a signed agreement or uploads a PDF and sends it for signature. <b>Files</b> holds uploads up to 25 MB — leases, P&Ls and the like.","The right-hand cards can be dragged by their ⠿ handle into the order you like and collapsed with ▾; the layout is remembered on your device."]},"list":{"lead":"The list is built to work your active book, not scroll through every name you ever imported.","body":["When no search or filter is on, the list can focus on companies active within your firm's recency window; the note above the list shows how many are hidden, with <span class=\"kbd\">Show all</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">Show only recently active</span> to switch. Your choice and last search are remembered.","Search matches name, market, type, tags and main contact. <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> narrows by Starred, Type, Market, Owner, Lead source, Tags and Created date range.","The <b>Company</b> name and <b>Type</b> cells edit in place — no need to open the file to fix a typo."]},"admin":{"lead":"Clean-up tools for admins, run from the row checkboxes.","body":["Tick rows in the list and choose <span class=\"kbd\">Change owner…</span> to assign them to one rep or set them to Unassigned.","Choose <span class=\"kbd\">Merge…</span> with two or more selected, pick the record to <b>keep</b>, and the others merge into it — concepts, locations, tags, notes and every linked contact, listing and agreement move over; duplicate concepts and locations are skipped; the extra companies are removed. This cannot be undone.","Users with delete permission also get <span class=\"kbd\">Delete</span> here and the × on each row."]}},"faq":[{"q":"What happens to the listings when I delete a company?","a":"They go with it. Deleting a company removes its concepts, locations and photos and every listing tied to it — records, data rooms and files. Contacts are kept but unlinked. It cannot be undone, and it needs delete permission."},{"q":"Why don't I see the Restaurant group checkbox?","a":"It only shows when the company <b>Type</b> is Restaurant Group. Pick that type and the ✨ option appears above the concept list."},{"q":"Why can't I delete the BizBuySell company?","a":"It's a system record that BizBuySell leads attach to. It's marked <b>System · permanent</b> and is protected from delete, including bulk delete."},{"q":"Who sees which companies?","a":"If your admin has turned on permissions and you don't have see-all access, the list shows only the companies you own."}]},"people":{"intro":"Contacts holds one record per person — buyers, sellers, tenants, landlords, brokers — tied to their company and to every listing, agreement and conversation they're part of.","overview":["Find it under <b>Book of Business → Contacts</b>. Each row is a person; click the name to open the contact record. That record is where you log touches, send email, schedule, match buyers and tenants to deals, and start a listing or a tenant search.","People come in by hand with <span class=\"kbd\">+ New contact</span>, through <span class=\"kbd\">⤓ Import ▾</span> (Gmail, BizBuySell leads, spreadsheets), or automatically as you work deals — a buyer named on a deal or offer is added to your book for you."],"feats":{"buybox":{"lead":"Turn your buyer list into a matching engine.","body":["On a buyer's record, the <b>Buy-Box</b> card captures target concepts, markets, max price, minimum SDE, unit-count range, whether they need real estate, and timeline and financing.","Flip <b>Active</b> on when they're genuinely looking and click <span class=\"kbd\">Save buy-box</span>. The card then lists the listings that match, each with why it matched.","It runs the other way too: a listing shows the active buyers whose buy-box fits it."],"tip":"Only switch Active on for buyers who are really in the market — it keeps the matches worth calling."},"tenantbox":{"lead":"The tenant-rep side of matching: what the client needs in a space, and which open sites fit.","body":["The <b>Tenant-Box</b> card holds one requirement per concept: size range, condition, lease or purchase, required amenities, markets and max rent. Mark it <b>Active</b>, save it with <span class=\"kbd\">Save tenant-box</span> and the card lists matching spaces, strongest first, each tagged with why it matched.","<span class=\"kbd\">✦ Fill from SSC</span> reads the Site Criteria on file for this client and drafts a concept for you to review — no retyping what the client already told you.","A size range is required: a space must fall inside it and match at least one other criterion. Other empty fields are wildcards."]},"selleractions":{"lead":"The seller side of a contact, laid out in the order you actually work it.","body":["The quick buttons under the name — <span class=\"kbd\">Start Seller Screening</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Send an Agreement</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">Start a Listing</span> — cover the first moves.","<span class=\"kbd\">Biz Seller Actions ▾</span> lays out the full path: screening, financials, the seller interview (send it, record it yourself, or skip it), the valuation (BOV), the listing agreement and taking the listing to market. Finished steps get a ✓, and steps that depend on earlier ones stay locked with a note saying what's needed first — for example, the listing agreement waits on a valuation.","Agreements are sent for e-signature from the record and file themselves back onto it when signed."],"tip":"Click View Seller Interview once it comes back for the transcript, debrief and video; ✨ Clean up for buyers turns the raw transcript into a readable version."},"tenantrep":{"lead":"The front door for tenant representation, right on the client's record.","body":["Open <span class=\"kbd\">Tenant Rep Actions ▾</span> on the contact.","<b>Start Tenant Rep Engagement</b> signs the client to a space search and opens the tenant pipeline at Needs Analysis. <b>Start Site Criteria (SSC)</b> captures their space and concept criteria, linked to this record.","<b>Send a Tenant Rep Agreement</b> sits under Paperwork (optional) — many tenant-rep deals are non-exclusive, so no agreement is needed to start."]},"feed":{"lead":"The contact record is the running history of the relationship.","body":["The <b>Activity</b> card has three tabs: <b>Log Activity</b> (a call, tour, meeting or other touch, with date and time — pick To-Do and it becomes a task for the rep you choose), <b>Send Email</b> and <b>Create Note</b>.","<b>Send Email</b> starts from your shared template library, grouped by folder, with Cc/Bcc and the template's attachments. With Gmail connected it sends from your own Gmail and lands in your Sent folder. Every send is logged to the timeline and stamps <b>Last contacted</b>.","Filter the feed by type with the dropdown or tick <b>Condensed</b> for a tighter view. Gmail threads with the contact show on the record too."]},"prep":{"lead":"Walk into the call knowing where things stand.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">✨ Prep for call</span> in the Activity card header. The AI reviews the last 15 activities, the person's listings and agreements, and their notes and tags.","You get a one-line snapshot, the last touch, <b>Move forward</b> items, suggested <b>Openers</b>, and a <b>Watch out</b> note when something needs care.","It's an AI call, so you confirm before it runs. If there isn't much on the record yet, it tells you to log a few activities first."]},"tasks":{"lead":"Keep the next step attached to the person it's about.","body":["The <b>Tasks</b> card takes a follow-up with a date and optional time; the quick chips <span class=\"kbd\">2 hr</span> <span class=\"kbd\">4 hr</span> <span class=\"kbd\">1 day</span> <span class=\"kbd\">3 days</span> <span class=\"kbd\">1 week</span> set the due time in one tap. You can assign it to another rep.","The <b>Calendar</b> card's <span class=\"kbd\">+</span> books a meeting with time, location, attendees and notes. Set a <b>Reminder</b> from at-start to a day before, and tick <b>Email a calendar invite now</b> to send the invite.","On the Contacts list, the <b>Next follow-up</b> column shows each person's earliest open task and turns red when it's overdue."]},"automations":{"lead":"Put a follow-up sequence on autopilot for one person.","body":["On the <b>Automations</b> card, pick one from <b>⟳ Add to an automation…</b> and click <span class=\"kbd\">Enroll</span>.","Each enrollment shows on the card with <span class=\"kbd\">Pause</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Resume</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">Stop</span> controls.","If the list is empty, no automations have been built yet — an admin creates them under Settings → Automations."]},"value":{"lead":"See the money side of the relationship before you pick up the phone.","body":["Above the activity feed the record shows <b>Closed Deals</b> (closed transactions where they were the seller or the buyer), <b>Transaction Volume</b> (the dollar total of those deals) and <b>Commission</b> from the closed listings where they were the client, with pipeline value on hover."]},"flags":{"lead":"Triage your book without opening every record.","body":["Click the <b>VIP</b>, <b>Star</b> or <b>Bad news</b> icon in a row to switch it on or off. The <b>Primary</b> checkbox makes the person their company's main contact.","<b>Description</b>, <b>Main Interests</b> and <b>Last contacted</b> edit right in the cell.","To flag many at once, tick the rows and choose <span class=\"kbd\">Set flags…</span>: set or clear VIP, Star, Bad news and Main contact for all of them, leaving the rest on No change.","Tick one row and choose <span class=\"kbd\">New listing…</span> to start a listing with that person as the client, or use <b>Send agreement</b> at the end of any row."]},"bbs":{"lead":"Stop retyping BizBuySell lead emails.","body":["Open <span class=\"kbd\">⤓ Import ▾</span> → <b>BizBuySell leads</b>. Paste the lead emails, upload the BizBuySell CSV export, or click <span class=\"kbd\">✉ Pull from Gmail</span>. <span class=\"kbd\">Preview</span> shows what it found before you import.","Each lead becomes a buyer contact, an inquiry on the matching listing (matched by Listing #, listing name or Ref ID) and a follow-up task. A lead already on that listing is counted as a duplicate rather than added as a second inquiry.","Tick <b>Auto-check my Gmail</b> and set how often (every 15 minutes by default) and new leads are pulled in the background.","If no listing matches, a stub listing is created from the lead's listing name or number for you to finish setting up — set the BizBuySell Ref ID or Listing # on the right listing before importing to avoid that.","The same menu also has <b>From Gmail contacts</b> and <b>Spreadsheet / CSV</b> for general imports."]},"filters":{"lead":"Build the segment once, reuse it every morning.","body":["<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> narrows by Main interests, Owner (including no owner), Lead source, Tags (must match all), Flags, Created dates and Last contacted dates — each a type-to-search chip field.","<span class=\"kbd\">Save search…</span> names the view and asks whether to share it with the whole team or keep it to yourself. Saved searches appear at the top of the filter window; click one to apply it.","Click the ☆ next to a saved search to make it your default — it applies every time you open Contacts. Search on the list matches name, nickname, company, emails, phones, tags and lead source.","With no search or filter on, Contacts opens on people active in the last 90 days — the note above the list says how many are hidden. Click <b>Show all</b> to see everyone, or <b>Show only recently active</b> to go back; your choice is remembered on this browser."]},"admin":{"lead":"Admin clean-up tools on the list's row checkboxes.","body":["Tick two or more rows and choose <span class=\"kbd\">Merge…</span>. Pick the record to keep; emails, phones, tags, notes, tours and any linked deals or agreements move onto it, and the extras are removed. This cannot be undone.","Tick rows and choose <span class=\"kbd\">Change owner…</span> to assign them to a rep or set them to Unassigned.","Users with delete permission also see <span class=\"kbd\">Delete</span> and the ✕ on each row; names stay on past offers, tours and NDAs."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I create a contact?","a":"First and last name and at least one <b>Main interest</b> are required. If the email is already on someone else, you're offered that contact instead."},{"q":"Where do the emails on a contact come from?","a":"Connect Gmail and your threads with that person show on the record; anything you send from the record is logged and stamps Last contacted."},{"q":"I only see some of the team's contacts.","a":"When your admin turns on permissions and you don't have see-all access, Contacts shows only the people you own. Also check the note above the list — with no filter on, it shows only people active in the last 90 days until you click <b>Show all</b>."},{"q":"Does Start a Listing need an agreement first?","a":"No. <b>New listing…</b> from the list or <b>Start a Listing</b> on the record creates the listing with that person as client; the listing agreement step comes later in Biz Seller Actions."}]},"documents":{"intro":"Documents is the firm's document library: everything the platform generates — agreements, LOIs, valuations, marketing packs, lease abstracts, site criteria, seller screenings and interviews, questionnaires — plus every file your team uploads and the firm-wide forms.","overview":["Find it under <b>Book of Business → Documents</b>. Documents land here on their own as your team works deals; you don't have to file them. Use the type tabs to narrow the list, and click a document's name to open it where it lives.","You can also upload your own files here and tie each one to a contact, company or listing, so it shows up on that contact's or company's record too."],"feats":{"library":{"lead":"Stop hunting through deal files for a document — they all collect here.","body":["The list pulls in agreements, LOIs, valuations (BOVs), marketing packs, site criteria (SSC), seller screening calls, seller interviews, lease abstracts, questionnaires, uploaded files and firm-wide forms, newest first.","Each row shows what it is, who and what it's <b>Linked to</b> (company, contact, listing), the owner, when it was created and its status. Click the name to open it — a generated record opens in its own tool, an uploaded file opens or downloads in a new tab.","Search matches the title, linked company, contact and listing, owner, type and status."],"tip":"Arriving with ?q= in the address (for example from global search) pre-fills the search box."},"tabs":{"lead":"Each document type gets its own view instead of one generic table.","body":["The tabs across the top — <b>All</b>, <b>Agreements</b>, <b>LOIs</b>, <b>Valuations</b>, <b>Marketing Packs</b>, <b>Lease Abstracts</b>, <b>Site Criteria</b>, <b>Seller Screening</b>, <b>Seller Interviews</b>, <b>Questionnaires</b>, <b>Files</b>, <b>Firm-wide</b> — each carry a live count.","Switching tabs swaps the columns: <b>Agreements</b> shows agreement type, party, Effective and Expires; <b>LOIs</b> shows property, parties and deal type; <b>Valuations</b> shows value and basis; <b>Files</b> shows document type, what it relates to, who uploaded it and notes."]},"filterbar":{"lead":"Quick filters right above the list.","body":["The <b>Filter</b> bar has <b>All owners</b>, <b>All document types</b> (the types used on uploaded files) and a created window — Last 7 days, Last 30 days, Last 90 days, Last 12 months or This year.","Active filters are highlighted; <span class=\"kbd\">Clear filters</span> appears when any is on and resets them in one click. Filters stack with the tab and the search box."]},"hidesys":{"lead":"Cut the list down to what people actually uploaded.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">Hide system docs</span> in the filter bar. Everything generated by another tool drops out — agreements, LOIs, valuations, marketing packs, lease abstracts, site criteria, seller screenings, seller interviews and questionnaires — leaving uploaded files and firm-wide forms; the button reads <b>✓ System docs hidden</b> while it's on.","Your choice is remembered on this device. Click it again to bring them back."]},"upload":{"lead":"Put the lease, P&L or COI where everyone can find it.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Upload</span> and choose a PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, image, TXT, CSV or ZIP file up to 25 MB.","Give it a <b>Document name</b> (defaults to the file name), pick a <b>Document type</b> — Lease, Financials, Tax / W-9, Insurance / COI, Offer / LOI and so on — and use <b>Relates to</b> to search for the company, contact or listing it belongs to. Add <b>Notes</b> if useful.","It's stamped with who uploaded it and when. A file linked to a contact or company also appears on that record."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Upload</span>.","Choose the file.","Set the document type.","Search and pick what it relates to.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Upload</span>."]},"saved":{"lead":"Keep the document views you pull up every week.","body":["Pick a tab, type a search and set any filters, then open <span class=\"kbd\">★ Saved</span> to save that view by name. Pick it from the same button later to snap back.","A saved view remembers the tab, the search text, the owner, document-type and date filters, and the Hide system docs toggle."]},"delete":{"lead":"Clean up, but know what you're deleting.","body":["Each row's × removes that document; tick several rows and choose <span class=\"kbd\">Delete selected</span> to remove a batch. You're asked to confirm first.","Deleting here deletes the record itself. Remove a seller screening and its answers are gone; remove an interview and its transcript and debrief go with it; remove an uploaded file and it's gone for everyone.","You need the Delete permission and the document has to be yours (admins can remove anything); firm-wide forms are admin-only. Anything that can't be deleted here is skipped, and anything you're not allowed to delete is counted as not permitted."]},"firmwide":{"lead":"One source of truth for the paperwork everyone needs.","body":["As an admin, click <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Upload</span> and tick <b>Firm-wide form</b>. Choose a <b>Shelf</b> — Document, Agreement or Training — and upload.","The form appears for every user on their dashboard and under the <b>Firm-wide</b> tab here. Only admins can add or remove firm-wide forms."],"tip":"Open the Firm-wide tab before clicking Upload and the Firm-wide box is ticked for you."}},"faq":[{"q":"I generated an agreement on a contact — do I need to file it here?","a":"No. Agreements, LOIs, valuations, marketing packs, lease abstracts, site criteria, screenings, interviews and questionnaires show up here automatically as they're created."},{"q":"Why don't I see my teammates' documents?","a":"If your admin has turned on permissions and you don't have see-all access, Documents shows the documents you created."},{"q":"Where's the lease abstract I built?","a":"On the <b>Lease Abstracts</b> tab — or search by business name."}]},"board":{"intro":"Listings is the sell-side pipeline board: every listing you're working, laid out by stage, with value and commission totals at the top of each column.","overview":["Listings sits under Business Sales in the sidebar. Each card is one listing; drag it to a new column to change its stage, or switch to <b>List</b> to work the same listings as a spreadsheet with inline edits and bulk actions. Click a card's name to open the full listing file.","The header shows how many listings are active, their total value, and how many are parked off the board. Reps see the listings they created or are the Owner on; admins and anyone with the see-all permission see the whole team, but see-all users can only change their own. The <b>Pipeline</b> selector switches to any other board (Space & Assets, Landlord Rep, Property Sales …) or jumps to the Buyers board."],"feats":{"drag":{"lead":"Move a listing through the pipeline by dragging its card from one column to another.","body":["Grab any card and drop it on a column. The stage saves immediately and the card's <b>days in stage</b> count starts over from today. A listing you can see but don't own (see-all access) can't be dragged — hover it for the <b>View only</b> note. If a save fails, the board reloads and the card snaps back.","A listing that has never been placed on a stage is positioned by how far its work has progressed, so a listing with its data room and seller call done shows up past the first column even before anyone drags it."],"steps":["Find the card in its current column.","Drag it onto the column for the new stage and let go.","Watch for the card to settle — the column counts and dollar totals update with it."],"tip":"Want to set several listings to the same stage? Use <a href=\"#bulk\">bulk edit</a> in List view instead of dragging one at a time."},"newlisting":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">+ New Listing</span> creates a complete listing file from four fields.","body":["Type the business name, pick a <b>Market</b>, type the <b>Seller / client contact</b> and choose the <b>Owner / assigned to</b> rep (defaults to you). The contact box searches your book as you type — arrow keys and Enter pick a match.","The hint under the form tells you what will happen: <b>Linking to existing client</b> means the listing joins that contact's record; <b>New client</b> means a contact will be created. A seller company is opened (or reused) for the business, the confidential data room is built with its standard folders, and the listing gets its RRG listing number.","New listings start with status <b>Unqualified</b> in the first stage of the pipeline, and you land on the listing file as soon as it's created. A seller contact is required — every listing has to tie back to a person."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New Listing</span> at the right end of the toolbar.","Fill in <b>Business / listing name</b>, <b>Market</b>, <b>Seller / client contact</b> and <b>Owner / assigned to</b>.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Create listing</span>."],"tip":"Pick the existing contact from the dropdown rather than retyping the name, so you don't end up with a duplicate person."},"listview":{"lead":"The <span class=\"kbd\">☰ List</span> toggle shows the same filtered listings as a sortable table you can edit without opening each file.","body":["Columns: Listing, Name, Stage, Contact, Market, BOV, Listing Price, Commission, Status, Owner and Last activity (BBS # is available but hidden). Click a column header to sort.","Click a <b>Stage</b>, <b>Market</b>, <b>Listing Price</b>, <b>Commission</b>, <b>Status</b> or <b>Owner</b> cell to edit it; Enter saves, Esc cancels. Cells on a listing you can only view stay locked. <b>BOV</b> is editable only when the value wasn't produced by a BOV — a BOV-backed value stays locked to the valuation.","Use <b>Columns</b> to show, hide and reorder columns. <b>Export</b> (CSV) and <b>Print</b> appear only for users with export permission and cover the ticked rows, or the whole filtered list if none are ticked."],"tip":"The board remembers whether you last used Board or List, so it opens the way you left it."},"bulk":{"lead":"In List view, tick the rows you want and use the bulk actions bar.","body":["Bulk actions: <b>Set status…</b>, <b>Set stage…</b>, <b>Set owner…</b>, <b>Set market…</b> and <b>Delete selected</b>. Pick a value from the menu and it's applied to every ticked listing; a note tells you how many saved.","Owner and market menus include a <b>— Clear —</b> choice to blank the field. Stage choices come from the current pipeline.","<b>Delete selected</b> asks you to confirm and removes the listings and their records for good. It needs the delete permission, and you can only delete listings you can see."],"steps":["Switch to <span class=\"kbd\">☰ List</span>.","Tick the listings to change.","Choose the bulk action, then the value."]},"offboard":{"lead":"The <b>Off the board</b> bar under the columns holds listings you've parked without pushing them to the end of the pipeline.","body":["Drag a card onto the bar and pick <b>Park (On Hold)</b>, <b>Mark Lost</b> or <b>No Deal</b> (didn't qualify / no terms). The listing's status changes; its stage is untouched.","Parked listings drop out of the columns and the active totals. Click <b>Show</b> on the bar to see them, and <span class=\"kbd\">↩ Return</span> on any chip to put it back on the board as <b>Live</b>."],"tip":"Use No Deal for sellers who never got to terms, so your Lost count reflects real losses."},"filters":{"lead":"Search, <span class=\"kbd\">☰ Filters</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">★ Saved views</span> narrow the board and list to just the listings you want.","body":["The search box matches business, company, concept, contact, market, owner, status and stage. <b>Filters</b> opens <b>Rep / owner</b>, <b>Market</b> and <b>Status</b> (tick several at once), plus <b>Min $</b> and <b>Max $</b> on value. The red number on the button counts active filters; <b>Clear filters</b> resets them.","<b>Saved views</b> stores the current search and filters under a name. Click a name to apply it, × to delete it.","Filters are remembered per pipeline, and saved views live in this browser — they won't follow you to another computer."]},"flags":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">⚑ Card flags</span> puts small badges on cards that need a look.","body":["<b>High Value</b> shows a green <b>$</b> on any listing valued at or above the amount you set (default $200,000).","<b>Stalled in stage</b> shows a red <b>!</b> once a card has been in its stage for at least that stage's <b>Target days</b> from <span class=\"kbd\">Settings → Pipelines</span>. The number in the menu (default 30 days) is the fallback for stages with no target. Hover the badge to see how long it's been.","Turn either flag off with its checkbox. Until you change them, flags follow the firm's defaults; your own settings are saved in this browser."]},"cards":{"lead":"The toolbar controls on the board shape how much each card shows.","body":["<b>Sort</b> orders the cards in every column — newest or oldest activity, value high to low or low to high, name A → Z, or newest listing. <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Card fields</span>: turn Photo, Contact, Asking price, Value, Commission, Days in stage, Market, Status, BBS # and Last activity on or off. The contact name links to the contact record.","<b>Cards</b> <span class=\"kbd\">Full</span> / <span class=\"kbd\">Compact</span>: Compact shows just the name, flags and owner — click a card to expand it and use <b>Open deal →</b>. <b>Per column</b> caps how many cards show (10, 25, 50 or All) with a <b>+ more</b> button for the rest. The ▾ on a full card rolls it up to one line.","Badges on the name: a round <b>B</b>, <b>A</b> or <b>O</b> tells you going-concern business sale, asset sale (space, fixtures and equipment) or other; <b>Provisional</b> marks a listing still waiting on approval. Until you change them, card fields and flags follow the firm's defaults; once you pick your own, your choices are kept in this browser."]},"value":{"lead":"Totals update live as you filter and drag.","body":["Each column header shows the number of cards and the sum of their values in green. The line beside the Pipeline selector reads like <b>18 active · $6,450,000 · 3 off board</b>.","Totals follow your search and filters, so filtering to one rep or market gives you that rep's or market's pipeline value. Parked listings (On Hold, Lost, No Deal) are left out of the dollar figures."]},"open":{"lead":"A card's name opens the listing file — the home for pricing, buyers, NDAs, tours, offers, data room activity and the deal.","body":["On the listing file, <b>‹ Prev</b> and <b>Next ›</b> (with a count like <b>4 of 18</b>) step through the listings in the same order and filters you had on the board, so a pipeline review is just clicking Next.","If you open a listing directly instead of from the board, Prev / Next falls back to all the listings on its board, newest activity first."],"tip":"Filter the board to one rep before a pipeline meeting, open the first listing, and click Next through the rest."},"switch":{"lead":"One page runs every pipeline your firm has set up.","body":["The <b>Pipeline</b> selector lists every board — Business Sales, Space & Assets, Tenant Rep, Landlord Rep, Property Sales and any your admin added. Pick one and the columns change to that pipeline's stages. The last option, <b>Business Sales (Buyers) →</b>, opens the <a href=\"rrg_buyer_board.html\">Buyers</a> board.","<span class=\"kbd\">📣 Marketplace ↗</span> opens the public confidential marketplace in a new tab, so you can see what buyers see for the listings you've published."]},"stages":{"lead":"The columns on this board come from <span class=\"kbd\">Settings → Pipelines</span>.","body":["For each stage an admin sets the name, <b>Target days</b> (how long a listing should sit there before the red <b>!</b> stalled flag appears) and <b>Likelihood %</b>. Reorder, add or remove stages there and this board's columns follow.","A board with no stages shows a reminder to add them. If a stage is renamed or removed, listings that sat in it are re-placed by how far their work has progressed until someone drags them."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I see a teammate's listing?","a":"Reps see the listings they created or are the Owner on. Admins, and users given the see-all permission, see everyone's — see-all users can look at a teammate's listing but only its owner or an admin can change it."},{"q":"I dragged a card and it jumped back.","a":"The save was refused, so the board reloaded to show the real stage. Cards you can only view — a teammate's listing seen through see-all access — can't be dragged at all."},{"q":"Where did my filters and saved views go?","a":"They're stored in your browser. Another computer, a private window or cleared site data starts fresh."},{"q":"What's the difference between Lost and No Deal?","a":"Lost is a listing that went away after you were engaged. No Deal is a seller who never qualified or never reached terms. Both park the card off the board."}]},"buyer_board":{"intro":"Buyers pools every buyer lead from all your sell-side listings onto one board, grouped by your buyer pipeline's stages.","overview":["Buyers sits under Business Sales. Each card is one buyer lead on one listing — the same leads you see in <b>Buyer Leads</b> on the listing file, whether they came in from BizBuySell, the public marketplace or were entered by hand. Move a card and that buyer's stage changes on its listing.","Leave the <b>Listing</b> box on <b>All listings</b> to work your whole buyer book, or pick one listing to run its buyer funnel by itself. The board shows only listings that use the buyer pipeline on screen. Reps see buyers on the listings they created or are the Owner on; admins and anyone with the see-all permission see the whole team. Tenant-rep engagements don't appear here."],"feats":{"pool":{"lead":"One board for the whole buy side of your book.","body":["Columns are the stages of your buyer pipeline (for example Contacted → Qualified → NDA Sent → Toured → Offer). Each column shows how many buyers are in it, and the header reads like <b>42 buyers across 9 listings · 5 off board</b>.","A buyer whose saved stage isn't one of the columns — say, from an older pipeline — is shown in the first column so it never disappears; drag it to the right stage to fix it."]},"filter":{"lead":"The <b>Listing</b> box is a search-as-you-type picker.","body":["Click the box and start typing — it matches loosely, so a few letters of the name are enough. Each listing shows its buyer count. Use the arrow keys and Enter, or click.","Once a listing is picked the box turns blue with a folder icon and the board shows only that listing's buyers. Click <b>×</b> or pick <b>All listings</b> to go back. Only listings that have at least one buyer appear in the list."],"tip":"Esc closes the menu and keeps your current choice."},"drag":{"lead":"Advance a buyer by dragging the card.","body":["Drop the card on another column. A note at the bottom confirms <b>Moved to …</b>. If the move is refused, the card goes back and the reason is shown.","You can move buyers only on listings you own, unless you have the see-all permission. The stage must exist in the buyer pipeline the listing itself uses, or be one of the standard stages (Unqualified, Contacted, Qualified, NDA Sent, Toured, Offer, Passed, Dead).","If the buyer pipeline's stages have <b>On leave</b> or <b>On enter</b> automations (Settings → Pipelines), moving the card runs them on that buyer."]},"offboard":{"lead":"Clear dead or paused buyers out of the columns without losing where they were.","body":["Drag a card onto the <b>Off the board</b> bar, or click <b>···</b> on the card, then choose <b>Park (On Hold)</b> or <b>Mark Lost</b>.","The buyer keeps its stage. Click <b>Show</b> on the bar to see parked buyers and <span class=\"kbd\">↩ Return</span> to put one back exactly where it was."]},"listview":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">☰ List</span> shows every buyer for the current filter, including the ones parked off the board.","body":["Columns: Buyer, Company, Email, Listing, Stage, Status, Source, Added and Updated. Click a header to sort; click an email to start a message.","Click a <b>Stage</b> or <b>Status</b> cell to change it in place — Status takes the buyer to <b>Active</b>, <b>On Hold</b> or <b>Lost</b>. <b>Filter</b> narrows the rows, <b>Columns</b> hides or reorders them, and <b>Export</b> (CSV) and <b>Print</b> show for users with export permission."],"tip":"The page remembers whether you last used Board or List."},"bulk":{"lead":"In <span class=\"kbd\">☰ List</span>, tick the box on each buyer (or the header box for the whole page) and an <b>Actions</b> bar appears.","body":["<b>Mark Lost</b>, <b>Put On Hold</b> and <b>Return to board (Active)</b> change every selected buyer at once. Their stage is kept, so a returned buyer lands back where it was. These work on buyers on your own listings; anything else is skipped and the message says how many.","<b>Remove from listing…</b> shows only for users with the delete permission. It takes the buyer leads off their listings after a confirm. The contacts stay in your registry, but the leads drop out of the board and the buyer reports, and it cannot be undone."],"tip":"Use Mark Lost for anyone you actually talked to — it keeps the record of who looked at the deal. Save Remove for junk and duplicates."},"cards":{"lead":"Everything you need to call a buyer back is on the card.","body":["The buyer's name opens their contact record. Under it: company and email, a tag showing where the lead came from (BizBuySell, Marketplace …) when it wasn't entered by hand, and — when you're viewing All listings — a <b>📁</b> chip naming the listing, which opens it.","The <b>▾</b> on a card rolls it up to just the name; the board remembers which cards you rolled up."]},"pipelines":{"lead":"The <b>Pipeline</b> menu groups your boards in two sections.","body":["<b>Buyer</b> lists every buyer pipeline; pick one to show its stages and the listings that use it. Without a choice the board uses the firm's default buyer pipeline.","<b>Other boards</b> opens the matching board on the Listings page — Business Sales, Tenant Rep, Landlord Rep and the rest."]},"setup":{"lead":"This board needs at least one pipeline whose <b>Practice area</b> is <b>Buyer</b>.","body":["Until one exists the page shows <b>No buyer pipeline yet</b> with a link to <a href=\"rrg_admin_pipelines.html\">Settings → Pipelines</a>. Add a pipeline, set Practice area to Buyer, and add stages such as Contacted → Qualified → NDA Sent → Toured → Offer.","Each listing picks its buyer pipeline in the <b>Buyer pipeline</b> field of its Manage panel."]}},"faq":[{"q":"A buyer won't move — it says Unknown stage.","a":"That listing's buyers move through a different buyer pipeline than the one on screen — the board only shows listings on the pipeline you have up, so its <b>Buyer pipeline</b> was likely changed in the Manage panel. Reload the board, or switch the Pipeline menu to that pipeline."},{"q":"Where do these buyers come from?","a":"They're the buyer leads logged on each listing — imported BizBuySell inquiries, marketplace inquiries, and leads you add by hand on the listing file."},{"q":"A listing is missing from the Listing box.","a":"Only listings with at least one buyer lead that use the buyer pipeline on screen appear there. Switch the Pipeline menu to see listings on another buyer pipeline."}]},"rooms_queue":{"intro":"Data Rooms lists every confidential deal room you run, with its status, document count, downloads and the last buyer who got in — one click from editing, uploading or previewing the buyer side.","overview":["Data Rooms sits under Business Sales. Each row is one room: a private, per-deal document vault with its own buyer link. Rooms are built automatically when you create a sell-side listing, or you can start one here with <b>+ New data room</b>.","Admins see every room on the team; reps see the rooms they run. Open a room with <b>Edit data room</b> to load documents, authorize buyers, set permissions and read engagement."],"feats":{"status":{"lead":"Badges and Quick view chips tell you where each room stands.","body":["<b>● Live</b> means the room has documents. <b>🔗 Shared</b> means at least one buyer is authorized (hover for how many). <b>Setup</b> means nothing loaded and no buyers yet. <b>Archived</b> marks a room closed after a sale.","The <b>Quick view</b> chips — <b>All rooms</b>, <b>Live</b>, <b>Setup</b>, <b>Shared</b> — filter to that state and show a count. The columns show documents, created date, <b>Last access</b>, <b>Accessed by</b> and <b>Downloads</b>, leaving out buyers you've removed from the room."],"tip":"Check the Setup chip weekly — those are rooms you promised a seller and haven't loaded."},"create":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">+ New data room</span> builds a room with the standard folder structure already in place.","body":["Fill in the <b>Dataroom name</b> and the <b>Contact</b> (the seller or owner — required; a new name creates the contact). Optionally link a <b>Listing</b>, and set the <b>Rep</b> and <b>Date</b>.","The room starts with Financials and Tax Returns folders for the last five years, plus Lease, Equipment & FF&E, Staffing & Payroll, Licenses & Permits, Legal & Corporate, Menus & Marketing, Photos and Other. Every Financials year gets a T12 folder. <b>Per-year subfolders</b> adds <b>12 calendar months + Full Year</b> or <b>13 four-week periods + Full Year</b> under each Financials year. <b>Customize folders…</b> lets you tick exactly which sections, years and months to start with; <b>Reset</b> goes back to the standard set.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Create data room</span> and you land inside the new room."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New data room</span>.","Enter the name and the seller contact; link the listing if there is one.","Pick per-year subfolders or <b>Customize folders…</b>, then <span class=\"kbd\">Create data room</span>."]},"upload":{"lead":"<b>Upload files</b> on a row opens the room with the add-documents panel already open.","body":["Drag a batch (up to 40 files, 25 MB each) onto the room page. The assistant reads each file and puts it in the matching folder — P&Ls in Financials, the lease in Lease, and so on. Fix any misfile with the folder controls.","Every upload is checked for duplicates — the same file, or the same name already in the room. A single file asks before adding a second copy; a batch lists what it held back and asks once: <b>Upload anyway</b> or <b>Skip duplicates</b>."]},"buyerview":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">👁 Buyer view</span> opens the buyer-facing room in a new tab.","body":["Because you're signed in as the room's owner (or an admin), you skip the access-code screen and see the branded buyer-facing room. You can open and download any file from the preview without a code, and nothing counts as buyer activity — no views, alerts or watermark.","Buyer view isn't offered on archived rooms.","The preview shows <b>every folder</b>. Each buyer only sees the folders and files you've granted them, so check a buyer's own access in the permission grid inside the room."],"tip":"Run Buyer view after every upload batch to catch a file that landed in the wrong folder."},"link":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">Copy general access link</span> puts the room's tokenized link on your clipboard.","body":["The link alone doesn't open the documents. As soon as a room holds documents or has an authorized buyer, anyone using the link lands on a code screen and needs the personal access code you issue them inside the room.","If a link may have been forwarded, regenerate it inside the room — the old one stops working. An archived room's link shows a closed page."]},"access":{"lead":"Inside a room, every buyer is authorized one at a time.","body":["Add the buyer (linked to their contact), pick a starting level, and authorize — that issues their personal access code. <b>Email access</b> sends the link and code in one message.","Then click cells in the permission grid to set each folder to No access, View, Download or Edit (upload), and hide or show single files for a buyer. One toggle cuts off a buyer without deleting their history."]},"engagement":{"lead":"Every view, download and upload through the buyer link is logged and rolled up per buyer and per document.","body":["On this list, <b>Last access</b>, <b>Accessed by</b> and <b>Downloads</b> give you the headline. Inside the room you get each buyer's documents opened, views, downloads and read time, a day-by-day activity trail, and a red <b>Hot</b> badge on buyers who have opened key financials and been active in the last 7 days.","Read time pauses when a buyer switches tabs, so it reflects actual reading."],"tip":"Sort this list by Last access to see which deals have buyers in them this week."},"alerts":{"lead":"The room's rep gets an email when a buyer first opens a document that signals real interest.","body":["Key documents are matched by name: P&L, profit, financials, tax returns, lease, SDE, EBITDA, rent roll, balance sheet, revenue, CIM and offering memo.","Each buyer triggers one alert per document, so repeat visits don't flood your inbox. Alerts are on by default; an admin can turn them off firm-wide."]},"questions":{"lead":"Buyer questions stay with the deal instead of scattering across email.","body":["An authorized buyer can ask a question from inside the room. You get an email, and the question shows on the room page under <b>Buyer questions</b>, flagged <b>Awaiting reply</b> until you answer.","Each buyer has one private thread — buyers never see each other's questions. Type your reply under the thread; it's posted to that buyer's thread in the room and they're emailed that you answered (they read the answer behind their access code).","A closed room can't take replies until you reopen it, and a buyer whose access you revoked isn't emailed."],"tip":"Open rooms with <b>Awaiting reply</b> threads first — a buyer with a question is a buyer still working the deal."},"protect":{"lead":"Two ways to keep a seller's documents from walking.","body":["<b>Watermarking</b>: every PDF a buyer downloads is stamped with their name, email and the time, so a leaked copy traces back to them. On by default; admins can switch it off.","<b>Redact</b>: on any PDF in the room, drag boxes over sensitive text and click <span class=\"kbd\">Apply redactions</span>. Buyers get the redacted copy; your original stays in the room as a hidden, broker-only file."]},"verify":{"lead":"Two-step verification makes a buyer prove they own the email on file.","body":["After entering their access code, the buyer gets a one-time code by email. Once verified, that device isn't challenged again for 10 days.","Each room follows the firm default or overrides it. Every authorized buyer needs an email address while it's on."]},"archive":{"lead":"When a deal closes, archive its room from inside the room with <span class=\"kbd\">Close & archive room</span>.","body":["Archiving retires the buyer link and revokes every buyer's access at once, and the room turns read-only. Nothing is deleted, and you can reopen it if the deal comes back.","On this list, <b>Hide archived</b> (with a count of archived rooms) appears once you have any. Archived rows show the date and reason they were closed."]},"find":{"lead":"Narrow a long room list quickly.","body":["The search box matches the room name, the rep who created it, and the last person who accessed it. <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> adds ranges on <b>Documents</b>, <b>Created</b>, <b>Last access</b> and <b>Downloads</b>; the red number counts active filters.","<span class=\"kbd\">★ Saved</span> stores the current quick view, search and Hide archived setting under a name so you can call it back later.","Tick rows and use <b>Delete selected</b> to remove several rooms at once. Deleting removes the room and all its files, and the buyer link stops working. It needs the Delete permission, and rooms you don't own are skipped and counted."]}},"faq":[{"q":"I sent a buyer the link and they hit a code screen.","a":"That's expected once the room has documents. Authorize the buyer inside the room and use <b>Email access</b> to send them their personal code."},{"q":"Why don't I see a teammate's room?","a":"Reps see the rooms they run. Admins see every room."},{"q":"Does deleting a room just archive it?","a":"No. Delete removes the room and its files for good. To keep the history, use <b>Close & archive room</b> inside the room instead."}]},"deals":{"intro":"Deals is the roll-up of every buyer transaction across your listings: who's buying, at what price, where it stands, when it closes, and whether the commission has been billed and paid.","overview":["Find it under <b>Business Sales → Deals</b>. A deal lives on the listing that went under contract — one per listing — and this page lists them all in one place. Click the listing name to open it, or the buyer to open their contact.","Deals are opened and edited from the <b>Deal</b> card on the listing. <span class=\"kbd\">+ New deal</span> here just picks the listing and takes you straight to that card. Closing a deal doesn't create an invoice — invoices are made by hand from the Deal card."],"feats":{"tiles":{"lead":"The tiles answer \"where's my money\" before you read a row.","body":["The six tiles show <b>All deals</b>, <b>Under contract</b>, <b>Closing</b>, <b>Closed</b>, <b>Commission due</b> (unpaid, invoiced or partial) and <b>Paid</b>, each with a live count.","Click a tile to filter the list to it; click it again to clear. The tile filter stacks with <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span>."]},"open":{"lead":"The deal belongs to the listing, so that's where it's opened.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New deal</span>, pick the <b>Listing (business under contract)</b> — only listings without a deal are offered — and click <span class=\"kbd\">Open deal on listing →</span>.","The listing opens scrolled to its <b>Deal</b> card with the form ready. You can also open a deal from the listing itself with the <span class=\"kbd\">+</span> on the Deal card."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New deal</span>.","Pick the listing that went under contract.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Open deal on listing →</span>.","Fill in the deal form and click <span class=\"kbd\">Save deal</span>."]},"form":{"lead":"One record for the transaction from LOI to close.","body":["The Deal form on the listing holds <b>Buyer</b>, <b>Buyer email</b>, <b>Buyer company</b>, <b>Price</b>, <b>Status</b> (LOI, Under Contract, Due Diligence, Financing, Closing, Closed, Dead), <b>Opened</b>, <b>Expected close</b>, <b>Closed</b>, <b>Notes</b>, <b>Terms</b>, and <b>Commission rate</b>, <b>due</b>, <b>paid</b> and <b>status</b>.","Here on the Deals list, <b>Close</b> shows the actual close date, or the expected date with a ~ in front until it closes. <b>Commission paid</b> and <b>Rate</b> are hidden columns you can turn on from the list's column menu."]},"buyer":{"lead":"No retyping the buyer into the CRM.","body":["Type the buyer's name — the field suggests people already in your book — and add their email. On save the deal links to that contact, or creates one tagged as Buying.","On the Deals list the buyer's name links to their contact record. Once the deal is <b>Closed</b>, it counts toward that buyer's <b>Closed Deals</b> on their record."]},"split":{"lead":"See what the firm actually keeps on this deal.","body":["In the Deal form, fill <b>Co-broker (if any)</b> and <b>Our side share %</b> (100 means you're the sole broker), and <b>Referral to</b> with <b>Referral % off the top</b>. A referral already set on the listing carries in.","As you type, a line under the fields shows <b>Company dollar</b>: commission due, minus the referral, minus the co-broke share. Your own split is then applied on the Commissions side."]},"invoice":{"lead":"From closed deal to invoice without retyping the numbers.","body":["On the listing's Deal card, click <span class=\"kbd\">Create invoice</span>. A new invoice opens pre-filled with the listing, the client as bill-to and the commission due as the amount.","Once the invoice is sent (or a payment is recorded on it), the deal's commission status moves to <b>Invoiced</b> and it shows under the <b>Commission due</b> tile. Payments move it to <b>Partial</b> and then <b>Paid</b>, and <b>Commission paid</b> follows what's collected — voiding an invoice or removing a payment moves it back.","The listing's <b>Invoices</b> card lists what's been billed, collected and outstanding, and <b>Deal Expenses</b> beside it tracks costs on the deal."],"tip":"A commission status or paid figure typed on the Deal card by hand counts as an override — invoices can raise it but never lower it."},"filters":{"lead":"Pull the exact slice you need — this quarter's closings, everything still unpaid.","body":["<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> narrows by <b>Status</b>, <b>Commission</b> status and a <b>Close</b> date range (the actual close date, or expected close when it hasn't closed).","Filtered views can be saved and recalled, kept to yourself or shared with the team.","The list sorts on any column; by default it's ordered by commission due.","The search box matches the listing, buyer, buyer company, deal status, commission status and price — every word you type has to match."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why isn't my listing in the + New deal picker?","a":"It already has a deal — a listing carries one deal. Open the listing and edit the existing Deal card instead."},{"q":"Why can't I see a teammate's deal?","a":"Deals shows deals on listings you own unless you're an admin or have see-all access."},{"q":"How do I remove a deal that fell apart?","a":"Set its status to <b>Dead</b> to keep the history, or use <b>Remove</b> on the listing's Deal card. Removing the deal leaves the listing in place."}]},"board_tenant":{"intro":"Tenants is the tenant-rep pipeline board: every restaurant client you're finding space for, laid out by stage, with their site criteria right on the card.","overview":["Tenants sits under Tenant Rep in the sidebar. It's the same board as Listings, switched to the Tenant Rep pipeline — by default the stages run Needs Analysis → Site Search → Tours → LOI Out → Lease Negotiation → Build-out → Open. Each card is one engagement; drag it to change stage, or switch to <b>List</b> for a table with inline edits and bulk actions.","Click a card to open its tenant engagement file, where you keep the site selection criteria, space shortlist, tours, LOIs, the Market Attack Plan and the lease. Reps see the engagements they created or are the Owner on; admins and anyone with the see-all permission see the whole team, but see-all users can only change their own."],"feats":{"drag":{"lead":"Move an engagement through the tenant-rep pipeline by dragging its card.","body":["Grab a card and drop it on another column. The stage saves at once and <b>days in stage</b> restarts from today. An engagement you can see but don't own (see-all access) can't be dragged — hover it for the <b>View only</b> note. If a save fails, the board reloads and the card goes back where it was.","Column headers show how many tenants are in each stage. Stages are set by your admin in <span class=\"kbd\">Settings → Pipelines</span>."],"steps":["Find the tenant's card.","Drag it onto the new stage column and let go."],"tip":"Moving a batch at once? Use <a href=\"#bulk\">bulk edit</a> in List view."},"newtenant":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">+ New Tenant</span> starts a tenant-rep engagement from four fields.","body":["Enter the <b>Tenant / concept name</b>, then build <b>Target markets / trade areas</b> as chips — type or pick one, press Enter, and add the next. Backspace on an empty box removes the last chip. Type the <b>Client contact</b> (it searches your book as you type) and choose <b>Owner / assigned to</b>.","The client is linked to the existing contact if the name matches, or onboarded as a new contact. A company record is opened for the concept. Your target markets are copied into the engagement's site criteria, so the search starts with them filled in.","Unlike a sell-side listing, no data room is built — a tenant search has nothing to sell. The engagement starts as <b>Unqualified</b> in the first stage and opens as soon as it's created."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New Tenant</span>.","Fill in the concept, target markets, client contact and owner.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Create tenant</span>."]},"criteria":{"lead":"Tenant cards carry the brief the search runs against.","body":["Pulled from the engagement's <b>Site Selection Criteria</b>: preferred size (for example <b>2,000–3,500 SF</b>), use / concept, rent budget, and target markets.","Turn any of them on or off under <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Card fields</span> → <b>Tenant criteria</b>. Update the criteria on the engagement file and the card follows on the next load."]},"listview":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">☰ List</span> shows the same filtered tenants as a table.","body":["The first column reads <b>Engagement</b>, and BOV and Listing Price are left out — they don't apply to a tenant search. Click a column header to sort. Click a <b>Stage</b>, <b>Status</b>, <b>Owner</b> or <b>Market</b> cell to edit it in place; Enter saves, Esc cancels.","<b>Columns</b> lets you show, hide and reorder columns. <b>Export</b> (CSV) and <b>Print</b> show only for users with export permission and cover the ticked rows, or everything in the list if nothing is ticked."],"tip":"Cells on an engagement you can only view stay locked — only its owner or an admin can change it."},"bulk":{"lead":"In List view, tick the engagements to change and use the bulk actions.","body":["<b>Set status…</b>, <b>Set stage…</b>, <b>Set owner…</b> and <b>Set market…</b> apply one value to every ticked row. Owner and market include <b>— Clear —</b>.","<b>Delete selected</b> asks to confirm and permanently removes the engagements and their records. It needs the delete permission."]},"offboard":{"lead":"The <b>Off the board</b> bar holds searches that are paused or dead.","body":["Drop a card on the bar and choose <b>Park (On Hold)</b>, <b>Mark Lost</b> or <b>No Deal</b>. Only the status changes; the stage is kept.","Click <b>Show</b> on the bar to list parked tenants and <span class=\"kbd\">↩ Return</span> to bring one back as <b>Live</b>."]},"filters":{"lead":"Search, <span class=\"kbd\">☰ Filters</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">★ Saved views</span> cut the board down to the tenants you care about.","body":["Search matches concept, company, contact, market, owner, status and stage. <b>Filters</b> offers <b>Rep / owner</b>, <b>Market</b> and <b>Status</b> (pick several), plus <b>Min $</b> / <b>Max $</b>.","<b>Saved views</b> keeps the current search and filters under a name. The Tenants board remembers its own filters separately from Listings; saved views are kept in this browser."]},"flags":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">⚑ Card flags</span> marks cards that need attention.","body":["<b>Stalled in stage</b> puts a red <b>!</b> on a card once it's been in its stage for at least the stage's <b>Target days</b> (set in <span class=\"kbd\">Settings → Pipelines</span>); the days in the menu are the fallback for stages without a target. Hover the badge to see the count.","<b>High Value</b> adds a green <b>$</b> above a dollar amount you set. Both flags can be switched off; until you change them they follow the firm's defaults, and your own settings stay in this browser."]},"cards":{"lead":"Shape the board to fit how many searches you run.","body":["<b>Sort</b> orders the cards in every column — by activity, value, name or newest engagement. <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Card fields</span> toggles Photo, Contact, Days in stage, Status, Last activity and the <b>Tenant criteria</b> fields. <b>Compact</b> shows one line per card — click to expand, then <b>Open deal →</b>.","<b>Per column</b> limits each column to 10, 25 or 50 cards with a <b>+ more</b> button, and the ▾ on a card rolls it up. Until you change them, card fields and flags follow the firm's defaults; your own choices are saved in this browser."]},"open":{"lead":"Every card links to its tenant engagement file.","body":["The engagement file is where the search is actually run: <b>Site Selection Criteria</b>, the <b>Space shortlist</b> and <b>Space Pipeline</b>, <b>Location Tours</b>, <b>Site Fit</b> analyses, LOIs, the <b>Market Attack Plan</b>, broker blasts, the lease abstract and commission.","The contact name on a card opens the client's contact record instead."]},"tours":{"lead":"One list of every space your tenants have toured.","body":["Click <b>⊚ Tours</b> at the right of the Tenants board toolbar. Reps see the tours on their own searches; admins see the whole firm.","Filter by tenant or reaction, search the notes, and sort by date, gross $/SF or rating. Click a tenant name to open the engagement, where tours are logged on the <b>Location Tours</b> card and the client <b>Summary ↗</b> is printed."]},"stages":{"lead":"The columns come from the Tenant Rep pipeline in <span class=\"kbd\">Settings → Pipelines</span>.","body":["For each stage an admin sets the name, <b>Target days</b> (drives the stalled flag) and <b>Likelihood %</b>. Add, rename or reorder stages there and this board follows.","The <b>Pipeline</b> selector at the top of this page switches to any other board, including Listings and the Buyers board."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why didn't a data room get created for my new tenant?","a":"Tenant-rep searches don't get one — there's nothing to sell. Rooms are built for sell-side and landlord listings."},{"q":"How do I add a tenant from a contact instead?","a":"Open the contact and use <b>Tenant Rep Actions → Start Tenant Rep Engagement</b>. It lands on this board at the first stage."},{"q":"Why can't I see a teammate's tenant?","a":"Reps see the engagements they created or are the Owner on. Admins and users with the see-all permission see everyone's — see-all users can look, but only the owner or an admin can change one."}]},"space_tracker":{"intro":"Spaces is the firm's inventory of available restaurant and bar space — rents, size, the bones already built, and which of your tenants each one fits.","overview":["Spaces lives under Tenant Rep. Every space anyone logs — by hand, from a pasted listing, from a PDF flyer or straight out of a broker email — lands in one shared list with asking rent, NNN, size, position and status. Click a space name to open its own page: the restaurant-ready score, tenant fits, Texas alcohol filings at the address, nearby restaurants, listing-broker requests and rent history.","The status tiles across the top (Available, Toured, LOI Out, Leased, Passed, Off-market) filter the list. Flip to <b>Map</b> to see the same filtered set on a map, or tick rows and pull a market survey PDF for a client."],"feats":{"log":{"lead":"Every available space goes in one shared list with its economics and what's already built.","body":["Click <b>+ New space</b>. Enter the address (or a name), pick the <b>Shopping center</b> from your Centers list — or choose <b>＋ Add new center…</b> to create one on the spot — then <b>Size (SF)</b>, <b>Base rent ($/SF/yr)</b>, <b>NNN ($/SF/yr)</b>, <b>Position type</b>, <b>Status</b> and the landlord or listing broker.","Under <b>The bones</b>, tap everything already in place — drive-thru, hood, grease trap, gas, walk-in, bar, patio, fire suppression, 3-phase power, ADA restrooms, 2nd-gen restaurant. Those chips drive the restaurant-ready score and the tenant matching, so don't skip them.","Once a space is saved, editing it autosaves as you type. Use <b>+ Add brochure / photo</b> to attach the flyer and pictures; the first photo is the one that prints on the market survey."],"tip":"The <b>Total $/SF</b> column adds base rent and NNN for you — sort on it to compare true occupancy cost."},"aifill":{"lead":"Stop retyping flyers — AI reads the listing and fills the space for you.","body":["In the <b>New space</b> window, paste the listing text into <b>AI fill from a listing</b> and click <b>Fill the fields ↓</b>. AI pulls address, center, market, size, rent, NNN, position, bones and notes into the form for you to check and save.","<b>⬆ Upload PDF(s)</b> with one flyer fills the form the same way. Pick several flyers at once (up to 40, 25 MB each) and each one is created as its own space right away: a flyer whose address is already in the list is skipped, and each new space is linked to its shopping center (created if it's new). The message tells you how many were created, already in the system or skipped.","Have a Placer.ai report? Paste it into <b>Paste a Placer.ai report</b> and click <b>Summarize into Notes ↓</b> — visits, trend, trade area, dwell time, peak dayparts and top origins drop into the space's Notes."],"tip":"A multi-PDF batch has no review step — check the new spaces in the list afterward."},"autoimport":{"lead":"Broker marketing emails turn into spaces on their own, on a schedule you set.","body":["Click <b>📥 Auto-import</b>, tick <b>Auto-import on</b>, and choose <b>Check every</b> (15 minutes to 12 hours) and <b>Look back</b>. Each broker email and its PDF flyer is read, the space is created, the flyer is stored on it, and the shopping center is created if it's new. A space whose address is already in the list is skipped.","Put broker domains or addresses in <b>Sources to watch</b> (cbre.com, jll.com, broker@firm.com) and only those senders get scanned — far less junk. Under <b>Auto-enrich the shopping center from each brochure</b>, pick what AI fills on the center: store the brochure, restaurant fit, contacts and leasing economics, demographics and foot traffic. It only fills blanks.","When a flyer can't be read cleanly, it isn't dropped: a <b>⚑ Review</b> button appears on the toolbar with a count. Fix the address, rent or size there and click <b>Accept → add space</b>, or <b>Dismiss</b> it. Reps see their own review items; admins see everyone's.","<b>⟳ Scan now</b> runs it immediately with a live count. Inside <b>New space</b>, <b>✉ Scan email → auto-create</b> does a one-time sweep of up to two years."],"steps":["If the window says Gmail isn't connected, connect it on your Account page (your initials menu → Account → Connect Gmail).","Click <b>📥 Auto-import</b> and tick <b>Auto-import on</b>.","Add your regular listing brokers under <b>Sources to watch</b>.","Click <b>Save</b>, then <b>⟳ Scan now</b> for the first pass."]},"ready":{"lead":"A fast read on how close a space is to opening as a restaurant, and what that's worth in the TI talk.","body":["The <b>Ready</b> column scores each space 0–100 from the bones on file: hood 20 points, grease trap 15, gas 10, then 3-phase power, walk-in, former restaurant, ADA restrooms, bar, patio, drive-thru and fire suppression. 60 and up grades <b>Restaurant-ready</b>, 35 and up <b>Partial</b>, below that <b>Shell</b>. Hover the pill to see the build-out it saves.","On the space's own page, the <b>Restaurant-ready</b> card shows the gauge and a checklist you can tick, plus hood length, grease trap gallons, amps, parking, patio SF, seats, ceiling height and previous use. Each item carries a typical build-out cost, so the card tells you roughly how much a tenant avoids — <b>Build-out avoided (est.)</b> — for the rent and TI negotiation.","Entering a hood length, grease trap size, patio SF or 400+ amps counts the same as ticking that item. A space with nothing on file shows a dash, not a zero."]},"fits":{"lead":"The system checks every space against every active tenant search you run, so a good fit doesn't slip by.","body":["The <b>Tenant fits</b> column counts how many of your active tenant-rep engagements a space fits (55 or better out of 100). Hover to see who; click to open the space.","Scoring weighs size against the tenant's range, location against their markets and trade areas, base rent against their budget, and build needs (hood, grease, gas, patio, drive-thru, bar, end cap, pad…) against the bones on file. Leased and Passed spaces are left out, and a space well outside the size range or in a different metro doesn't count.","On the space page, <b>Tenant fits</b> lists each tenant with the score, what lines up and what's missing, plus an <b>LOI</b> button that opens the <a href=\"rrg_loi_builder.html\">LOI Builder</a> with the tenant and this space filled in. When a new space starts fitting a tenant, it's posted to the activity feed once, and the week's new fits are fed to the Daily Brief."],"tip":"No count? The engagement needs Site Selection Criteria with a size range, or a location plus a budget or build need."},"aimatch":{"lead":"A judgment read of your whole inventory against one client's brief.","body":["Click <b>✨ AI match</b>, choose the client from <b>Client — from Site Criteria on file</b> (the Site Criteria Screening forms on file), add anything extra under <b>Extra notes</b>, and click <b>Rank spaces</b>.","AI ranks your spaces 0–100 with a one-line reason for each. No screening on file? Type the criteria in the notes box instead.","This runs on demand and is separate from the automatic <a href=\"#fits\">Tenant fits</a> scoring, which works off each engagement's criteria."]},"sitefit":{"lead":"Turn a match into a full fit analysis without retyping the space.","body":["On a space page, <b>✚ Run Fit</b> opens Site Fit with the address, suite, size, base rent, NNN, parking and the center’s traffic count and co-tenants filled in. Each tenant under <b>Tenant fits</b> has a <b>Fit</b> button that links the tenant too.","Every analysis run on the space is listed under <b>Site Fit analyses</b> on the space page with its score and call."]},"map":{"lead":"See where the inventory sits before you plan a tour day.","body":["Click <b>🗺 Map</b> next to the count. The map shows whatever the tiles, search and column filters leave in the list, each pin colored by status. Click a pin for size, type and gross rent, then <b>Edit space →</b>.","Spaces without saved coordinates show at their city's center first (a faded pin), then move to the exact address as it's looked up. Once placed, the location is saved on the space — which is also what the space page needs to pull <a href=\"#siteintel\">nearby restaurants</a> and what puts the space on the survey map."]},"survey":{"lead":"A client-ready tour book in one click.","body":["Tick the spaces you want in the list, then click <b>⬇ Market survey PDF</b> in the bar that appears. From a space page, <b>⬇ Survey PDF</b> builds a one-space survey.","The PDF carries your logo and firm address, a map of the spaces (when your admin has set a Google Maps key and the spaces have been mapped), a summary table of size, base, NNN, gross, position and ready score, then one page per space: photo (the space's own, or its center's), the facts, the restaurant-ready checklist with build-out avoided, nearby restaurants, co-tenants and your notes.","Up to 40 spaces per survey."]},"comps":{"lead":"Your own deals become the rent comp set.","body":["Click <b>▤ Rent comps</b>. Filter by market and position to see counts and medians for asking rent, leased rent, NNN and days on market, broken out by market and position.","Leased comps come from your own deals: when a space goes to <b>Leased</b>, open its page and record <b>Leased at ($/SF)</b>, <b>Leased to</b> and the date under <b>History & comps</b>. Every one you add builds the comp set, and each space page lists leased comps in its market."]},"history":{"lead":"Know when a landlord blinks.","body":["Every change to base rent, NNN, size, status, position, condition or leased rent is logged with who and when. The space page shows the timeline under <b>History & comps</b> — rent cuts marked green, increases red.","In the list, the Status column flags a rent cut in the last 45 days (<b>↓$</b> and the amount), <b>stale</b> when an available space hasn't been updated in 60+ days, and days on market once it passes 120. Days on market counts from when the space was last set to Available."]},"broker":{"lead":"Chase listing brokers without leaving the space.","body":["On a space page, fill in the <b>Listing broker</b> name, company, email and phone, then click <b>✉ Request info</b> or <b>Request a tour</b>. The message is pre-written for a restaurant tenant — flyer, asking rent and NNN, delivery condition, hood, grease, gas and power — and you can edit it before <b>Send request</b>.","It goes out from the firm's address with your signature, and the broker's reply comes straight to you. Opens are tracked, and it's logged on the space and on the broker's contact record if they're in your contacts. When the broker answers, click <b>Got a reply</b>.","The card header keeps score for that broker across every space they list: requests answered and average response time."],"tip":"The buttons are greyed out until an admin sets up outgoing email."},"siteintel":{"lead":"Who was in this space, how they did, and who's around it.","body":["<b>Texas alcohol records</b> on a space page looks up the Comptroller's mixed-beverage filings at that street address: each operator's last 24 months, trailing-12 sales, change versus last year and liquor/wine/beer mix. An operator that stopped filing is flagged, TABC permits are listed with status and expiry, and a credit-law delinquency at the address gets a warning. Click an operator for its full page in <a href=\"rrg_market_radar.html\">Alcohol Sales</a>.","<b>Nearby restaurants</b> lists restaurants within about a mile from Google, with a cuisine mix and how many nearby spots are permanently closed — co-tenancy and competition at a glance. The center's anchor, co-tenants and traffic count show on top.","State records refresh weekly and nearby restaurants every 30 days; <b>Refresh</b> on either card pulls fresh now. Nearby needs a Google Maps key from your admin and a space that's been placed on the <a href=\"#map\">map</a>."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why doesn't a space show any Tenant fits?","a":"Fits only count active tenant-rep engagements you can see whose Site Selection Criteria has a size range, or a location plus a budget or build need. Leased and Passed spaces are never matched."},{"q":"Why can't I delete a space?","a":"Only admins can delete. Set the status to <b>Passed</b> instead — it drops out of matching and keeps the history."},{"q":"Nearby restaurants says the space has no map location.","a":"Open the <b>🗺 Map</b> view once so the address gets pinpointed, then click <b>Refresh</b> on the space page."},{"q":"Will auto-import create duplicates?","a":"No — both the Gmail import and a multi-PDF upload from the New space window skip any address already in the list."}]},"tours":{"intro":"Tours collects every space you've shown a tenant, across your tenant-rep engagements, with the rents, the tenant's reaction and their rating.","overview":["Tours lives under Tenant Rep. Tours are logged on each tenant engagement under <b>Location Tours</b>; this page pulls them all into one list you can filter by tenant, reaction or keyword. You see the engagements you own; admins see all of them.","Pick a tenant and click <b>Client summary ↗</b> for a one-page tour summary — ranked, with a top pick and your notes — ready to print or save as a PDF for the client."],"feats":{"log":{"lead":"Tours are recorded where the deal lives — on the tenant engagement.","body":["Open the tenant engagement and click <b>+</b> on <b>Location Tours</b>. Start typing in <b>From Space Tracker</b> to pull in a space you already track — property, suite, SF, rent and NNN fill in — or type the details yourself. Add the date, who showed it, the tenant's <b>Reaction</b> (Loved, Liked, Maybe, Passed), a 1–5 <b>Rating</b> and <b>Tenant's thoughts</b>, then <b>Log tour</b>.","Everything you log shows up on this page automatically. This page is read-only — edit or delete a tour back on the engagement."],"tip":"Picking the space from Space Tracker links the tour to it, so the LOI Builder later lists that space first, marked ★ Toured."},"list":{"lead":"One place to see what every tenant has seen.","body":["The top row counts <b>Spaces shown</b>, <b>Liked or better</b>, tours in the <b>Last 30 days</b> and <b>Tenants touring</b> — all for whatever the filters leave in view.","Narrow it with <b>All tenants</b> (each with its tour count), <b>Any reaction</b>, and the search box, which looks through tenant, property, suite, type, who showed it and the tenant's thoughts. Click a column header — Date, Tenant, Space, SF, Gross $/SF, Reaction, Rating — to sort.","Gross $/SF is base rent plus NNN. Passed spaces stay in the list, greyed out. Click a tenant name to open the engagement."]},"toppick":{"lead":"The front-runner rises to the top on its own.","body":["Each tour carries a reaction pill and a 1–5 star rating. Sorting by <b>Rating</b> ranks on stars first, then reaction (Loved over Liked over Maybe).","On a client summary the highest-ranked space that wasn't passed is tagged <b>Top pick</b>, and its gross $/SF shows up top. A space with no rating and no reaction can't be the top pick."]},"summary":{"lead":"A clean recap for the client after a tour day.","body":["Choose a tenant in the <b>All tenants</b> filter and click <b>Client summary ↗</b>. The summary lists every space shown, numbered and ranked by rating, with toured date, type, SF, base, NNN, gross, reaction, stars and the tenant's thoughts, plus <b>Spaces shown</b>, <b>Liked or better</b>, <b>Top pick</b> and the top pick's gross $/SF.","Click <b>Print / PDF</b>. The printout drops the buttons and filters and adds a header and confidentiality footer with your firm's name and the date.","<b>← Engagement</b> goes back to the tenant's file; <b>All tours</b> returns to the full list."]},"notes":{"lead":"Your read on the search, kept with the tours.","body":["At the bottom of a client summary, type in <b>Overall notes & next steps</b> — front-runner, backups, what you're countering, next tours. It saves a moment after you stop typing (a green <b>Saved</b> flashes) and again before printing.","The note is stored on the engagement, so everyone on the deal sees the same one, and it prints as plain text on the summary."]},"import":{"lead":"Move tours that only lived on one computer onto the server, where the team can see them.","body":["If this browser still holds tours from the retired Tour Tracker, a yellow box appears at the top: <b>Tour logs saved in this browser only.</b> Each old client is listed with its space count and a guess at the matching engagement.","Pick the engagement for each one (or leave <b>— don't import —</b>) and click <b>Import to server</b>. Imported tours, ratings, reactions and overall notes join the engagement and are cleared from this browser; anything that fails or you skipped stays put for later."],"tip":"Create the tenant engagement first if it doesn't exist yet — the import needs somewhere to put the tours."}},"faq":[{"q":"I logged a tour but it isn't here.","a":"Tours shows space tours on tenant-rep engagements you own (admins see all). Buyer tours of a business on a sale listing don't appear here."},{"q":"Can I edit a tour from this page?","a":"No — open the tenant engagement and edit it under <b>Location Tours</b>. This page reads from there."},{"q":"Does the client see this page?","a":"No. It's internal. Send the client the printed <b>Client summary</b> instead."}]},"loi_builder":{"intro":"The LOI Builder drafts lease and business-purchase letters of intent from your firm's clause library, keeps every version, sends them tracked, and follows the negotiation term by term.","overview":["LOI Builder lives under Tenant Rep but handles both tenant-rep (lease) and business-sale LOIs. It opens on <b>All LOIs</b>: every letter you can see, with type, property, the parties, status, version, rounds and the response-due date — overdue ones in red. <b>Edit</b> opens a letter to revise; <b>Manage →</b> opens its negotiation window.","Start from a tenant engagement, a space page or a business-sale listing and the builder comes pre-filled — the tenant, the space's rent and NNN, or the seller's preferred terms. You see your own LOIs and those on engagements you own; admins and see-all reps see everything."],"feats":{"build":{"lead":"From blank to a letterhead draft without opening Word.","body":["Click <b>+ New LOI</b>, pick <b>Tenant Rep (Lease)</b> or <b>Business Sale</b>, then fill <b>Key info</b> and <b>Key terms</b>. Escalations, renewal options and the personal guaranty (with fixed or rolling burn-off) have their own inputs. Blank terms print as fill-in lines. Tenant, landlord, buyer and seller fields search your contacts and companies as you type — picking one links the LOI to that record.","Under <b>Optional sections</b>, search, filter by venue (Restaurant, Bar, Dance Hall) and tick the clauses you want; click <b>preview</b> to read one first. Then click <b>Build LOI →</b>.","The letter opens on screen with your logo. Click anywhere in it to edit the wording — edits are saved with the version. Switch <b>Format</b> between Classic and Modern. <b>← Back to edit</b> returns to the fields."]},"prefill":{"lead":"Don't retype what the system already knows.","body":["On a tenant-rep LOI, the <b>Space</b> box beside <b>Key info</b> searches <a href=\"rrg_space_tracker.html\">Spaces</a>. Pick one and property, premises, rentable SF, base rent, NNN and the landlord fill in. Spaces this tenant has toured are listed first, marked <b>★ Toured</b> with their reaction.","Start the LOI from a tenant engagement and the tenant is filled and linked, and the LOI is saved to that engagement — it shows on the engagement's LOIs card and in the side-by-side <a href=\"#compare\">comparison</a>. The <b>LOI</b> button on a space page's Tenant fits does both at once."]},"versions":{"lead":"Every draft is kept, and you can see what moved between them.","body":["<b>💾 Save draft</b> creates the LOI. Open it again with <b>Edit</b> and the banner reads <b>Revising vN</b>; saving makes the next version of the same letter, and the earlier one is kept with its terms, sections and edited wording. Want a different letter instead? Click <b>Start a separate LOI instead</b>.","In <b>Manage →</b>, <b>Versions</b> lists each one with date and author. <b>What changed vs vN</b> shows every term that moved, old value struck through and new value in green — or says only the wording or sections changed.","Saving a new LOI with a linked contact logs <b>LOI Drafted</b> on that contact's timeline."]},"files":{"lead":"The letter you edited, in the format the other side wants.","body":["From the built letter, click <b>⬇ PDF</b> for a letterhead PDF or <b>⬇ Word</b> for a .docx the landlord's attorney can redline. Unsaved changes are saved first, so the file always matches a numbered version.","The same <b>⬇ PDF</b> and <b>⬇ Word</b> buttons sit in <b>Manage →</b> for any LOI in the list. <b>Print</b> prints just the letter."]},"send":{"lead":"Sending, logging and the follow-up reminder in one step.","body":["From the built letter, click <b>✉ Send…</b>. The recipient fills in from the linked landlord or seller when an email is on file; the subject and a short cover note are written for you, and <b>Response due</b> defaults to the LOI's expiration (or five days).","<b>Send LOI</b> attaches the letterhead PDF, adds your email signature and tracks opens. If the recipient is a contact, it's logged on their record as <b>LOI Sent</b> (and shows in Tracked Emails); a draft LOI is marked <b>Sent</b>, a sent round is recorded, and a high-priority task <b>LOI response due — …</b> lands on your list for that date. The task closes itself when the LOI is marked Accepted or Dead.","Outgoing email has to be set up by an admin first."]},"nego":{"lead":"See where every term stands without rereading the redlines.","body":["Click <b>Manage →</b> on any LOI. Set the <b>Status</b> (Draft, Sent, Countered, Negotiating, Accepted, Dead) or use <b>Mark accepted</b> / <b>Dead</b>. Accepted and Countered are noted on the linked contacts' timelines.","Under <b>Terms — click a chip to cycle</b>, each key term shows its value and a chip that steps Open → Proposed → Accepted → Countered → Rejected.","Under <b>Rounds</b>, log each exchange as <b>We sent</b> or <b>We received</b> with a note. <b>Response due</b> at the top can be changed any time — it updates the task — and shows <b>Overdue</b> once it passes."]},"counter":{"lead":"Turn a marked-up counter into updated term chips in seconds.","body":["In <b>Manage →</b>, paste their counter or reply under <b>✨ Log a counter</b> and click <b>Analyze counter</b>. AI compares it to your current terms and lists each change with its status and the old and new values, plus a one-line summary.","Click <b>Apply to terms + log round</b> to flip those term chips, set the LOI to <b>Countered</b>, record a received round with the changes, and note it on the linked contacts."]},"parse":{"lead":"Start your response from their letter, not a blank form.","body":["In the builder, click <b>✨ Fill from a received LOI</b>, paste the letter and click <b>Pull the terms</b>. AI fills the matching fields — parties, property and the key terms your library tracks — and tells you how many it filled, with a summary of the letter.","Review the fields, close the window, then build your own version."]},"aicheck":{"lead":"A second set of eyes before it goes out.","body":["<b>✨ Suggest</b> on <b>Optional sections</b> reads the deal info and ticks the sections it recommends, each with a reason, and drafts any extra clauses worth adding.","After you build the letter, <b>✨ Review draft</b> reads it and returns a verdict, flags ranked HIGH, MED or LOW with a suggested fix, and a list of anything possibly missing.","Both are metered AI calls, so you'll be asked to confirm first. <b>Cancel</b> stops a run."]},"compare":{"lead":"The number that decides the deal: net effective rent over the term.","body":["For an LOI tied to a tenant engagement, click <b>Compare ↗</b> in <b>Manage →</b> (or open it from the engagement's LOIs card). Every lease LOI on that engagement sits in its own column: SF, term, delivery condition, base rent, escalations, NNN, year-1 gross and occupancy cost, final-year base, free rent, TI and total cost over the term.","The bottom row is <b>Net effective gross rent</b> — base rent over the term, less free rent and TI, spread over SF and years, plus NNN — with <b>Best effective rent</b> marked. Green marks the best value in every row.","If an LOI doesn't state NNN, type an estimate in its cell; it's used for the math without creating a new version. Tick <b>Show dead</b> to include dead letters. <b>Print / PDF</b> prints landscape with your logo."]},"listing":{"lead":"Hand buyers an offer letter already shaped the way your seller wants it.","body":["On a business-sale listing, <b>✍ Draft buyer LOI →</b> on the <b>Offers — IOIs & LOIs</b> card opens a Business Sale LOI with a <b>Buyer LOI</b> banner. The business name, seller and asking price are always pulled live from the listing, layered over the listing's saved offer template (or your firm's standard terms).","Set the terms and sections the seller prefers, then click <b>💾 Save as this listing's offer template</b> — the next buyer LOI on that listing starts from them. The listing's owner, LOI managers and admins can save the template.","Fill in the buyer and build as usual."]},"library":{"lead":"One clause library keeps every LOI on the firm's language.","body":["Admins and anyone given the LOI-manage permission see <b>Manage clause library →</b> in the builder header. It opens <b>LOI Settings</b>, where each LOI type's <b>Boilerplate</b> (opening and closing language), <b>Key terms</b>, <b>Optional sections</b> (with venue tags and order) and <b>Deal defaults</b> are maintained.","Changes apply to every new LOI the next time a rep opens the builder. Letters saved from the built on-screen letter keep their own wording."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I see a colleague's LOI?","a":"You see LOIs you wrote and those on engagements you own. Admins and reps set to see all deals see everything."},{"q":"Does saving overwrite my last draft?","a":"No. Saving an existing LOI makes the next version and keeps the earlier one; <b>What changed vs vN</b> in Manage shows the difference."},{"q":"The Compare button is missing.","a":"Comparison works per tenant engagement. Start the LOI from the engagement (or the space page's LOI button) so it's tied to one."},{"q":"Where does the response-due reminder show up?","a":"As a high-priority task on your task list, titled <b>LOI response due</b>. It closes when the LOI is marked Accepted or Dead."}]},"landlord_rep":{"intro":"Space Listings is where you represent a property owner leasing restaurant or bar space — one file per space, from listing to executed lease.","overview":["Space Listings lives under Landlord Rep. It lists every landlord engagement you're running with the space, landlord, market, size, asking rent, status and last activity. Click a space name to open its listing file, where the space details, the leasing pipeline, tenant prospects, tours and LOIs live.","A landlord listing runs on the same engine as your business-sale listings — pipeline, tours, LOIs, documents and data room — pointed at tenants instead of buyers."],"feats":{"list":{"lead":"Every landlord space you're working, in one sortable list.","body":["Each row shows the space, the landlord contact, market, size, asking rent, status and when it was last updated; a 🔒 code name shows next to listings that have one. Click a column header to sort.","The search box matches every word you type against the space name, landlord, market, status, size, asking rent, use and code name — \"austin bar\" finds Austin spaces with bar in the use.","Click the space name to open its listing file."]},"new":{"lead":"Three fields open a new landlord engagement.","body":["Click <b>+ New space listing</b>. Enter the <b>Space / listing name</b> (e.g. \"Former Cantina — 1200 S Congress\"), the <b>Market</b> from your market list, and the <b>Landlord contact</b>.","As you type the landlord, the note below tells you whether it's linking to an existing contact or will onboard a new one. A landlord contact is required — every listing links to a contact.","<b>Create listing</b> puts it on the Landlord Rep board, links the landlord, builds the listing's data room and opens the file so you can add the space details."]},"space":{"lead":"The facts a prospective tenant asks for first.","body":["On the listing file's <b>Manage</b> panel, the <b>Space for lease</b> box holds size, asking rent, lease term, available date, condition, suitable use, key features and space notes. It autosaves as you type.","Size, asking rent and use also show in this list and feed its search."]},"pipeline":{"lead":"The stage is the deal's status at a glance.","body":["Every space sits on the Landlord Rep pipeline: <b>Listing Setup</b>, <b>Marketing</b>, <b>Tours</b>, <b>LOI Received</b>, <b>Lease Negotiation</b>, <b>Executed</b>. Change the stage from the listing file's Manage panel or work the board the same way you work sale listings."]},"tenants":{"lead":"The same deal machinery as a sale, aimed at tenants.","body":["On the listing file, record each showing under <b>Location Tours</b> and capture each letter of intent under <b>Offers — IOIs & LOIs</b> as it comes in.","Use the <a href=\"rrg_loi_builder.html\">LOI Builder</a> to draft a lease LOI.","The <b>Prospect tenants</b> card links to the <b>Tenants</b> board so you can see which tenant searches in your own book might fit the space."]},"docs":{"lead":"Every space's paper in one place.","body":["On the listing file, the <b>Documents</b> card (<span class=\"kbd\">＋ Add document</span>) keeps the space's paperwork — site plan, floor plan, current lease or LOI and any exhibits. Each listing also gets its own confidential data room, built when the listing is created.","The <b>Data Room Activity</b> card shows who has been in the room and what they opened, with <b>Open room →</b> to add files and share it with a prospective tenant. Hover a photo and hit <b>⤴</b> to send it to the listing's data room."]},"report":{"lead":"Keep the owner posted without writing the update yourself.","body":["The <b>Landlord activity report</b> card on the listing file previews the numbers for the period — <b>Tenant inquiries</b>, <b>Showings</b>, <b>LOIs</b> and <b>Room views</b> — and shows who it goes to and when it was last sent.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Send now</span> and confirm, and the landlord contact gets an email summary of leasing activity on the space. It only goes out when you send it — there's no automatic schedule for landlord listings — and it needs an email on the landlord contact."],"tip":"Check the numbers on the card before you send — it goes straight to the client."}},"faq":[{"q":"How is this different from Tenant Rep?","a":"Tenant Rep represents the tenant hunting for space. Landlord Rep represents the owner marketing a space for lease. Same platform, opposite sides."},{"q":"Where do the markets come from?","a":"Your admin's market list (Settings → Markets) — the same list every other section uses."},{"q":"Is this the same as Spaces under Tenant Rep?","a":"No. <a href=\"rrg_space_tracker.html\">Spaces</a> is the inventory of available spaces you show tenants. A Space Listing is an engagement where you represent the landlord."}]},"property_listings":{"intro":"Property Listings holds the real estate you're marketing — investment and owner-user buildings and land, for sale or lease — with price, cap rate, NOI, land and improvements.","overview":["Property Listings lives under Landlord Rep. Each listing is a property, not a business: asking price, cap rate and NOI for a sale, asking rent and NNN for a lease, plus the building, the land, restaurant infrastructure, seller and co-broker, marketing copy and documents. The list shows what it's for, type, price or rent, cap rate, building SF and status.","A green ◍ means the property is live on the public marketplace; 📋 means it's being worked on the Property Sales pipeline board."],"feats":{"add":{"lead":"One record for the real estate, organized the way CoStar asks for it.","body":["Click <b>+ New property</b>. Give it a name (required), <b>Listing for</b> (sale, lease or both), <b>Status</b>, <b>Property type</b> and secondary type, tenancy, address, county, market, APN and interest conveyed. <b>Upload photo</b> adds the thumbnail.","Open the sections you need: <b>Sale economics</b> (sale type, price, price/SF, cap rate, NOI, occupancy, taxes, owner-occupiable SF, sale conditions), <b>Lease economics</b> (asking rent, NNN/CAM, available SF, term, TI, lease type), <b>Building & land</b> (SF, lot, year built and renovated, construction, sprinklers, ceiling, zoning, parking, frontage, traffic) and <b>Amenities & features</b>.","<b>Internal notes</b> at the bottom are for you — seller motivation, who's circling."]},"fit":{"lead":"Answer \"can a restaurant run here?\" on the record.","body":["The <b>Restaurant fit & infrastructure</b> section captures drive-thru, end-cap, pad / out-parcel, grease / hood / vent, patio potential and whether it's a 2nd-gen restaurant, plus <b>Existing / former use</b> (what's left — hood, walk-ins, bar, FF&E) and <b>TABC / liquor license notes</b> (wet/dry, distance rules, permits in place).","These carry onto the CoStar / LoopNet entry sheet as <b>Restaurant Infrastructure</b>, <b>Existing / Former Use</b> and <b>Liquor / TABC</b>."]},"enrich":{"lead":"Let the OM fill in the building and land facts.","body":["Attach the OM, brochure or flyer under <b>Documents & map</b> first. Then, in <b>CoStar / LoopNet entry sheet</b>, click <b>✨ Enrich from documents</b>.","AI reads up to three attached PDFs or Word files and fills the physical fields; when your admin has set a Google Maps key it verifies the address and fills city, state and ZIP; and when county parcel records are connected it adds APN, lot size, year built, zoning, county and owner of record. The message tells you what came from where.","It only fills blanks — it never overwrites what you typed. AI must be enabled for your account."]},"costar":{"lead":"No more hunting through the OM while you fill in CoStar.","body":["Click <b>📋 Build entry sheet</b>. You get the property's details grouped like CoStar/LoopNet's tabs — Listing, Sale, Lease (when offered for lease), Property and Highlights — each line labeled with the CoStar field name. Only the physical property goes on it; nothing about a business.","<b>Copy sheet</b> puts it on your clipboard. Or open <b>Or email it to a co-broker / rep</b>, enter their name and email, and <b>Open email draft →</b> starts an email in your mail app with the sheet in the body. The name and email are remembered for next time.","Put your CoStar bullet points in <b>Highlights</b> (one per line) under Marketing so they come through."]},"marketplace":{"lead":"Put the property in front of buyers and tenants on your public site.","body":["Under <b>Marketing & marketplace</b>, write the <b>Public description</b> and <b>Highlights</b>, set <b>Listing start</b> and <b>Listing expires</b>, then tick <b>Show on the public marketplace</b>. It's an open listing — address, price and photo are visible. Tick <b>Feature it near the top of the marketplace</b> to pin it up front.","Marked <b>Sold</b> or <b>Off-market</b>, it drops off the marketplace automatically. Visitors can send an inquiry from the marketplace page."]},"board":{"lead":"When a property turns into an active assignment, run it like a listing.","body":["Open a saved property and click <b>Work on the board</b> in the bar at the top. It creates a linked listing on the <b>Property Sales</b> pipeline — with the seller as its contact — and opens it so you can track stages, tasks and offers.","After that the bar reads <b>On the Property Sales board</b> with <b>Open on board →</b>, and the list shows 📋 beside the status.","Add a seller name or email first — every pipeline listing links to a contact."]},"docs":{"lead":"Keep the property's paper with the property.","body":["Save the property first, then open it again and use <b>＋ Add document</b> under <b>Documents & map</b> — PDFs, Word, Excel, CSV or images up to 25 MB each.","Opening the section also drops a pin on the map from the address, city, state and market you entered.","Record the <b>Seller & co-broker</b> contacts in their own section; the seller is who the board listing links to."]}},"faq":[{"q":"What's the difference between this and Space Listings?","a":"Property Listings is the real estate itself — a building or land for sale or lease, priced on cap rate and NOI. <a href=\"rrg_landlord_rep.html\">Space Listings</a> is a landlord engagement for one space for lease."},{"q":"Why can't I delete a property?","a":"Only admins can delete. Set the status to Off-market or Sold instead — it comes off the marketplace."},{"q":"Enrich didn't fill anything.","a":"Attach the OM or flyer under Documents first. Enrich also skips any field you've already filled."}]},"centers":{"intro":"Centers is your book of shopping centers. One center holds many spaces; spaces link here from Spaces, and each center carries its anchors, traffic, demographics, contacts and leasing economics.","overview":["Centers sits under Landlord Rep. The list shows each center's photo, type, anchor and co-tenants, GLA, traffic count and the spaces linked to it with their status. Click <b>Edit</b> to open a center's full record.","Most centers build themselves: when a space is imported from a broker email, its center is created and filled from the flyer. Use <b>✨ Enrich centers</b> to fill the gaps across the whole book at once."],"feats":{"record":{"lead":"Co-tenancy and draw, written down once and reused everywhere.","body":["Click <b>+ New center</b>, or <b>Edit</b> on a row. Fill the name (required), address, city, market, <b>Center type</b>, <b>Total GLA (SF)</b>, <b>Anchor(s)</b>, <b>Notable co-tenants</b>, landlord / owner, <b>Traffic (VPD)</b> and notes. <b>Upload photo</b> sets the picture.","The anchor, co-tenants and traffic show on each linked space's page and print on the market survey; the center photo is used when a space has none of its own.","Search finds centers by name, market, city, anchor, co-tenants, address or landlord. Show the hidden <b>Landlord</b> column from <b>Columns</b>."]},"spaces":{"lead":"See the open suites in a center without leaving the list.","body":["Spaces link to a center from <a href=\"rrg_space_tracker.html\">Spaces</a> — pick it in the <b>Shopping center</b> field, or create the center right there. Auto-imported flyers link themselves.","Here, the <b>Spaces</b> column lists up to four linked spaces with their status and a count of the rest; click a space's name to open it. Filter or sort on it to find centers with the most linked spaces.","Deleting a center (admins only) unlinks its spaces; the spaces themselves stay."]},"enrichall":{"lead":"Clean up the whole book in one pass.","body":["Click <b>✨ Enrich centers</b> and confirm. Every center gets its address, city and metro filled from the spaces and flyers linked to it, plus a photo when one can be found. AI then fills a missing type, anchor or co-tenants, working only from the center's name and its spaces' notes.","When your admin has added a Google Maps key, addresses are verified against Google and a Street View or Places photo is pulled; without one it uses your own listings only. With Google on it can take a couple of minutes.","It only fills blanks — nothing you've typed is overwritten (a type it guessed on an earlier run can be revised). The count line reports how many centers were filled, Google-verified and given photos."]},"brochures":{"lead":"Turn the flyers you already have into center data.","body":["Open a saved center and click <b>✨ Auto-fill from brochures</b>. It reads up to four flyers attached to the center's linked spaces and fills blank fields, and can copy the brochures into the center's documents.","What it fills follows your choices under <b>Auto-enrich the shopping center</b> in the <b>📥 Auto-import</b> window on Spaces — by default restaurant fit, contacts and leasing economics, and filing the brochure; demographics are off unless you turn them on.","It only fills blanks. If no linked space has a flyer, it tells you to attach or import one first."],"tip":"Turned every Auto-enrich box off on Spaces? Then this button has nothing to run — turn the parts you want back on."},"fit":{"lead":"Can a restaurant open here — and who's already feeding this center?","body":["The <b>Restaurant fit & infrastructure</b> section records drive-thru, end-cap availability, pad / out-parcel, grease / hood / vent, patio potential and 2nd-gen availability, plus <b>Existing restaurants / bars in center</b> and <b>TABC / liquor license notes</b> (wet/dry status, distance rules to schools and churches)."]},"demo":{"lead":"Is there enough demand around the center?","body":["Under <b>Demographics & foot traffic</b>, enter population at 1, 3 and 5 miles, daytime population and median household income.","Paste a Placer.ai report into the box and click <b>✨ Pull key figures</b>. AI adds visits, trend, trade area, dwell time, peak dayparts, top origins, visitor profile and highlights to <b>Foot-traffic summary</b>, below anything already there."]},"contacts":{"lead":"Who to call and what it costs, per center.","body":["<b>Contacts & leasing economics</b> holds asking rent low and high ($/SF), NNN / CAM, occupancy and available suites, then name, phone and email for the <b>Landlord</b>, <b>Property manager</b> and <b>Leasing broker</b>."]},"docs":{"lead":"The center's paper, kept with the center.","body":["Save the center, open it again and use <b>＋ Add document</b> under <b>Documents & map</b> — site plans, rent rolls, aerials, flyers (up to 25 MB each). Brochures filed by auto-import or <a href=\"#brochures\">Auto-fill from brochures</a> show here too.","Opening the section drops a pin on the map from the address, city and market."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Where did these centers come from? I didn't add them.","a":"When a space is imported from a broker email (auto-import or the email scan), its shopping center is created automatically if it's new, and filled from the flyer."},{"q":"Why can't I delete a center?","a":"Only admins can delete. Deleting unlinks the center's spaces but doesn't remove them."},{"q":"Enrich centers didn't add photos.","a":"Photos come from Google when an admin has set a Google Maps key; otherwise only from flyers on the linked spaces."}]},"mass_studio":{"intro":"Campaigns is the Email Studio: branded email blasts to your subscriber lists, sent through Amazon SES with unsubscribes, bounces and complaints handled for you.","overview":["Campaigns lives under Marketing and is admin-only. It sends list-wide blasts and newsletters, not one-off emails to a contact. Subscribers come from the <a href=\"rrg_mail_subscribers.html\">Subscribers</a> page, and every send skips anyone on the shared suppression list.","The status bar at the top tells you whether the mail engine is ready. <b>Storage not connected</b> means the database is missing and nothing works. <b>Sending is off</b> means you can draft and preview but not send. <b>Connected</b> shows the From address and the send rate. Broker requirement blasts started from a tenant engagement land here too, so every scheduled blast shows up in one queue."],"feats":{"compose":{"lead":"Write the email in a visual block editor that already carries your firm's logo, colors and email style.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New campaign</span>. Fill in <b>Campaign name</b> (internal only), <b>Subject line</b>, <b>Preheader</b> (the preview text after the subject), <b>From name</b>, <b>From email</b> and an optional <b>Reply-to</b>.","In the <b>Design</b> card, <span class=\"kbd\">Visual</span> builds the email from blocks: header, hero image, title, stats, text, highlights, button, signature and footer. <span class=\"kbd\">HTML</span> switches to the raw code for full control. If you paste custom HTML the visual editor can't read, switching back to Visual warns you before it replaces your edits.","Use <b>{{first_name}}</b>, <b>{{last_name}}</b> and <b>{{email}}</b> to personalize the email and the preheader. Every send gets a footer with an <b>Update preferences</b> link and a one-click <b>Unsubscribe</b> link (plus your firm name and postal address when they're set on the server), so you don't have to add them yourself.","<span class=\"kbd\">Open preview</span> opens the email in a new tab with sample data (Jane Doe)."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New campaign</span>","Fill in the name, subject and preheader","Edit the blocks under <b>Design</b>","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save draft</span>"],"tip":"The logo, colors and the email style (modern, bold or warm) come from Admin branding, so every blast looks the same without anyone fiddling with it."},"listing":{"lead":"Start a new-listing blast from the listing record instead of typing it in.","body":["In the <b>Design</b> card click <span class=\"kbd\">Pull from listing</span> and type at least two letters of the listing name. Pick one from the results.","The editor rebuilds the email with a <b>New Listing</b> headline, a stats row (<b>Asking Price</b>, <b>Location</b>, <b>Listing #</b>, <b>Status</b>), an opening line that greets the reader by first name, three highlight placeholders, a <b>View this listing</b> button with an NDA note, and a placeholder hero image marked as representative.","Pulling a listing replaces what's in the editor, so do it first and write your highlights after."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">Pull from listing</span>","Type the listing name and pick it","Fill in the highlights, the hero photo and the button link"],"tip":"The button links to rrgcre.com by default. Point it at the listing's CIM or landing page before you send."},"audience":{"lead":"Choose who gets the blast with the <b>Send to</b> dropdown.","body":["<b>Send to</b> lists <b>All active subscribers (whole list)</b> plus every list with its member count. You build lists on the <a href=\"rrg_mail_subscribers.html\">Subscribers</a> page.","The recipient list is built when the email goes out: active subscribers in that audience, minus anyone on the suppression list. Each scheduled repeat builds it again, so people who unsubscribed or bounced since the last send drop out.","Broker requirement blasts sent from a tenant engagement target <b>Brokers · area-matched</b>: broker subscribers who chose all areas or one of the engagement's markets. The <b>Audience</b> column shows which kind of audience each campaign uses."],"tip":"Check the list's member count in the dropdown before you send. A list with zero members sends nothing."},"test":{"lead":"Send yourself a proof, then send the blast.","body":["In the bottom bar, type an address in the test box and click <span class=\"kbd\">Send test</span>. The draft saves first, and the test goes out with the preheader and footer just like the real thing; the subject is marked [TEST] and name fields come out blank.","<span class=\"kbd\">Send campaign</span> saves, asks you to confirm, and starts sending right away. This can't be undone. Emails go out at the throttled rate shown in the status bar.","Once a campaign has started, opening it shows a status card with <b>Audience</b>, <b>Sent</b>, <b>Failed</b>, <b>Opens</b>, <b>Clicks</b>, <b>Bounces</b>, <b>Complaints</b> and <b>Unsubs</b>; while it's sending, the card updates on its own every few seconds. Use <span class=\"kbd\">Pause</span> to stop a send in progress and <span class=\"kbd\">Resume</span> to pick up where it left off.","Both send buttons stay grayed out until Amazon SES is set up on the server."],"steps":["Type your email in the test box","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Send test</span> and check it on your phone","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Send campaign</span> and confirm"],"tip":"A campaign that's sending can't be deleted. Pause it first."},"schedule":{"lead":"Click <b>Schedule</b> on any campaign row to set one send or a repeating series without opening the editor.","body":["The <b>Schedule Sends</b> window has a <b>Recurrence</b> section: <b>Frequency</b> (Does not repeat, Daily, Weekly or Monthly), <b>First send</b>, <b>Repeat every</b> and <b>Number of sends</b>. The <b>Sends</b> table below shows every date before you save. New rows are marked <b>New</b> and existing ones <b>Scheduled</b>.","Unless you tick <b>Allow weekends & holidays</b>, any date that lands on a weekend or a major US holiday moves to the next business day and is tagged <i>moved to next business day</i>. Use the box only for something like a holiday greeting.","Only one blast per morning and one per afternoon can go out across the whole firm. If another campaign already has that slot, the server skips your send and the message tells you how many were skipped and why. You can schedule up to 52 sends on one campaign.","Every scheduled send goes to the campaign's audience again. A/B testing only applies to the first send."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">Schedule</span> on the campaign row","Pick a Frequency and the first send date and time","Check the dates in the <b>Sends</b> table","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Schedule N sends</span>"],"tip":"You can also cancel any saved send right from that table."},"cadence":{"lead":"Load your firm's standard blast series into a campaign with one button.","body":["In the campaign editor's <b>Schedule</b> card, <span class=\"kbd\">Standard cadence</span> fills the send list. Out of the box that's 12 sends on weekdays at 9 AM: the first 6 about 26 days apart and the rest about 30. Weekends and major holidays are skipped. It starts from the <b>Send time</b> date if you set one, otherwise tomorrow.","You can also add single times with <span class=\"kbd\">+ Add this time</span>, or build an every-N-days or every-N-weeks run with <b>Or make it repeat</b> and <span class=\"kbd\">Generate</span>. Each pending time has <b>reschedule</b> and <b>remove</b> links. Nothing is booked until you click <span class=\"kbd\">Schedule these times</span>.","Tick <b>Deliberate send</b> to allow a weekend or holiday date on purpose.","The defaults (number of sends, AM and PM time, gaps) are set in <a href=\"rrg_admin_settings.html\">Admin Settings</a> under <b>Campaign send cadence</b>."],"steps":["Open the campaign","Optionally set a <b>Send time</b> to start from","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Standard cadence</span>","Adjust dates, then click <span class=\"kbd\">Schedule these times</span>"],"tip":"The editor and the row's <b>Schedule</b> window both allow up to 52 sends per campaign — one a week for a year."},"queue":{"lead":"The <b>Scheduled</b> tab lists every send that hasn't gone out yet, including broker blasts from tenant engagements.","body":["The summary line shows how many sends are scheduled, when the next one goes out, and how many fall this week and this month.","Search by campaign or subject, filter by <b>All</b>, <b>Broker</b> or <b>Campaigns</b>, and set the <b>Range</b> to 7 days, 30 days or All. Each row shows <b>Date</b>, <b>Time</b>, <b>When</b> (like <i>in 3d</i>), <b>Audience</b>, an estimated <b>Recipients</b> count and the <b>Type</b>. Sends that override the weekend and holiday blackout are tagged <b>Deliberate</b>.","<b>Open</b> opens the campaign. <b>Reschedule</b> lets you pick a new date and time, at least a minute out. <b>Cancel</b> drops that send after you confirm."],"tip":"The tab opens on a 7-day range. Click <b>All</b> under Range to see the whole queue."},"ab":{"lead":"Find out which subject line gets opened before the whole list sees it.","body":["In the editor's <b>A/B split test</b> card, tick <b>Enable</b>. Enter <b>Subject B (variant)</b>, a <b>Test audience %</b> (default 30), <b>Winner by</b> (Open rate or Click rate) and <b>Decide after (hours)</b> (default 4).","When you send, a random slice of that size is split evenly between subject A and subject B. Everyone else waits. Once the test emails are out and the hours have passed, the subject with the better rate goes to the rest automatically. A tie goes to A.","The campaign row shows an <b>A/B</b> badge, and once the test is decided it shows which subject won, like <b>A/B · B won</b>."],"tip":"Open rate is the safe default. Links are tracked, so <b>Click rate</b> works too — it picks the subject that drew more unique clicks."},"results":{"lead":"See how each blast did without leaving the list.","body":["The tiles at the top show <b>Campaigns</b> (and how many are sending now), <b>Emails · 30 days</b>, <b>Emails sent</b> all time, <b>Opens</b> with the open rate, <b>Clicks</b> and <b>Active subscribers</b>. Archived campaigns aren't counted.","The list has tabs for <b>All</b>, <b>Drafts</b>, <b>Scheduled</b>, <b>Sent</b> and <b>Archived</b>. Every column sorts: <b>Times sent</b>, <b>Recipients</b>, <b>Opens</b>, <b>Clicks</b>, <b>Unsubs</b>, <b>Complaints</b> and <b>Bounces</b> (each with its %), <b>Created</b>, <b>Date sent</b> and <b>Last sent</b>. A row also shows the next scheduled date and how many more sends are queued.","Hover a <b>Clicks</b> figure to see unique versus total clicks. <b>Export</b> downloads a CSV of every recipient with status, sent time, opened time, first click time and click count. <b>Preview</b> opens the email filled in with sample data."],"tip":"Opens come from a tracking pixel, so recipients who block images won't show up as opens even if they read the email."},"history":{"lead":"Click <b>History</b> on a sent campaign to see every send on its own row.","body":["Each send (#1, #2 …) shows <b>Date sent</b>, <b>Recipients</b>, <b>Sent</b>, <b>Opens</b>, <b>Clicks</b>, <b>Unsubs</b>, <b>Complaints</b> and <b>Bounces</b>, with a bold <b>Total</b> row at the bottom.","Unsubscribes aren't stamped with a send number, so each one is counted against the most recent send before it happened.","Use it to see whether a long drip is wearing the list out. If unsubscribes climb send over send, it's time to change the message."],"tip":"The <b>History</b> link only appears once a campaign has gone out at least once."},"calendar":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">📅 Blast calendar</span> gives you a link the whole team can subscribe to.","body":["The window shows a private link and <span class=\"kbd\">Copy</span>, plus how to add it in Google Calendar, on an iPhone or in Outlook. Subscribers get a separate <b>Email Blasts</b> calendar with every scheduled send and recently finished blasts, and it updates on its own.","If the link gets passed around outside the firm, click <span class=\"kbd\">Reset link</span>. The old link stops working and everyone has to subscribe again with the new one.","Scheduled and sent blasts also show on the in-app Calendar for every signed-in teammate. That view is read-only."],"tip":"Reps can see what's going out to the market and when, so nobody sends a duplicate blast the same morning."},"suppress":{"lead":"Nobody on the suppression list gets another blast from any list.","body":["Every email carries a one-click unsubscribe header that Gmail and Apple Mail show as an Unsubscribe button, plus footer links to <b>Unsubscribe</b> or <b>Update preferences</b>. On the preferences page the recipient can pick the areas and lists they want, or stop everything.","Hard bounces and spam complaints reported by Amazon SES go on the suppression list automatically. Every address is checked again right before its email goes out.","For someone who asked off by phone or reply, click <span class=\"kbd\">✖ Mark unsubscribed</span>, paste the addresses (one per line, commas work too) and click <span class=\"kbd\">Mark unsubscribed</span>. To unsubscribe a whole file, use <b>Import</b> on the Subscribers page with the status set to Unsubscribed."],"tip":"Suppression holds until an admin restores the address on the Subscribers page or the person re-subscribes from their preferences page."},"manage":{"lead":"Keep the campaign list down to what's live.","body":["<b>Archive</b> moves a campaign to the <b>Archived</b> tab and keeps its results. <b>Restore</b> brings it back. Archived campaigns are left out of the KPI tiles.","<b>Delete</b> removes the campaign along with its send history, opens and anything still scheduled. A draft only needs a confirm. A campaign that has already gone out makes you type <b>DELETE</b>. A campaign that's sending can't be deleted until you pause it."],"tip":"Archive instead of deleting anything that went out. You'll want the numbers later."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why is the Send campaign button grayed out?","a":"Amazon SES isn't set up on the server yet. The status bar will say <b>Sending is off</b>. You can still write, save and preview drafts."},{"q":"My scheduled send didn't get booked. Why?","a":"Only one blast per morning and one per afternoon can go out across the firm, and weekends and holidays are blocked unless you allow them. The message after you schedule tells you how many were skipped and why. Pick another slot."},{"q":"Does a repeat send go to people who unsubscribed after the first one?","a":"No. Each send builds its recipient list again from active subscribers and checks the suppression list right before each email goes out."},{"q":"How are link clicks counted?","a":"Every link in a campaign is swapped for a signed tracking link that counts the click and sends the reader straight on to the real page. <b>Clicks</b> shows unique clicks (the first click per recipient); hover it for total clicks."}]},"mail_subscribers":{"intro":"Subscribers is everyone who can get an email blast: brokers and restaurant operators, the areas they care about, the lists they're on, and whether they can still be emailed.","overview":["Subscribers lives under Marketing and is admin-only. It's a separate list from your CRM contacts. These are the people <a href=\"rrg_mass_studio.html\">Campaigns</a> and broker requirement blasts send to. Website listing-alert sign-ups land here automatically.","The page opens to a prompt instead of dumping thousands of rows on you. Search, tap a tile or chip, or use <b>⚙ Filters</b> to pull up the slice you need. Click any address to open its record."],"feats":{"import":{"lead":"Load a spreadsheet of addresses in one go, even a big one.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Import</span>. Choose a CSV with <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Choose CSV file</span>, or paste either a header row and data or one email per line. The importer finds the email column wherever it sits and whatever it's called, and reads first name, last name, status and type columns when they're there.","Set a <b>Source label</b> (the file name fills in for you), a <b>List type</b> (Brokers or Restaurants), a <b>Default status</b> (Active, Unsubscribed or Bounced) and optionally <b>Add to list</b>, including <b>+ New list…</b>.","Large files go up in batches of 500 with a progress bar, and names, status and type carry through on every batch whatever the columns are called. At the end you see how many were added, updated, skipped and already unsubscribed. Someone who opted out before is never turned back on by an Active import. Imported people get email for all areas until you narrow it.","Each import is posted once to the activity feed."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⬆ Import</span>","Choose the file or paste the addresses","Pick the List type and, if you want, a list","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Import</span> and keep the window open until it finishes"],"tip":"To unsubscribe a whole file of opt-outs at once, import it with <b>Default status</b> set to Unsubscribed."},"tiles":{"lead":"The tiles show how big and how healthy your audience is, and the type tiles double as filters.","body":["The first four tiles are <b>Total</b>, <b>Active</b>, <b>Unsubscribed</b> and <b>Bounced</b> (complaints count as bounced). Next come <b>Brokers</b>, <b>Restaurants</b> and <b>Untyped</b>, each with how many are still active.","Click a type tile to filter the list to that group. Click it again to clear it. <b>Untyped</b> is your cleanup list: subscribers who still need a Broker or Restaurant tag before a typed blast can reach them.","The chip bar below has the same quick filters: <b>Status</b> (All, Active, Unsubscribed, Bounced, each with a count) and <b>Type</b>."],"tip":"Broker requirement blasts only go to subscribers typed <b>Broker</b>, so keep the Untyped count low."},"filter":{"lead":"Narrow thousands of subscribers down to exactly who you'd send to.","body":["The search box matches every word you type against email, name, type, source, lists and areas, so <i>houston broker</i> finds brokers interested in Houston. It remembers your last search the next time you open the page.","<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> adds stacking filters: <b>Status</b>, <b>Type</b>, <b>Area of interest</b>, <b>List</b>, <b>Source</b> and a <b>Date added</b> range. They combine with the tiles and chips.","Columns sort and resize: <b>Email</b>, <b>Name</b>, <b>Type</b>, <b>Status</b>, <b>Areas of interest</b>, <b>Source</b>, <b>Lists</b> and <b>Date added</b>. Newest additions show first."],"tip":"Filter to a group, then use <a href=\"#lists\">Add to list</a> to turn it into an audience you can send to."},"lists":{"lead":"A list is a saved audience, like <i>Houston Brokers</i>, that a campaign can target.","body":["Filter the grid, tick the rows you want (or select all), and pick the <b>Add to list</b> bulk action. Choose an existing list or <b>+ New list…</b>. People already on the list are skipped, and the message tells you how many were added.","<span class=\"kbd\">☰ Lists</span> shows every list with its member count, and lets you <b>Rename</b> or <b>Delete</b> a list or <b>+ Create</b> an empty one. Deleting a list keeps the subscribers. A campaign that pointed at the deleted list goes back to sending to all active subscribers.","Lists are re-checked against the suppression list on every send, so you never have to scrub a list before a blast."],"steps":["Filter to the group you want","Tick the rows","Choose <b>Add to list</b> and pick or name the list","In Campaigns, pick that list under <b>Send to</b>"],"tip":"Before you delete a list, check whether a scheduled campaign is still using it. Otherwise that campaign goes to everyone."},"bulk":{"lead":"Clean up a batch of addresses in one move.","body":["Tick the rows and pick <b>Unsubscribe</b> from the bulk actions. Those addresses go on the suppression list and won't get any campaign or broker blast from any list.","<b>Restore</b> does the reverse. It takes the addresses off the suppression list and sets them back to Active. Use it only when someone was taken off by mistake or asked to come back.","Unsubscribes from email links, hard bounces and spam complaints are added automatically. You don't have to do anything for those."],"tip":"Restoring an address that bounced or complained puts your sender reputation at risk. Only restore people who really asked back in."},"record":{"lead":"Every subscriber has a full record page.","body":["Click an email to open the record. Under <b>Subscriber details</b>, edit first and last name, <b>Type</b> (Broker, Restaurant, Buyer or Investor), <b>Status</b> (Active or Unsubscribed / suppressed) and <b>Areas of interest</b>. Under <b>Lists</b>, tick or untick the lists they belong to.","If the address is suppressed, a <b>Suppression</b> card shows why (unsubscribe, bounce, complaint or manual), the detail and the date. Set Status to <b>Active</b> and save to restore it.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save changes</span> to save. The header shows the source and the date they were added."],"tip":"Broker requirement blasts only reach subscribers typed exactly <b>Broker</b>."},"engagement":{"lead":"See one subscriber's history with your blasts.","body":["The record's <b>Engagement</b> card totals <b>Sent</b>, <b>Opened</b> and <b>Clicked</b>. <b>Campaign history</b> lists every campaign they were sent, newest first, with the subject, delivery status, sent time, opened time and a ✓ under <b>Clicked</b> if they clicked a link.","Opens come from a tracking pixel, so someone who blocks images can read the email and still show no open. Clicks are counted through tracked links, so a click is a surer sign of interest than an open."],"tip":"Someone who opens every listing blast is a warm buyer. Pull them into the CRM and call them."},"areas":{"lead":"Areas of interest decide which area-matched blasts a subscriber gets.","body":["On the record, <b>Areas of interest</b> is either <b>All areas</b> or <b>Specific areas</b>, picked from your firm's subscriber-areas list. A broker requirement blast for a Houston tenant only goes to brokers set to all areas or to Houston.","Every campaign footer has an <b>Update preferences</b> link. It opens a private page with no login where the subscriber can change their name, areas and lists, or unsubscribe from everything or sign back up. Their changes save straight to the record here.","Website listing-alert sign-ups come in with the markets they picked already set."],"tip":"Keep the subscriber-areas list short, just the metros you actually send blasts for."},"website":{"lead":"You don't have to re-key website sign-ups.","body":["When someone signs up for listing alerts on the website, they're added here with source <b>Website</b>. Brokers are typed <b>Broker</b>, operators are typed <b>Restaurant</b>, and the markets they ticked become their areas of interest. Someone who ticked no markets gets all areas.","Filter <b>Source</b> to Website to see who came in that way."],"tip":"Someone who unsubscribed earlier and then signs up again on the website stays suppressed here. Restore them by hand if they really want back in."},"export":{"lead":"Take a segment out of the system when you need to.","body":["<span class=\"kbd\">⬇ Export</span> saves the filtered list to CSV. If you've ticked rows, only those go out. <span class=\"kbd\">⎙ Print</span> works the same way. <span class=\"kbd\">▦ Columns</span> picks which columns show.","Export and Print only show up for admins and users with the <b>Export & print lists</b> permission."],"tip":"Filter first. An export of the whole audience is rarely what you need."},"legacy":{"lead":"A one-time cleanup that only shows up while the old list still has records.","body":["A yellow <b>Old subscriber list</b> banner lists what's still in the pre-Studio store. Nothing sends from that list anymore.","Leave <b>Copy into the Email Studio first (opt-outs carried over)</b> ticked and click <span class=\"kbd\">Retire old list</span>. The records are copied in and the old store is retired. A backup is kept on the server."],"tip":"Once it's retired the banner goes away for good. Nothing else to do."}},"faq":[{"q":"How is this different from Contacts?","a":"Contacts is your CRM. Subscribers is only the email audience, with its own areas, lists and unsubscribes. Adding someone to one doesn't add them to the other."},{"q":"Why is the list empty when I open the page?","a":"It opens blank on purpose. Search, tap a tile or chip, or use <b>⚙ Filters</b> to pull up records."},{"q":"Can an import turn an unsubscribed person back on?","a":"No. An Active import skips anyone already on the suppression list. Only <b>Restore</b>, setting the record's Status to Active, or the person re-subscribing from their preferences page brings them back."},{"q":"Who gets a broker requirement blast?","a":"Active subscribers typed <b>Broker</b> who chose all areas or one of the engagement's markets, minus anyone suppressed."}]},"email_templates":{"intro":"Email Templates is the firm's library of canned emails: buyer replies, seller follow-ups, NDA requests. Write one once, and it fills in the right names every time you send it.","overview":["Email Templates lives under Marketing. You see your own Personal templates plus every Shared template the firm has published. A folder rail on the left keeps the library organized. The list shows name, folder, visibility, subject, owner and when each one was last updated.","Templates get used in two places. One is the email composer on a contact, where you pick a template and the subject, body and attachments drop in. The other is an automation email step, where you start the step from a template."],"feats":{"reuse":{"lead":"Stop retyping the same buyer reply and seller follow-up.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New template</span>, give it a <b>Template name</b>, a <b>Subject</b> and a <b>Body</b>, and click <span class=\"kbd\">Save</span>. The body editor has formatting and a <b>{ }</b> menu for merge fields.","When you email a contact, the <b>Template</b> picker lists your templates grouped by folder. Choose one and the subject, body and any attachments fill in. Edit before you send if you want.","Shared templates show <i>(shared)</i> after the name in the picker."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New template</span>","Name it, write the subject and body","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save</span>","On a contact, open the email composer and pick it from <b>Template</b>"],"tip":"Name templates by who they're for and when, like <i>Buyer — follow-up (day 5)</i>. The picker sorts by name."},"tokens":{"lead":"Tokens make a canned email read like you wrote it for that person.","body":["Put a token in the subject or body and it's replaced when the email goes out. <b>Contact</b>: {{first_name}}, {{last_name}}, {{name}}, {{company}}, {{title}}, {{email}}, {{phone}}. <b>You</b>: {{my_name}}, {{my_title}}, {{my_phone}}, {{my_email}}. <b>Listing</b>: {{listing_name}}, {{listing_number}}. <b>Other</b>: {{today}}.","In the editor, use the <b>{ }</b> menu in the body toolbar to insert one without typing the braces.","A token with no value for that contact, like a buyer with no company, comes out blank. A misspelled token prints as written, so check your spelling."],"tip":"Put {{first_name}} in the subject line. Personal subject lines get opened."},"visibility":{"lead":"Every template is either Personal (only you) or Shared (the whole firm).","body":["Set <b>Visibility</b> in the editor, or click the <b>Visibility</b> cell in the list. Only admins can publish a template as <b>Shared</b>. Anyone else saving it as Shared gets told to save it as Personal or ask an admin.","You can only edit or delete templates you own. A teammate's Shared template opens as <b>View template</b>, read-only. Use <span class=\"kbd\">+ New template</span> to make your own version.","<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> narrows the list by <b>Visibility</b> or <b>Owner</b>."],"tip":"Admins: publish the emails you want every rep sending word-for-word, like the NDA request and the BOV cover note, as Shared."},"folders":{"lead":"Keep a growing library findable.","body":["The rail on the left shows <b>All templates</b>, <b>Unfiled</b> and every folder with a count. Click <b>+</b> to create one. Hover a folder for <b>✎</b> (rename) or <b>×</b> (delete).","File a template by clicking its <b>Folder</b> cell in the list or setting <b>Folder</b> in the editor. Clicking <span class=\"kbd\">+ New template</span> while a folder is selected files the new one there.","Renaming a folder moves every template with it. Deleting a folder only makes its templates Unfiled. Folders are one level and the whole team shares them."],"tip":"The composer's Template picker uses the same folders, so a clean library here means a fast pick on a contact."},"inline":{"lead":"Quick fixes don't need the full editor.","body":["On your own templates, click the <b>Template</b>, <b>Folder</b>, <b>Visibility</b> or <b>Subject</b> cell to edit it in place. Folder and Visibility are dropdowns.","Only the field you change is saved, so the body and attachments stay as they are. Open <b>Edit</b> for the body."],"tip":"Coming back from a template puts you back on the same folder, search and scroll spot."},"attachments":{"lead":"Stop hunting for the same one-pager or NDA every time.","body":["In the template editor, under <b>Attachments</b>, click <span class=\"kbd\">+ Add file</span>. Save the template first, because new templates can't take files until they have been saved.","Each file can be up to 15 MB, and a template holds up to 10. When a rep picks the template on a contact, the files are attached to that email automatically.","Only the template's owner, or an admin for Shared templates, can add or remove files."],"tip":"Attach your firm one-pager or blank NDA to the first-response buyer template."},"starters":{"lead":"You don't start from a blank page.","body":["The library comes with a set of Shared templates owned by RRG. The core ten, on the buyer side: <i>first response</i>, <i>follow-up (day 5)</i>, <i>last note</i>, <i>NDA & qualification</i>. On the seller side: <i>exploring a sale</i>, <i>after the qualification call</i>, <i>valuation questionnaire request</i>, <i>your Broker's Opinion of Value</i>, <i>ready to go to market</i>, <i>we have an offer</i>.","They're written with merge fields already in place. Use them as they are, or open one and make your own Personal version."],"tip":"Admins can edit the Shared starters to match how your firm talks."},"automations":{"lead":"Templates are a fast way to write the emails your follow-up sequences send.","body":["In <a href=\"rrg_admin_automations.html\">Automations</a>, an email step has a <b>Start from a template</b> dropdown. Pick one and its subject and body copy into the step.","It's a copy. If you change the template later, the automation keeps the old wording until you load the template into the step again."],"tip":"After you rewrite a template that a sequence uses, reload it into that automation step."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I make a template Shared?","a":"Only admins can publish to the whole firm. Save it as Personal, or ask an admin to publish it."},{"q":"A teammate's template won't let me edit it.","a":"You can only edit templates you own. Shared templates from others open as view only. Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New template</span> to make your own version."},{"q":"Does editing a field in the list wipe the body?","a":"No. An inline edit saves only that field."},{"q":"Does deleting a folder delete its templates?","a":"No. They move to Unfiled."}]},"admin_automations":{"intro":"Automations are follow-up sequences: a list of steps (an email, a task, a heads-up or pop-up for the rep, a pipeline move, a new contact) spaced out by days, hours or minutes, run on a contact for you.","overview":["Automations lives under Marketing. Only admins can build or edit them here. Reps put contacts into them from the <b>Automations</b> card on a contact record, and other parts of the system can enroll contacts too, like a listing's buyer-lead plan, Gmail intake, a screening call outcome or an executed agreement.","Each contact moves through the steps in order. The system checks about every five minutes and runs any step that's due. Every email, task and note an automation creates is logged on the contact as done by <i>Automation</i>."],"feats":{"build":{"lead":"An automation is a named list of steps that run one after another.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New automation</span>, name it (like <i>Buyer inquiry follow-up</i>) and add steps with <span class=\"kbd\">+ Email step</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Task step</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Notify step</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Log activity</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Update assignment</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Move stage</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">+ Pop-up alert</span> or <span class=\"kbd\">+ Create contact</span>. You can switch a step's type with the dropdown at its top.","Each step has a <b>Timing</b> line: the first step runs that long after the contact is enrolled (0 means right away), and each later step waits that long after the one before it. Give a step an optional <b>Step name</b>, and use <b>↑</b>, <b>↓</b> and <b>✕</b> to reorder or remove it.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save</span>. Empty steps — no subject or body, no task title, no notification message, no alert text, no contact name, or no pipeline picked — are dropped when you save."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New automation</span> and name it","Add your steps and set each one's timing","Leave <b>Active</b> ticked","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save</span>"],"tip":"Steps run on a roughly five-minute check, so a 2-minute wait really means up to about 5."},"email":{"lead":"An email step sends a personalized email to the enrolled contact.","body":["Write a <b>Subject</b> and <b>Body</b>, or use <b>Start from a template</b> to copy in one of your <a href=\"rrg_email_templates.html\">Email Templates</a>. Click a token chip (First name, Company, Listing name, Data room link and others) to drop it into the field you clicked last. The <b>My name</b>, <b>My email</b> and <b>My phone</b> chips fill in with the rep who enrolled the contact — or the listing's rep, then the contact's owner — and fall back to the firm's details.","Emails go out from the firm's address. When a rep enrolls the contact from the contact record, replies go to that rep. Each email is tracked for opens and logged in the contact's email history and activity.","If email isn't set up, the contact has no valid address, or they've unsubscribed, that step is skipped and noted, and the sequence keeps going."],"tip":"Loading a template copies it into the step — editing the template later doesn't change automations already using it. Load it into the step again to pick up the new version."},"task":{"lead":"A task step puts the next human touch on the rep's task list.","body":["Enter a <b>Task title</b> and an optional <b>Task note</b>. Tokens work here too. When the step runs, the task is assigned to the rep who enrolled the contact and is due that day.","The task is also logged on the contact's activity."],"tip":"Mix emails and calls: email on day 0, call task on day 2, second email on day 5."},"notify":{"lead":"A notify step tells a person, not the contact, that something needs doing.","body":["Write the <b>Notification message</b>, like <i>{{first_name}} is a hot buyer — call today</i>. Under <b>Notify who?</b> enter a teammate's email, or leave it blank to alert the rep who enrolled the contact from their record (automatic enrollments alert the firm's sending address instead). The email includes the contact's name, company, email and a link to their record.","Set <b>Send as</b> to <b>Text (SMS) to the rep</b> to text the enrolling rep's mobile instead. That option only appears once texting is turned on (see <a href=\"#sms\">Text notifications</a>).","A <b>Pop-up alert</b> step (<i>Pop-up alert to rep</i>) shows an <b>Alert title</b> and <b>Alert message</b> on screen for the rep who enrolled the contact, the next time they have the app open. Tokens work in both."],"tip":"Use a notify step right before a call task so the rep gets the nudge on their phone."},"deal":{"lead":"Some steps update the CRM instead of sending anything.","body":["<b>Log activity</b> writes a note of the <b>Activity type</b> you choose on the contact, like <i>Engagement agreement executed</i>.","<b>Update assignment</b> can <b>Set status</b> (New, Live, Under Contract, Closed, On Hold, Lost), <b>Advance pipeline stage</b> (Outreach through Closing) and <b>Mark the listing live</b>. It only works when the automation was started from a listing: a pipeline stage change, or an executed agreement that's linked to a listing.","<b>Move to pipeline & stage</b> puts the linked listing into the pipeline and stage you pick (or that pipeline's first stage). It's skipped when the contact has no linked listing. Pipelines are set up under Settings → Pipelines.","<b>Create contact</b> adds a new contact (name required; company, email, phone and notes optional — tokens work) marked as referred by the enrolled contact. Its company is matched by name or created."],"tip":"Pair these with the executed-agreement trigger — tick <b>Default automation for executed listing agreements</b> on the automation: signed listing agreement, then status Live, stage Agreed, and a kickoff task for the rep."},"timeline":{"lead":"Under the steps, <b>Process timeline</b> lays out the whole sequence.","body":["Each row shows the step number, its name and type, the gap since the step before and the running total, like <i>+3d · 8d</i>. The bottom line is the <b>Total process length</b>.","Compare it with how long the real process takes. If buyers usually go quiet after two weeks, a 45-day drip is too long."],"tip":"The timeline updates as soon as you change a wait."},"enroll":{"lead":"An automation does nothing until a contact is enrolled in it.","body":["<b>By hand:</b> on a contact record, the <b>Automations</b> card has <b>Add to an automation…</b> and <span class=\"kbd\">Enroll</span>, and lets the rep <b>Pause</b>, <b>Resume</b>, <b>Stop</b> or remove a running one. A task can also start an automation through <b>Run automation</b>.","<b>Automatically:</b> a listing can have a lead automation that every BizBuySell buyer lead for it goes into. Gmail intake, screening call outcomes and answers, booking types and appointments, signed agreements and agreement expiry reminders can each be set to start one. A pipeline stage's <b>On enter</b> / <b>On leave</b> automation (Settings → Pipelines) runs on the listing's seller contact when a listing moves, and on the buyer when a buyer moves on the Buyers board or the listing page.","A contact can't be in the same automation twice at once. Inactive automations don't enroll anyone.","Tick <b>Default automation for executed listing agreements</b> on one automation and it runs for the client whenever a listing agreement is fully executed, unless that agreement names its own. Only one can be the default (a private one becomes shared), and it shows a <b>Listing-agreement default</b> badge in the list."],"tip":"Turn off <b>Active</b> to stop new enrollments without deleting the automation. Contacts already in it keep going."},"visibility":{"lead":"Control who sees an automation in the enroll pickers.","body":["Set <b>Visibility</b> to <b>Shared — everyone on the team can use it</b> or <b>Private — only you (and admins)</b>. The list shows each automation's <b>Status</b> (Active or Off), visibility and owner, number of steps and when it was last updated.","Untick <b>Active (reps can apply it)</b> to retire an automation without deleting it. Deleting one doesn't erase the history on contacts who already went through it."],"tip":"Keep test sequences Private until they're ready."},"sms":{"lead":"Turn on texting for notify steps across all automations.","body":["Above the list, tick <b>Allow text (SMS) notifications in automations</b>. The box stays grayed out until SMS (Twilio) is set up under Settings → SMS.","Once it's on, notify steps get a <b>Text (SMS) to the rep</b> option. Each rep needs a mobile number on their account, or the text is skipped and noted."],"tip":"Use texts for time-sensitive nudges only. Reps tune out a phone that buzzes all day."}},"faq":[{"q":"A step said it was skipped. Why?","a":"Email steps skip contacts with no valid email, contacts who unsubscribed, or when email isn't set up. Listing steps skip when no listing is linked. The sequence keeps going either way."},{"q":"If I change an automation, do contacts already in it get the new steps?","a":"Yes. Contacts already in it pick up the saved steps from wherever they are in the sequence. If the step a contact was on has been removed and nothing is left after it, their run is marked complete instead of hanging."},{"q":"How do I stop one contact's sequence?","a":"On the contact, in the <b>Automations</b> card, click <b>Pause</b> or <b>Stop</b>."}]},"commission":{"intro":"Your personal commission statement: every deal you own, run through the firm's commission waterfall, with what's been paid to you and what's still coming.","overview":["<b>Commissions</b> (Financials in the sidebar) is your running statement. It pulls every deal on listings you own, takes the gross commission entered on each deal, and runs it down the waterfall — referral off the top, co-broke side split, then your split of the company dollar — so you see your net, not just the firm's gross.","The numbers come straight from the <b>Deal</b> card on each listing file. If a figure looks wrong here, open the deal (click its name) and fix <b>Commission due</b>, <b>Commission paid</b>, the co-broke or the referral there — this page updates the next time it loads."],"feats":{"statement":{"lead":"The tiles across the top sum up where you stand on every deal you own.","body":["<b>Your net — earned</b> is your share of commission on closed deals, with the count of closings and your split %. <b>Owed to you</b> is your share of commission the firm hasn't collected yet; <b>Paid to you</b> is your share of what it has.","<b>Your net — pipeline</b> projects your share on open deals. <b>Firm GCI</b> is the gross commission on your closed deals before anything comes off. <b>Referrals + co-broke</b> is what went out the door to referral sources and co-brokers on those closings.","Below the tiles, the <b>Closed — earned</b> table shows each closing line by line; <b>Open pipeline — projected</b> shows the deals still in play."],"tip":"Only deals that have a Deal card (a transaction) show up — a listing with no buyer deal yet isn't on your statement."},"waterfall":{"lead":"Every row walks the gross commission down to your take-home.","body":["The order is fixed: <b>GCI</b> (the deal's Commission due) → <b>Referral</b> (Referral % off the top) → <b>Co-broke</b> (whatever isn't our side's share) → <b>Company $</b> → your <b>Split</b> % of the company dollar → <b>Your net</b>.","Referral and co-broke come from the deal: <b>Referral to</b> / <b>Referral % off the top</b> and <b>Co-broker</b> / <b>Our side share %</b> on the Deal card. If the deal has no referral %, the listing's referral % is used. A blank Our side share means we're sole (100%).","Your split % is your personal split if an admin set one on your user record (<b>Settings → Users</b>), otherwise the firm default from <b>Settings → Commission Structure</b>.","The referral and co-broke names show under the deal name so you can see who got paid."],"tip":"The Deal card on the listing previews the company dollar (GCI less referral and co-broke) while you type — enter it right there and both screens agree."},"owed":{"lead":"See exactly how much of your money is still out there.","body":["For each deal, <b>Paid</b> is your net times the share of GCI the firm has received; <b>Owed</b> is the rest. If half the commission has come in, half your net shows as paid.","\"Received\" means the <b>Commission paid</b> amount on the deal's Deal card. The <b>Payment</b> pill (Unpaid, Invoiced, Partial, Paid) is the deal's <b>Commission status</b>. Both follow the payments recorded on the deal's invoices.","Owed amounts show in red so they jump out."],"tip":"Record the client's payment on the deal's invoice — Commission paid and the Payment pill update from it, so Owed moves on its own."},"pipeline":{"lead":"What your open deals pay you if they close as written.","body":["The <b>Open pipeline — projected</b> table lists every deal on your listings that isn't closed, with its status (LOI, Under Contract, Due Diligence and so on) and expected close date.","The math is the same waterfall as closed deals, using the Commission due entered on the deal. Deals with no commission entered show dashes.","Both tables sort by close date, latest first."]},"year":{"lead":"Narrow the whole statement to this year.","body":["Use the <b>All time</b> / <b>This year</b> switch next to the statement name. This year keeps closed deals whose close date falls in the current year, plus open deals expected to close by year-end or with no expected close set.","Open deals expected to close after this year drop out of the pipeline table and tile while This year is on.","Every tile — net earned, owed, paid, pipeline, GCI, referrals and co-broke — and every table total is added up from exactly those deals."],"tip":"The CSV export follows the same switch."},"csv":{"lead":"Take your statement into Excel.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">Export CSV</span> at the top right. The file has one row per deal: Deal, Buyer, Status, Close, GCI, Referral, Co-broke, Company $, Split, Your net, Paid to you, Owed to you and Payment.","It exports whatever you're looking at — your statement (or the rep an admin picked), all time or this year."]},"deallink":{"lead":"Fix the source, not the statement.","body":["Every deal name links to its listing file. On the <b>Deal</b> card, click <span class=\"kbd\">Edit</span> to change <b>Commission due</b>, <b>Commission paid</b>, <b>Commission status</b>, <b>Co-broker</b>, <b>Our side share %</b>, <b>Referral to</b> or <b>Referral % off the top</b>.","Save the deal, come back, and the statement reflects it.","From the same Deal card, <span class=\"kbd\">Create invoice</span> bills the commission — see <a href=\"rrg_invoices.html\">Invoices</a>."]},"anyrep":{"lead":"Admins can look at any active rep's statement.","body":["Admins see a rep dropdown beside the statement title. Pick a name and the whole page — tiles, tables and the CSV — switches to that rep, using that rep's own split.","Reps only ever see their own statement; the server ignores a rep's attempt to request someone else's."],"tip":"Use it with Export CSV to hand a rep their statement at payout."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why didn't my Owed number drop after the client paid the invoice?","a":"Owed follows <b>Commission paid</b> on the deal's Deal card, which updates itself from payments recorded on the deal's invoices. If the money came in but nothing was recorded on an invoice, record the payment there (or update Commission paid on the deal)."},{"q":"Where does my split % come from?","a":"Your own split if an admin set one on your user record (<b>Settings → Users</b>); otherwise the firm default in <b>Settings → Commission Structure</b>. It's shown on the first tile."},{"q":"A closed deal is missing. Why?","a":"The deal must be on a listing you own and have a Deal card. Also check the switch — <b>This year</b> hides closings from earlier years and open deals expected after this year."}]},"invoices":{"intro":"Bill a commission or fee against a deal, take payment by check, wire or online, and watch the balance and status update themselves.","overview":["<b>Invoices</b> (Financials) is where the firm bills. Each invoice is tied to a deal or listing, gets the next <b>INV-</b> number automatically, and carries its own payments — so <b>Paid</b>, <b>Partial</b> and the balance are always current.","Invoices feed the rest of the books: payments roll into <a href=\"rrg_payments.html\">Payments</a>, revenue into <a href=\"rrg_pnl.html\">Profit & Loss</a>, and sent invoices post themselves to the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a>. Defaults — terms, tax, payment methods, items and online payments — are set in <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing</b>."],"feats":{"newinv":{"lead":"Create an invoice tied to a deal or listing.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New invoice</span>. Pick the <b>Deal / Listing</b>, enter <b>Bill to</b> and (optional) <b>Bill-to email</b>, set <b>Issued</b> and <b>Due</b>, then add line items with a description, income account and amount.","Status starts at <b>Draft</b>. Set it to <b>Sent</b> once it goes to the client — that's what posts it to the books; recording a payment on a Draft also issues it as Sent. <b>Void</b> takes it out of every total. <b>Partial</b> and <b>Paid</b> are set for you from the payments.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Save invoice</span>. The number (INV-1001, INV-1002 …) is assigned on first save."],"steps":["Click <b>+ New invoice</b>","Pick the <b>Deal / Listing</b> and fill in <b>Bill to</b>","Add lines with <b>+ Add line</b> (or pick a saved item)","Choose <b>Terms</b> and set <b>Status</b> to Sent when it goes out","Click <b>Save invoice</b>"],"tip":"Delete asks you to click twice — the second click on Really delete? removes the invoice and its payments."},"fromdeal":{"lead":"The fastest way to bill a closing.","body":["On a listing file's <b>Deal</b> card, click <span class=\"kbd\">Create invoice</span>. Invoices opens with a new invoice already tied to that deal, the listing contact as <b>Bill to</b>, and a <b>Brokerage commission — [business]</b> line for the commission due.","The <b>Invoices</b> card on listing, tenant-rep and landlord-rep files lists that deal's invoices with billed, collected and outstanding; its <span class=\"kbd\">+</span> opens a new invoice tied to that deal with the bill-to filled in (you add the lines).","The deal's <b>Commission status</b> follows its invoices: <b>Invoiced</b> once one is sent, <b>Partial</b> when part of the commission is collected, <b>Paid</b> when collections cover the commission due — and back again if a payment is removed or an invoice is voided or deleted. <b>Commission paid</b> on the Deal card tracks what's been collected (before tax)."],"tip":"A commission status or paid figure typed on the Deal card by hand counts as an override — invoices can raise it but never lower it."},"terms":{"lead":"Standard terms do the date math.","body":["The <b>Terms</b> list includes Due on receipt, Net 10, Net 15, Net 30, Due on Lease Execution, Due on Rent Commencement, 50% Execution / 50% Rent Commencement, Due at Closing and Upon Receipt of Landlord Payment.","Choosing Due on receipt or a <b>Net</b> term sets <b>Due</b> from the <b>Issued</b> date, and moves it again if you change the issue date. Event-based terms (closing, lease execution) leave the due date to you.","Admins can rewrite the list in <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Invoice payment terms</b>."]},"items":{"lead":"Reusable invoice lines for the fees you bill all the time.","body":["Next to <span class=\"kbd\">+ Add line</span>, the <b>+ Add item / service…</b> picker drops in a saved line with its description, rate and income account. It only appears once an admin has added items.","Each line carries an <b>Account</b> from the chart of accounts' income accounts (default 4000 Commission Income), so the ledger books the revenue to the right place.","Admins can turn on <b>Invoice line detail</b> to show Qty / Each / Extended columns; the extended amount computes as you type."],"tip":"Build the catalog in Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Items & services."},"payments":{"lead":"Take partial or full payments against any saved invoice.","body":["Open the invoice, scroll to <b>Payments</b>, enter <b>Date</b>, <b>Amount</b> and <b>Method / reference</b> (check #, wire confirmation), pick the method, and click <span class=\"kbd\">Record payment</span>.","The totals strip shows <b>Subtotal</b>, tax, <b>Total</b>, <b>Paid</b> and <b>Balance</b>. Status becomes <b>Partial</b> on the first payment and <b>Paid</b> once the balance hits zero. A payment on a Draft invoice issues it — it becomes <b>Sent</b> and is charged to A/R before the payment clears it.","Remove a payment with the <b>×</b> beside it. Payments can only be recorded after the invoice is saved."],"tip":"Every payment also shows on the Payments page, posts to Cash in the ledger, and moves the deal's commission status and Commission paid."},"paylink":{"lead":"Let the client pay online.","body":["When online payments are set up, a saved invoice shows an <b>Online payment</b> section with a secure link and <span class=\"kbd\">Copy link</span>. The client sees the invoice and a Pay button for the current balance, by card or US bank account.","When they pay, the payment is recorded on the invoice automatically as <b>Online (Stripe)</b>, a Draft invoice moves to Sent, and Stripe's processing fee is booked to Merchant / Processing Fees in the ledger so the books match the bank.","For a large commission you want by wire, tick <b>Hide the online pay link on this invoice</b> — the link then refuses payment."],"tip":"Admins connect Stripe in Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Online payments (Stripe)."},"recurring":{"lead":"Bill retainers and monthly fees once.","body":["In the invoice's <b>Recurring</b> section, choose <b>Repeat</b> (Weekly, Every 2 weeks, Monthly, Quarterly, Yearly), set the <b>Next issue date</b>, and leave <b>Series status</b> on Active.","When the date comes due, a new invoice is created as <b>Sent</b> with the same deal, bill-to, lines, tax and terms, and the same gap between issue and due dates. The server checks every few hours and whenever the list loads, so a series keeps running even if nobody opens the page.","The original shows a <b>↻ Monthly</b> (etc.) badge and a count of invoices generated; the generated ones show a small ↻. Set Series status to <b>Paused</b> to stop it."]},"tax":{"lead":"Charge sales tax when a fee needs it.","body":["Under the line items, enter a <b>Tax</b> label (Sales Tax by default) and a rate in %. The tax shows as its own line in the totals and is added to the total and balance.","New invoices start with the firm default from <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Default sales tax</b> (zero unless an admin set one).","In the ledger, tax collected goes to <b>2200 Sales Tax Payable</b>, not income."]},"totals":{"lead":"Know what's out there at a glance.","body":["<b>Billed</b>, <b>Collected</b> and <b>Outstanding</b> sum every invoice in the list except Void ones.","Search by invoice #, bill-to or deal, or open <span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> to narrow by Status, Deal / Listing, Bill to or an Issued date range. The tiles recalculate to match (Billed reads <b>Billed (shown)</b> when filtered).","Admins see a <b>Rep</b> column showing who created each invoice; reps only see their own invoices."]},"books":{"lead":"Invoices are the source for the firm's revenue entries.","body":["A <b>Sent</b> invoice debits Accounts Receivable and credits each line's income account (tax to Sales Tax Payable). Each payment debits Cash and clears A/R.","A <b>Draft</b> invoice doesn't post until you mark it Sent — or record a payment on it, which issues it. <b>Void</b> invoices and their payments drop out.","See the entries in <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a> and the totals in <a href=\"rrg_pnl.html\">Profit & Loss</a>."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Can I email the invoice from here?","a":"Not yet — there's no send button. Mark it <b>Sent</b> and share the online pay link (when Stripe is on), or send it from your own email."},{"q":"Why is my invoice showing Paid when I never changed the status?","a":"Paid and Partial are worked out from the payments. Once payments equal the total, the invoice shows Paid."},{"q":"Does paying an invoice update the deal's commission?","a":"Yes. The deal's commission status follows its invoices — <b>Invoiced</b> when one is sent, <b>Partial</b>, then <b>Paid</b> once collections cover the commission due — and <b>Commission paid</b> on the Deal card follows what's been collected. A figure typed on the Deal card by hand can be raised by invoices but not lowered."}]},"payments":{"intro":"One running list of every payment recorded against every invoice — checks, wires, cards and online payments — with totals that follow your filters.","overview":["<b>Payments</b> (Financials) is the cash-received side of invoicing. You don't enter anything here: payments are recorded on an invoice (by hand or automatically from an online payment) and roll up into this ledger.","Use it to answer \"what came in, from whom, and how\" — for deposits, month-end, or a bookkeeper's question. Click any invoice number to jump to the invoice itself."],"feats":{"ledger":{"lead":"Everything received, in one place.","body":["Each row is one payment: <b>Date</b>, <b>Invoice</b>, <b>Deal / Listing</b>, <b>Bill to</b>, <b>Method</b> and <b>Amount</b>. Admins also see a <b>Rep</b> column for who owns the invoice.","A <b>Reference</b> column (check #, wire confirmation, Stripe payment ID) is hidden by default — turn it on from the list's column menu.","This page is read-only. To add, fix or remove a payment, open its invoice.","A payment on an invoice that was later voided stays in the list, struck through and marked <b>void</b>, and isn't counted in the totals."]},"totals":{"lead":"Quick totals for what's come in.","body":["<b>Total collected</b> is every payment except those on voided invoices. <b>This year</b> counts payments dated in the current calendar year. <b>Payments</b> is the count. When any payments sit on voided invoices, an <b>On voided invoices</b> tile shows their total, marked not counted.","Filter or search and the first tile switches to <b>Collected (shown)</b> so you can total a single deal, payer, method or date range."],"tip":"Filter Method to ACH / Wire and a date range to tie out a bank deposit."},"toinvoice":{"lead":"Go from the payment to the bill behind it.","body":["Click the invoice number in any row. <a href=\"rrg_invoices.html\">Invoices</a> opens with that invoice loaded, showing its lines, balance and full payment history.","To record a new payment, open the invoice and use <span class=\"kbd\">Record payment</span> under <b>Payments</b>."]},"online":{"lead":"No keying in card or ACH payments.","body":["When online payments are turned on and a client pays through an invoice's pay link, the payment is written to the invoice with method <b>Online (Stripe)</b> and the Stripe payment ID as its reference — and appears in this ledger.","The amount shown is the gross the client paid. Stripe's processing fee is booked separately to Merchant / Processing Fees in the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a>."]},"filters":{"lead":"Find a specific payment fast.","body":["The search box matches invoice #, bill-to, listing, method and reference — so typing a check number finds it.","<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> narrows by <b>Method</b>, <b>Deal / Listing</b>, <b>Bill to</b> and a <b>Date</b> range."]},"export":{"lead":"Take the ledger out of the system.","body":["Use <span class=\"kbd\">Export</span> above the list for a CSV of the rows you're looking at, or <span class=\"kbd\">Print</span> for a paper copy.","Both buttons only appear for users with export permission (admins always have it)."]}},"faq":[{"q":"How do I add a payment here?","a":"You don't — open the invoice (click its number) and use <b>Record payment</b>. It appears here right after."},{"q":"Why doesn't the amount match my bank deposit for an online payment?","a":"The ledger shows what the client paid. Stripe deposits that minus its fee, which is booked to Merchant / Processing Fees in the General Ledger."}]},"expenses":{"intro":"Log what the firm spends — marketing, signage, photos, meals, software — tag it to a category and a deal, and track what's unpaid or owed back to a rep.","overview":["<b>Expenses</b> (Financials) is the firm's spend ledger. Each expense has a date, vendor, category, amount, method and status, and can be tied to a listing so the deal's costs show on its file.","Categories drive the reporting: each one maps to an expense account, so the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a> posts each expense to the matching account and <a href=\"rrg_pnl.html\">Profit & Loss</a>, built from the ledger, shows it on that account's line."],"feats":{"log":{"lead":"Add spend as it happens.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New expense</span>. Fill in <b>Date</b>, <b>Vendor / Payee</b>, <b>Category</b>, <b>Amount</b>, <b>Payment method</b> and <b>Status</b>, add <b>Notes</b> on what it was for, and click <span class=\"kbd\">Save expense</span>.","Date and an amount above zero are required. Click a row's date or <span class=\"kbd\">Edit</span> to change it; <span class=\"kbd\">Delete</span> asks to confirm."],"steps":["Click <b>+ New expense</b>","Enter date, vendor, category and amount","Pick a <b>Listing</b> if it's a deal cost","Set <b>Status</b> and tick <b>Reimbursable / billable</b> if it applies","Click <b>Save expense</b>"]},"listing":{"lead":"Know what each listing cost to market.","body":["Pick the deal in <b>Listing (optional)</b>. The expense then appears on that listing file's <b>Deal Expenses</b> card with the deal's total, unpaid and reimbursable amounts.","Filter this page by <b>Listing</b> to see one deal's spend at a glance."]},"status":{"lead":"See what's still owed — by you or to you.","body":["<b>Status</b> is Unpaid, Paid or Reimbursed. Tick <b>Reimbursable / billable</b> for spend that a client or rep should be paid back for; those rows carry a small <b>R</b> flag.","The tiles show <b>Total logged</b>, <b>Unpaid</b> (bills not yet paid) and <b>Reimbursable open</b> (flagged reimbursable but not yet marked Reimbursed)."],"tip":"Filter Status to Unpaid before a check run."},"receipt":{"lead":"Keep the paper trail with the expense.","body":["<b>Receipt link or reference</b> takes a link to the receipt (Drive, Dropbox) or a check # or note. It's a text field — files aren't uploaded here.","The search box also searches this field and the notes."]},"categories":{"lead":"Pick the category once; the reports follow.","body":["Categories: Advertising & Marketing, Meals & Entertainment, Travel & Mileage, Signage, Photography & Media, Dues & Subscriptions, Licenses & Fees, Professional Services, Software & Tools, Office & Supplies, Client Gifts, Other.","In the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a>, each expense debits its category's expense account. An <b>Unpaid</b> expense credits Accounts Payable; a Paid or Reimbursed one credits Cash.","<a href=\"rrg_pnl.html\">Profit & Loss</a> is built from the ledger, so each category's spend shows on its expense account's line."]},"filters":{"lead":"Slice the spend any way you need.","body":["<span class=\"kbd\">⚙ Filters</span> offers <b>Category</b>, <b>Status</b>, <b>Method</b>, <b>Listing</b> and a <b>Date</b> range. The search box covers vendor, category, listing, method, notes and receipt.","When filtered, the first tile reads <b>Logged (shown)</b> and all three tiles reflect only the visible rows."]},"team":{"lead":"One ledger for the firm.","body":["Admins see all expenses and a <b>Logged by</b> column, and can edit or delete any of them.","A rep sees and edits only the expenses they logged."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Can I attach a receipt image?","a":"Not directly — paste a link to the receipt (Drive, Dropbox) or a check # in <b>Receipt link or reference</b>."},{"q":"Can we add our own categories?","a":"The category list is fixed in this version. Use <b>Other</b> and say what it was in <b>Notes</b>."}]},"pnl":{"intro":"A one-page profit and loss for the firm, built from the General Ledger — revenue and operating expenses by account, and net income — for the period you pick.","overview":["<b>Profit & Loss</b> (Financials, admins only) is built from the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a> — issued <a href=\"rrg_invoices.html\">Invoices</a>, <a href=\"rrg_payments.html\">Payments</a>, <a href=\"rrg_expenses.html\">Expenses</a>, Stripe fees and manual journal entries to income or expense accounts — so it ties to the ledger. There's nothing to enter here. Pick a period and the statement rebuilds.","Revenue follows the firm's accounting basis: invoiced (accrual) or collected (cash). An admin sets it in <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Revenue recognition</b>. The basis only changes this report — the ledger itself is always kept on accrual."],"feats":{"statement":{"lead":"The firm's results on one page.","body":["Three tiles up top — <b>Revenue</b>, <b>Expenses</b>, <b>Net income</b> — then the statement: each income account (with its code) under <b>Revenue</b>, <b>Total revenue</b>, each expense account under <b>Operating expenses</b>, <b>Total expenses</b> and <b>Net income</b>.","A loss shows in parentheses and red."]},"period":{"lead":"Switch the reporting window.","body":["Use the dropdown at the top right: <b>This month</b>, <b>This quarter</b>, <b>This year</b> (the default), <b>Last year</b> or <b>All time</b>. The exact date range prints under the title.","Entries count by their ledger date: invoices by issue date, payments by payment date, expenses by their recorded date and journal entries by their own date."]},"basis":{"lead":"Count revenue the way your CPA does.","body":["<b>Accrual</b>: revenue is booked when an invoice is issued (Sent) — Drafts and Voids are left out. <b>Cash</b>: revenue is booked when a payment comes in, spread across that invoice's income accounts. Either way sales tax is left out — it's a liability, not revenue.","The basis shows under the title (e.g. \"accrual (invoiced) basis\"). Change it in <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Revenue recognition</b>.","The basis only changes the P&L. The General Ledger is always kept on accrual, charging A/R when an invoice is sent."]},"ar":{"lead":"Billing and collections next to the bottom line.","body":["The strip above the statement shows <b>Invoiced this period (incl. tax)</b>, <b>Collected this period</b> and <b>Outstanding A/R (all open)</b>.","Outstanding A/R isn't limited to the period — it's the ledger's Accounts Receivable balance across all open invoices, so you see the full amount clients still owe."]},"bycat":{"lead":"Where the money came from and where it went.","body":["Each account used in the period gets its own line, in account-code order. An expense lands in the account for its category; Stripe processing fees and manual journal entries to income or expense accounts show up on their accounts too.","Categories come from each expense in <a href=\"rrg_expenses.html\">Expenses</a>; the accounts themselves live on the <a href=\"rrg_gl.html\">General Ledger</a>'s Chart of Accounts."]},"print":{"lead":"Paper or PDF in one click.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">Print</span> next to the period picker. Use your browser's Save as PDF to email it."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Does the P&L match the General Ledger?","a":"Yes — it's built from the ledger, so its income and expense lines tie to the ledger's accounts. The one difference is on cash basis: the P&L counts invoice revenue when it's collected, while the ledger stays on accrual."},{"q":"Where do I switch to cash basis?","a":"<b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing → Revenue recognition</b> (admins)."}]},"gl":{"intro":"The firm's double-entry books: entries post themselves from invoices, payments and expenses, and you add manual journal entries on top.","overview":["<b>General Ledger</b> (Financials, admins only) keeps simple double-entry books. Invoices, invoice payments and expenses post themselves — you never re-key them. Manual journal entries cover everything else: owner draws, loans, bank fees, adjustments.","Four tabs: <b>Trial Balance</b>, <b>Journal</b>, <b>Ledger</b> and <b>Chart of Accounts</b>. Income accounts you add here show up as choices on invoice lines; invoice defaults live in <b>Settings → Accounting & Invoicing</b>."],"feats":{"autopost":{"lead":"No double entry between invoicing and the books.","body":["A <b>Sent</b> invoice debits <b>1100 Accounts Receivable</b> and credits each line's income account (4000 Commission Income if none), with any sales tax to <b>2200 Sales Tax Payable</b>.","A payment debits <b>1000 Cash</b> and credits A/R. For online payments, the Stripe fee is split out to <b>5900 Merchant / Processing Fees</b> so Cash matches the deposit.","An expense debits its category's expense account and credits <b>2000 Accounts Payable</b> if Unpaid, or Cash if Paid/Reimbursed.","A <b>Draft</b> invoice doesn't post until it's sent — recording a payment on it issues it, so it posts then. <b>Void</b> invoices and their payments drop out. Auto entries are read-only here — fix them at the invoice or expense."]},"trial":{"lead":"The books on one screen.","body":["The <b>Trial Balance</b> tab lists each account with activity — code, name, type, <b>Debit</b>, <b>Credit</b> and <b>Balance</b> — and totals at the bottom. The header shows <b>In balance ✓</b> or <b>Out of balance</b>.","<b>Roll up sub-accounts</b> combines each sub-account into its top-level parent for a summary; turn it off to see every account indented under its parent. The page remembers your choice."]},"journal":{"lead":"See exactly what posted and why.","body":["The <b>Journal</b> tab lists all entries with date, a source badge and the memo (e.g. \"Invoice INV-1004 — Smith Holdings\"), then each line.","Manual entries carry <span class=\"kbd\">Edit</span> and <span class=\"kbd\">Delete</span>; auto-posted ones don't."]},"manual":{"lead":"Book draws, loans, transfers and adjustments.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">＋ New journal entry</span>, set <b>Date</b> and <b>Memo</b>, and add at least two lines, each an account with a debit <i>or</i> a credit.","The chip at the bottom shows running <b>Debits</b> and <b>Credits</b> and turns green when they match; otherwise it shows how much you're <b>Off by</b>. Out-of-balance entries are refused."],"steps":["Click <b>＋ New journal entry</b>","Enter <b>Date</b> and <b>Memo</b>","Pick an account and amount on each line (<b>＋ Add line</b> for more)","Check the chip is green","Click <b>Save entry</b>"]},"ledger":{"lead":"Drill into one account.","body":["On the <b>Ledger</b> tab, choose an account. Each posting shows date, source, memo, debit, credit and the running <b>Balance</b>, ending with the <b>Ending balance</b>.","With <b>Roll up sub-accounts</b> on, a parent account's ledger includes its sub-accounts (each line names the sub-account)."],"tip":"Pick 1100 Accounts Receivable to see who's billed and who's paid."},"coa":{"lead":"Shape the accounts to how your CPA wants them.","body":["On <b>Chart of Accounts</b>, enter <b>Code</b>, <b>Account name</b>, <b>Type</b> (Asset, Liability, Equity, Income, Expense) and optionally <b>Sub-account of</b>, then <span class=\"kbd\">Add account</span>. <b>Edit</b> loads an account into the form to change it.","Parents get a caret to collapse or expand, plus <b>Collapse all</b> / <b>Expand all</b>.","1000, 1100, 2000, 3000 and 4000 are core accounts and can't be removed; an account used by a manual entry can't be removed until those entries are moved."]},"qbimport":{"lead":"Bring your existing chart over instead of retyping it.","body":["On <b>Chart of Accounts</b>, click <span class=\"kbd\">⇪ Import from QuickBooks</span>. Choose the CSV or paste it; confirm which columns hold the account name, type and (optional) account number.","QuickBooks types (Bank, Accounts receivable, Credit Card, Cost of Goods Sold …) map to ours. The preview marks each row <b>New</b>, <b>Already exists</b> or <b>Skip</b> before you click <span class=\"kbd\">Import</span>, and its counts are exactly what the import will do.","\"Parent:Child\" names become nested sub-accounts, and blank or clashing numbers get a free code in the right range. Tick <b>Update the type of accounts that already exist</b> to correct types on a re-import."]},"csv":{"lead":"Hand the CPA a file.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⭳ Export CSV</span> at the top. You get the current tab: Trial Balance, the full Journal (one row per line), the selected account's Ledger with running balance, or the Chart of Accounts with parents.","On the Ledger tab, pick an account first."]},"danger":{"lead":"Start the ledger over — carefully.","body":["At the bottom of <b>Chart of Accounts</b>: <span class=\"kbd\">Delete all journal entries</span> removes manual entries; <span class=\"kbd\">Reset accounts to default</span> restores the starter chart; <span class=\"kbd\">Delete everything in the ledger</span> removes every manual entry and every account except the system accounts the auto-posting uses — cash, A/R, A/P, sales tax, equity, commission income, processing fees and one account per expense category.","None of these can be undone. Invoices, payments and expenses aren't touched and keep posting.","Because Delete everything keeps the system accounts, invoices, payments and expenses still post and the Trial Balance stays whole — re-import or re-add any other accounts you need."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why isn't my invoice in the ledger?","a":"Only issued invoices post — <b>Sent</b>, or a Draft that has a payment recorded on it (the payment issues it). Open it in Invoices and set Status to Sent."},{"q":"Can I edit an auto-posted entry?","a":"No — change the invoice, payment or expense it came from and the entry updates. Use a manual journal entry for an adjustment."},{"q":"Does the cash/accrual setting change the ledger?","a":"No. The ledger always books receivables when an invoice is sent. The basis setting only affects Profit & Loss, which is built from the ledger."}]},"market_radar":{"intro":"Alcohol Sales reads the Texas Comptroller's monthly mixed-beverage filings and TABC permit records for a whole city, so you can see who's growing, who's sliding and who has stopped filing.","overview":["Alcohol Sales lives under Tools. Pick a city, county or metro area and every operator filing mixed-beverage taxes there loads with 24 months of sales, trailing-12 totals, change versus last year and the liquor/wine/beer mix. Falling sales and stopped filings are the earliest signs of a second-gen space coming open or an owner who may want to sell; fast growers are expansion-minded tenant prospects.","Click any operator for its full operator page — monthly and yearly history, the permit holder and phone, other locations under the same owner, city and zip rankings, and an AI broker read. Tick up to five to compare side by side. Area data is cached for a day, and the state reports with a one-to-two-month lag, so treat every signal as a lead to verify."],"feats":{"city":{"lead":"A whole city, county or metro’s bar and restaurant alcohol sales in one table.","body":["Click <b>Area</b> and start typing. The list holds every Texas city with mixed-beverage filers (with how many operators filed in the last four months), every county, and four metro areas — <b>Greater San Antonio</b>, <b>Greater Austin</b>, <b>Dallas–Fort Worth</b> and <b>Greater Houston</b>, each showing the counties it covers. Arrow keys and Enter pick; every pick returns data. Each row shows the operator, address, owner and permit, a 24-month sparkline, average and latest month, trailing-12 sales with the mix bar, liquor/wine/beer dollars, the change for your chosen window, status and last month filed. <b>Look up</b> searches the operator on Google.","A city covers operators whose state filing address is in that city — suburbs like Alamo Heights or Live Oak are their own cities. Pick a county or metro to take in every city at once; each row then shows its city, and <b>Track space</b> files the space under that city. The first load of a metro can take a few minutes; after that it’s cached for a day like a city.","The top row totals what's in view: matching operators, combined trailing-12 sales, how many stopped filing, and how many are down 25%+.","A city is cached for a day. <b>Refresh from the state</b> pulls fresh filings — a big city can take a minute."]},"views":{"lead":"The lead lists brokers ask for most, pre-built.","body":["<b>Stopped filing</b> — operators that went quiet after averaging $15K+ a month. <b>Sales down 25%+</b> — still filing, $12.5K+ a month, last three months down at least 25% from the same months a year earlier (the view the page opens on). <b>Growing 25%+</b> — the same, going up. <b>New operators</b> — filing four months or fewer.","<b>Pending permits</b> lists new TABC applications in the city — new concepts entering the market, competition for your listings, and tenants who may still need a space or a broker. <b>Credit-law delinquent</b> lists operators behind paying their alcohol distributors — a strong distress sign.","Each chip shows how many operators it would return before you click. Any filter change takes you off the preset."]},"filters":{"lead":"Cut a city down to the exact list you want to call.","body":["Filter by <b>Name (business)</b>, <b>Owner / taxpayer</b> and <b>Street</b>; <b>Status</b> (filing, stopped, new); <b>Sales change vs same period last year</b> — down or up at least X% over 3, 6 or 12 months; <b>Monthly sales ($)</b> on average, latest month or trailing-12; <b>Months since last filing</b>; <b>Avg / month before</b>; <b>Zip codes</b>; <b>Liquor share of sales</b> (bars run high, restaurants low); and <b>Filing history</b> of 12 or 24+ months.","A stopped operator is judged on what it did before it stopped, so a sales minimum still finds the big ones that went dark. Click any column header to sort; <b>Reset filters</b> clears everything.","Every filter is written into the page address — bookmark it or send the link and it reopens the same list."],"tip":"Try Stopped filing + a zip code + liquor share 60%+ to find bar spaces likely coming open in a submarket."},"chains":{"lead":"See a group's total footprint, not 14 separate rows.","body":["Under <b>Franchises & chains</b>, tick <b>Consolidate into one row</b> and choose <b>by brand name</b> or <b>by owner</b>. Brand matching ignores store numbers and location tags (\"#23\", \"- La Cantera\", \"(Stone Oak)\") and LLC/Inc; owner matching uses the state taxpayer number.","A group row shows the location count, the owner (or how many different owners — a franchise), combined 24-month trend, sales, mix and change, and a status such as <b>3 filing · 1 stopped</b>. Click the row to expand every location under it.","The who/where filters pick locations first; the sales and change filters then judge the group's combined numbers."]},"operator":{"lead":"Everything the state knows about one location, on one page.","body":["The top cards show the recent month, past quarter, 6 months, year and 2 years, each against the same window a year earlier (the 2-year card against the two years before) — only when that earlier window was fully filed. Below: a stacked 24-month chart (liquor, wine, beer, cover charges), monthly data with <b>% vs last year</b> for each month, and yearly data with partial years compared on the same months (marked YTD). Both tables export to CSV.","On the right: <b>Revenue mix</b> for the last 12 months, every TABC permit at the street address, <b>Rankings</b> in the city and the zip for total, liquor, wine and beer, and <b>All N locations</b> filed under the same owner.","<b>Map ↗</b> and <b>Look up ↗</b> open Google. A red tag warns when the operator hasn't filed in four months or more."]},"holder":{"lead":"Who to call about this location.","body":["The <b>Permit holder & contact</b> card on the operator page shows the permit holder and dba, the owner of record from tax filings, permit type, status, issue and expiry dates, phone, mailing address, taxpayer ID and how long they've been filing. Permit and phone come from TABC; owner and mailing address from the Comptroller. The state rarely publishes email.","<b>In your CRM</b> shows any companies or contacts already on file that match the owner or trade name, or the permit phone. If there are none, <b>＋ Add to CRM</b> creates the company with the location, permit, phone, mailing address and trailing-12 sales in its notes, lead source <b>Alcohol Sales</b>. If a company with that name already exists, it links to that one instead.","<b>Copy</b> puts the whole contact block on your clipboard."]},"ai":{"lead":"A broker's take on the numbers, in under 300 words.","body":["On an operator page, click <b>✨ AI summary</b>. In about 10–20 seconds you get <b>Bottom line</b>, <b>Sales trend</b>, <b>Mix & concept</b>, <b>Permit & ownership</b>, <b>Signals for RRG</b> (possible seller, expansion-minded group, space likely coming available) and <b>Next move</b>.","The summary is saved on the server for that permit, so the next person sees it instantly with who made it and when. When newer filings arrive it's marked <b>Newer filings since — regenerate</b>; <b>↻ Regenerate</b> rewrites it and <b>Copy</b> grabs the text.","It's drawn from state filings only — verify before it goes to a client."]},"compare":{"lead":"Comp a location against its neighbors or a client's own store.","body":["Tick the box beside up to five operators; a bar appears at the bottom (<b>N of 5 to compare</b>). Click <b>⇄ Compare</b>. On an operator page, <b>⇄ Compare</b> adds that location and opens the comparison.","On the compare page, add more with <b>Add a location</b> — search by name, owner or street, optionally with a city; arrow keys and Enter pick a result. Hover the chart to see every location's sales for that month.","The comparison is defined by its web address: <b>Copy link</b> to share it. <b>Clear</b> starts over."]},"pdf":{"lead":"A clean comp report for a seller, buyer or landlord.","body":["With two or more locations on the compare page, click <b>⬇ PDF report</b>. Add a <b>Title</b> (it suggests one from the names), <b>Prepared for</b> and <b>Broker notes</b>, then <b>Download PDF</b>.","The report carries your logo and firm details, the title and date, your notes, then the full comparison and chart."]},"track":{"lead":"From signal to a space you're working, in one click.","body":["Click <b>Track space</b> on a row (also on credit-law rows) or <b>Track as space</b> on an operator page. It creates a space in <a href=\"rrg_space_tracker.html\">Spaces</a> at that address — status <b>Off-market</b>, condition Second-gen, tagged 2nd-gen restaurant — with the signal (stopped filing, sales change, trailing-12) and the operator and permit in its notes.","If that address is already tracked, you get an <b>Open space</b> link to the existing space instead of a duplicate. The space page shows a <b>From Alcohol Sales</b> tag and runs the address through tenant matching like any other space."]},"export":{"lead":"Take the list into a spreadsheet or a mail merge.","body":["<b>⬇ Export CSV</b> saves exactly what's in view: operator, locations, owner, address, zip, permit, status, months since last filing, average and latest month, trailing-12, liquor/wine/beer dollars and shares, prior-12, 3/6/12-month change and the pre-stop average.","With chains consolidated, each group is one row with its location count, owners and every address. On the Pending permits or Credit-law views, it exports that list instead."]},"source":{"lead":"Where the alcohol-sales data comes from.","body":["In Settings → Integrations, <b>State alcohol-sales data</b> holds the <b>Source URL</b> (defaults to data.texas.gov, no key needed), a <b>State / label</b>, and the dataset IDs for mixed-beverage receipts, liquor licenses, pending applications and the credit-law list. Another state's portal can be used if it publishes the same kind of datasets.","<b>Test connection</b> checks each dataset; <b>Reset to Texas defaults</b> puts it back. Saving clears the cached city data so the next load pulls from the new source. Untick <b>Enabled</b> to turn the feature off — Alcohol Sales, operator pages and each space's alcohol records then show a message instead."]},"strong":{"lead":"The page opens on <b>Strong performers</b> — the healthy independents that sell as going concerns at full value.","body":["It shows independents filing for 24+ months, averaging $40,000+ a month in alcohol sales, with sales flat or up over the last 12 months versus the year before — rolled up by brand (multi-unit groups as one row) and ranked by <b>Sellability</b>.","The declining views (Sales down 25%+, Stopped filing) are still there — they point to asset sales and second-generation space for tenant rep, not to full-value business sales."],"tip":"Pair it with Consolidate (by brand) to see multi-unit independents as one row."},"sellability":{"lead":"The <b>Sellability</b> column ranks how likely an operator is a healthy business that sells as a going concern at full value — hover a score for its breakdown.","body":["<b>Size</b> 35 (average monthly sales, $20K = 0 up to $300K+ = 35) · <b>Growth</b> 25 (12-month change: −15% or worse = 0, flat = 12, +20% or better = 25) · <b>Consistency</b> 15 (steady months score high; a 3-month drop of 15%+ costs 5) · <b>Track record</b> 10 (months filing, of the 24 shown) · <b>Multi-unit</b> 10 (2 locations = 5, 3+ = 10 — a location of an independent brand counts its brand's other locations) · <b>Independent</b> 5.","<b>70+ = Prime seller</b>, 55–69 = Strong. Stopped operators score 0 — not a going concern. Sort by the column, export it in the CSV, and imports tag companies <b>Prime seller</b> or <b>Strong seller</b> with the score in their notes."],"tip":"The score reads the state filings only. Add what they can't see — lease expiration, owner tenure, partner changes — before you call."},"platforms":{"lead":"<b>Platforms (3+ units)</b> finds the next Cane Rosso: independent, founder-owned groups with three or more locations filing.","body":["A brand counts as <b>independent</b> when one owner — or LLCs that carry the brand's name, like \"Cane Rosso White Rock LLC\" — hold most of its locations. When three or more unrelated owners hold the same brand it is marked <b>Franchise</b>. Locations named \"Brand + neighborhood\" are rolled into one brand when their owner LLCs share that name.","Each group shows its combined trailing-12 sales, change vs last year and a <b>＋ permit pending</b> tag when a TABC application is pending under the same brand — usually a new store opening. The <b>Ownership</b> filter (Independents only, Platforms, Chains & franchises) works on any view."],"tip":"Sort by Trailing 12 for scale, by Change for momentum. A national chain with only one or two locations in the area can read as independent — the owner name gives it away."},"crmimport":{"lead":"<span class=\"kbd\">＋ Import to CRM</span> (admins) adds the operators in your current view as companies.","body":["Set a minimum average monthly alcohol sales (default $40,000; groups judged on combined sales), choose whether to leave out chains and franchises, and pick the owner — you as the house account, or a rep. <b>Check against CRM</b> shows how many are new and which are already there before anything is written.","Each company is tagged <b>Alcohol Sales</b> plus its signals — Independent, Platform target, Multi-unit independent, Sales declining or growing, Stopped filing, New permit pending, Multi-market — with the owners of record, locations, permits and sales in its notes. Anything already in the CRM (same name or owner name, or one of its permits) is skipped, never overwritten. Nobody is added to an email list. Up to 2,000 per import."],"tip":"Import a focused view — e.g. Platforms, or Independents down 15%+ — rather than a whole metro, so reps get a list worth working."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why does a busy place show as \"Stopped\"?","a":"Filings lag one to two months and some operators file late, so a late filer can look stopped for a month. Check the last month filed and verify before you act."},{"q":"Does this include food sales?","a":"No. Mixed-beverage receipts are alcohol sold for on-premise consumption only."},{"q":"Can I share a filtered list with a teammate?","a":"Yes — the filters live in the page address. Copy the URL from your browser and send it."},{"q":"Does the page remember which rows I've tracked?","a":"Yes. A row whose address is already a space shows <b>Open space</b> instead of <b>Track space</b>, even after you reload — and tracking an address that's already in Spaces never adds a duplicate."}]},"ssc_form":{"intro":"Site Criteria (the SSC) is the blueprint for every tenant search: the concept, target markets, space needs, licensing and zoning, economics and timeline, captured once with the client before you look at a single space.","overview":["Open it from a contact with Start Site Criteria (SSC) so it's tied to that client, or from Open Site Criteria on a tenant engagement's Needs Analysis stage. The RRG standard is printed at the top: no market search begins without a completed SSC.","Seven sections walk through the operator and concept, market and trade area, space requirements, licensing and zoning, economics, timeline and contacts. Submit & Email checks the required fields, emails a branded PDF to the tenant rep and files the SSC so the rest of the platform — Site Fit, Spaces matching and the contact's Tenant Box — can use it."],"feats":{"blueprint":{"lead":"The SSC walks you and the client through everything that decides where a concept can go.","body":["Sections: <b>1 Operator & Concept Profile</b> (years, locations, expansion objective, search discretion, concept type, alcohol, dayparts, positioning); <b>2 Market & Trade Area</b> (primary markets, submarkets, income, traffic drivers, lifestyle segments, synergistic brands); <b>3 Space Requirements</b> (ideal / min / max SF, BOH %, ceiling, hood, space type, kitchen and cold storage, bar, patio, walk-up, drive-thru, parking); <b>4 Licensing, Zoning & Use</b> (TABC, 300-ft distance rules, zoning, live music, hours); <b>5 Economics & Capital Reality</b> (projected sales, target occupancy %, max and viable rent, lease or buy, term, build-out budget, funding, TI and abated rent); <b>6 Timeline & Delivery</b>; and <b>Other Information</b>.","Each choice group says <i>Select one</i> or <i>Select all that apply</i>; select-one groups clear the other boxes for you. Number fields format themselves as you type. The page header updates with the concept name and target markets as you fill them in."],"tip":"Load sample fills a complete example (a country dance hall search) so you can see what a finished SSC looks like."},"contact":{"lead":"Starting from the contact ties the SSC to the client's record.","body":["On a contact, use <b>Start Site Criteria (SSC)</b>. The form opens with <b>Restaurant / Concept Name</b> and <b>Primary Contact</b> filled from the record, and the finished SSC carries the contact and company link.","When you submit, a note — \"Site Criteria (SSC) sent\" with the concept — is added to the contact's activity, and the SSC appears in that contact's documents as <b>Site Criteria</b>."]},"send":{"lead":"Submit & Email is how a finished SSC leaves the page.","body":["Enter the address under <b>Tenant Rep Email — SSC copy sent here</b> and click <b>Submit & Email</b>. Missing required fields turn red, a banner counts them, and the page scrolls to the first one.","When everything's in, the server renders a branded PDF and emails it to that address, copying the firm's email from <b>Brokerage Info</b> (or the firm's sending address if none is set). The SSC is filed on the server — with its primary markets — even if the email fails.","If sending fails (for example, server email isn't set up), you get <b>Copy email text</b> — a ready-to-paste plain-text version with <b>Open in email app</b>."],"steps":["Fill the required (*) fields.","Enter the tenant rep email at the bottom.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Submit & Email</span>."]},"print":{"lead":"Two print options cover the meeting and the file.","body":["<b>Print</b> checks the required fields, then prints a branded version: a cover with the concept, target market, footprint and lease-or-buy snapshot, then only the sections and answers you filled in. Choose \"Save as PDF\" in the print dialog for a file.","<b>Print blank copy</b> prints the empty form without checking anything, then puts your on-screen entries back."]},"draft":{"lead":"The form autosaves to your browser so a half-done SSC isn't lost.","body":["A <b>Draft saved ✓</b> chip flashes in the corner as you type. Reopen the form and a red bar says it restored your draft, with <b>Start fresh</b> to wipe it or <b>Keep editing</b>.","The draft lives in this browser only. <b>Clear form</b> empties the fields and the saved draft."]},"reuse":{"lead":"Once submitted, the SSC is reused across the tenant-rep tools.","body":["<b>Site Fit</b> → <b>Fill from SSC / PDF</b> lists every SSC on file and fills the concept section from it. On <b>Spaces</b>, <b>Match spaces to a client</b> ranks your tracked spaces against an SSC with AI. On the contact, <b>Fill from SSC</b> reads their latest SSC and proposes a Tenant Box for you to review.","Each filed SSC can be opened as a read-only view from the contact's documents. With permissions on, a rep without see-all access sees only the SSCs they saved or are named on as the RRG Advisor."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Does the client fill this out?","a":"No. The advisor completes it with the client, on a call or in a meeting. The client gets the PDF if you choose to send it on."},{"q":"I submitted but the email didn't arrive.","a":"The SSC is still on file. Use Copy email text in the error box to send it yourself, and ask an admin to check Settings → Email / SMTP."},{"q":"Who gets the PDF?","a":"The address you type under Tenant Rep Email, with the firm's Brokerage Info email copied (or the sending address if none is set). The confirmation tells you who was copied."}]},"lease_queue":{"intro":"Lease Abstracts turns a restaurant lease into a one-look summary of the terms a buyer cares about. Upload the lease and the AI analyst pulls the terms, or fill one in by hand.","overview":["Lease Abstracts lives under Tools in the left sidebar. The list shows every abstract you own (admins see the whole firm's), with months left on the term, option months, the expiration date and whether it's been built yet. Click a name to open it — finished ones open the abstract, unbuilt ones open the build screen.","A finished abstract is filed as a PDF on the linked contact's Documents and dropped into the deal's data room under Lease. It also feeds the Marketing Pack's occupancy and lease summary and the valuation (BOV), so the lease terms are typed once."],"feats":{"ai-read":{"lead":"The build screen sends the lease to an AI analyst that extracts the key terms into a structured abstract.","body":["Click <b>+ New abstract</b>, add the lease under <b>Lease document</b> (PDF works best; images are fine), then click <b>Build the abstract →</b>. A progress screen shows the elapsed time; when it finishes the abstract opens on its own.","The analyst is told to extract only what the documents say — exact figures and dates — and to write \"Not specified in the provided documents\" rather than guess. It fills parties, premises, term, base rent and the rent schedule, options, operating charges, deposit, use, assignment, guaranty, key provisions, critical dates, a plain-English summary and a list of watch items.","Send the lease and its amendments, not the full exhibit set. Uploads over about 44 MB are refused before sending; a cloud-only file (OneDrive, iCloud, Google Drive) that hasn't downloaded is flagged so you can open it once and re-add it."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New abstract</span>.","Optionally name it and link a listing and a contact under <b>Details</b>.","Click <b>+ Click to add the lease</b> and pick the file(s).","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Build the abstract →</span>."],"tip":"You can click Cancel build on the progress screen if you sent the wrong file."},"room-lease":{"lead":"If the linked listing's data room already has a lease in it, the build screen offers to use that copy.","body":["After you pick a <b>Linked listing</b>, the build screen checks that deal's data room for anything filed under Lease or named like a lease, amendment, sublease or estoppel. If it finds one, a green note reads <b>Using the lease already in the data room</b> with the file names, and <b>Build the abstract →</b> works with no upload.","Drop your own file in and it takes priority — the note dims and says your upload will be used instead."]},"manual":{"lead":"Every abstract can be built by hand instead of from a document.","body":["On the build screen click <b>or fill it in manually instead →</b>. The full abstract opens blank with editing on; type into any field and it saves itself. Once saved it shows as <b>Abstracted</b> in the list.","Unbuilt abstracts show a <b>Not built</b> pill and a <b>Build →</b> button in the list so you can come back to them."]},"edit":{"lead":"The abstract is a live document — every field is editable right on the page.","body":["With <b>Editing</b> switched on in the toolbar, click any value and type. The status chip reads \"Unsaved changes…\" and then \"Saved ✓\" with the time a moment after you stop; <b>Save</b> writes immediately. Turn <b>Editing</b> off for a clean read-only view.","Use <b>+ Add watch item</b>, <b>+ Add rent period</b> and <b>+ Add critical date</b> to add lines. <b>← Back</b> saves any pending edit, then returns you to the linked contact (or company, listing or valuation)."],"tip":"Read the AI's work against the lease before it goes to a buyer — it never replaces the executed lease."},"watch":{"lead":"The abstract opens with a summary and a Watch Items list of anything that helps or hurts a sale.","body":["The analyst writes a short <b>Summary</b> focused on transferability and remaining runway, then lists <b>Watch Items</b> — one line each, like \"Only 2 years remain with no renewal options\" or \"Landlord recapture right on assignment\".","<b>Critical Dates</b> at the bottom lists dated events such as the renewal-notice deadline. Both lists are editable."]},"occupancy":{"lead":"The Occupancy & Economics section turns the lease into the number a buyer's lender looks at first.","body":["The analyst fills annual base rent, NNN / CAM + taxes + insurance and total occupancy cost where the lease states them. Type the business's <b>Annual sales (from P&L / BOV)</b> and <b>Occupancy cost % of sales</b> calculates live: 10% or less reads healthy, up to 15% reads \"watch — tight for the buyer\", above that \"high — pressures the deal\".","If total occupancy is blank, base rent plus NNN is used. The section also has fields for key money / lease value and financeability."]},"redact":{"lead":"Redaction swaps the landlord and tenant names for \"Withheld — available under NDA\".","body":["Pick it on the build screen under <b>Redaction</b> (<b>Redact landlord info</b> and <b>Redact tenant info</b>, both checked by default), or flip <b>Redact Landlord</b> / <b>Redact Tenant</b> in the abstract's toolbar any time. The guarantor follows the tenant setting.","The same settings carry into the Marketing Pack's lease summary, so a buyer-facing copy doesn't leak names."]},"filing":{"lead":"Every saved abstract is snapshotted to a PDF and filed where the deal lives.","body":["Use <b>Linked listing</b> and <b>Linked contact</b> on the build screen to tie the abstract to a deal. Each time a finished abstract is saved, a PDF copy is regenerated; it shows on the contact's Documents as <b>Lease Abstract (PDF)</b> next to the live one, and is dropped into the deal's data room under Lease if a room exists.","If the room is created later, the abstract is added when the room is next opened. <b>Print</b> on the abstract gives you a clean copy any time."]},"feeds":{"lead":"An abstract tied to a deal is reused by the deal's other documents.","body":["A Marketing Pack builds its buyer-safe <b>Occupancy & Lease Summary</b> from the deal's abstract, honoring the redaction switches. Creating a Marketing Pack also starts a blank abstract for that deal if none exists.","The valuation (BOV) reads the abstract for remaining term and lease risk. When an abstract was started from a valuation, a green <b>Value the business now →</b> button on the abstract takes you straight back to finish it, and a lease dropped on the valuation screen carries over to the build screen."]},"list":{"lead":"The Lease Abstracts list sorts your book of leases by runway.","body":["The <b>Term</b> column shows months left on the lease and option months, with the expiration date underneath; sort it to see the shortest runways first. Option months are the number of options times each option's term — <i>Two (2) x 5 years</i> counts as 120. <b>Status</b> shows <b>Not built</b> or <b>Abstracted</b>, and <b>Date</b> shows when it was started or abstracted.","Tick rows and use <b>Delete selected</b> to remove several at once, or the trash icon on a single row. Reps see their own abstracts; admins see everyone's."]}},"faq":[{"q":"The build failed or took too long. What now?","a":"Send only the lease and its amendments, not the exhibit set, and use a digital PDF rather than a scan if you have one. You can also fill it in manually."},{"q":"Who can see my abstracts?","a":"You see the ones you created; admins see the whole firm's."},{"q":"Is the AI's abstract good enough to hand a buyer?","a":"Read it against the lease first. The analyst is told never to invent terms, but it's a summary, and the footer says it doesn't replace the executed lease."}]},"site_fit":{"intro":"Site Fit is a one-sheet analysis of whether a specific space works for a specific restaurant or bar concept. It runs the occupancy-cost math, scores ten weighted fit factors and makes the call.","overview":["Open it from Tenant Rep in the left sidebar. It opens on the list of every analysis you can see; click one to open it, or <b>+ New Analysis</b> to start one. Faster still: click <b>Fit</b> on a space in a tenant’s shortlist, or <b>✚ Run Fit</b> on a space, and the facts you already have fill in.","The sheet reads top to bottom: The Concept (who you’re placing), The Location (the space and trade area), The Money Test (occupancy cost against projected sales), The Scorecard, and The Call. The strip at the top keeps Overall Fit, Verdict, Occupancy Cost and Est. Annual Sales in view as you work.","It saves to the server as you type, linked to the tenant engagement and the space — the call shows on both. Print gives the branded sheet for the client."],"feats":{"start":{"lead":"The fastest way in is from the tenant or the space — the facts you already have fill the sheet.","body":["On a tenant engagement, every space in the <b>Space shortlist</b> — and every toured space picked from the Space Tracker — has a <b>Fit</b> button. It opens the analysis already run for that tenant on that space, or starts a new one with both linked. In the shortlist, a space that’s been scored shows its score on the button — <b>Fit 82</b>.","On a space page, <b>✚ Run Fit</b> starts an analysis on that space, and each tenant under <b>Tenant fits</b> has its own <b>Fit</b> button.","From the tenant’s Site Selection Criteria the sheet takes the concept name, <b>Prepared For</b>, the F&amp;B type when the use makes it clear, <b>Space Needed</b> and the must-haves. From the space it takes the address, positioning line, suite, <b>Available SF</b>, <b>Base Rent</b>, <b>NNN / CAM</b>, parking, and the center’s traffic count, anchor and co-tenants. Check every filled field — the scorecard ratings are always yours to make."],"tip":"Pick a <b>Tenant</b> or <b>Space</b> in the bar under the toolbar on any sheet and the blank fields fill the same way — nothing you typed is overwritten."},"list":{"lead":"Site Fit opens on the list of every analysis you can see.","body":["Each row shows the concept and address, the linked <b>Tenant</b> and <b>Space</b>, the <b>Fit</b> score, the <b>Call</b> (Strong Fit, Workable, Marginal or Pass), <b>Occupancy</b>, who made it and when it was last changed. Click a column header to sort; the search box looks through concept, tenant, space, address and the call.","Click a concept to open the sheet, or <b>+ New Analysis</b> to start a blank one."]},"moneytest":{"lead":"The Money Test answers the question that kills most restaurant deals: can the concept carry this rent?","body":["Enter <b>Average Check</b>, <b>Est. Guests / Day</b> and <b>Days Open / Year</b> (defaults to 358) under The Concept, and <b>Available SF</b>, <b>Base Rent</b> and <b>NNN / CAM</b> under The Location. The sheet computes <b>Projected Annual Sales</b>, <b>Total Annual Occupancy</b> and the <b>Occupancy Cost Ratio</b> — green under 10%, amber under 12%, red above.","The note underneath reads it out in plain English. When occupancy runs rich, it tells you the sales volume — and guests per day at your check — the space needs to hold occupancy at 10%.","Write your own read in <b>Broker read on the economics</b>: is the rent defensible, what's the break-even."],"tip":"Use realistic mature-volume guests per day, not opening-month numbers — the whole score leans on this."},"scorecard":{"lead":"The Scorecard turns your site read into a 0–100 weighted fit score.","body":["Rate each factor from 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent) with the buttons on the right. Occupancy-cost economics and trade-area demographics carry triple weight; daypart match, co-tenancy, visibility, access and parking, competition and buildout carry double; customer segment and deal flexibility count single.","<b>Occupancy-Cost Economics</b> is scored for you from the Money Test: under 8% scores 5, under 10% scores 4, under 12% scores 3, under 15% scores 2, otherwise 1.","Unrated factors count as zero against the total, so rate every factor before you read the score."]},"verdict":{"lead":"The Call turns the score into an RRG recommendation.","body":["78 and up reads <b>Strong Fit</b> (pursue), 62–77 <b>Workable</b> with the right terms, 45–61 <b>Marginal</b> (only with real concessions), and below 45 <b>Pass</b>.","Economics can override the score: at 15% occupancy or worse the call becomes <b>Pass on economics</b>, and at 12% or worse a Strong Fit is knocked down to Workable until the rent is fixed. The verdict pill also shows in the summary strip at the top."]},"narrative":{"lead":"Under the verdict, three boxes hold the broker's argument.","body":["<b>Why this site works</b> and <b>Risks & what to negotiate</b> take one point per line. <b>Next steps</b> is for the LOI number, TI ask, exclusive use, contingencies and what to verify before committing.","Boxes grow as you type and print at full length."]},"print":{"lead":"The whole sheet prints as a branded document.","body":["Fill the cover fields — <b>Concept / Client Name</b>, candidate address, positioning line, <b>Prepared For</b>, <b>Prepared By</b> and <b>Date</b> (today's date fills in on its own). Then click <b>Print</b> and choose \"Save as PDF\" for a file.","<b>Prepared By</b> starts filled with your own name and contact line from your profile — check it before you print."]},"draft":{"lead":"Every analysis is saved on the server as you type.","body":["The chip in the toolbar reads <b>Saving…</b> then <b>Saved ✓</b>. A new sheet isn’t saved until you type something or link a tenant or space, so opening one just to look doesn’t leave an empty record behind. If a save fails the chip turns red — click it to try again.","The <b>Tenant</b> and <b>Space</b> pickers link the analysis. A linked analysis shows on the tenant engagement’s <b>Site Fit</b> card (score, call, occupancy) and in <b>Site Fit analyses</b> on the space page; <b>Open tenant ↗</b> and <b>Open space ↗</b> jump to them.","You see your own analyses and any linked to tenant engagements you own; admins and anyone with <b>See all records</b> see every one. Only the maker, the tenant’s owner and admins can change one — everyone else gets it view-only. <b>Delete</b> needs the delete permission (admins always can)."],"tip":"Analyses made before this update were kept only in the browser they were made in. Open Site Fit in that browser and click <b>Open &amp; save</b> on the amber banner to move it to the server."},"prefill":{"lead":"Fill from SSC / PDF isn't available in this version yet.","body":["The <b>↧ Fill from SSC / PDF</b> button opens a picker for an on-file Site Criteria Screening or a concept or location document, but this version can't read them yet — click <b>Fill top section</b> and you'll get a message saying so.","Fill the concept and location fields by hand for now. Everything else on the sheet — the Money Test, the Scorecard and the Call — works the same."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Where’s my analysis saved?","a":"On the server, as you type — the toolbar chip says Saved ✓. It’s in the list on this page, on the linked tenant’s Site Fit card and on the space page."},{"q":"Who can see and change it?","a":"You, the owner of the linked tenant engagement, admins, and anyone with See all records (view only). Only the maker, the tenant’s owner and admins can change it; deleting needs the delete permission."},{"q":"I had an analysis open before this update — where did it go?","a":"It was kept only in that browser. Open Site Fit in the same browser and click Open & save on the amber banner to move it to the server."},{"q":"Why is my score low when every factor I rated is a 4 or 5?","a":"Unrated factors count as zero. Rate all nine manual factors; economics fills itself once the Money Test has numbers."},{"q":"What counts as healthy occupancy?","a":"The sheet treats under 10% of sales as healthy, 10–12% as workable but tight, and anything over 12% as rich. At 15% or worse it calls a pass on economics."}]},"calculators":{"intro":"Four deal calculators on one page — cap rate, sale commission, lease commission and net effective rent. Pick a tab, type the numbers, and the math updates as you go.","overview":["Calculators lives under Tools in the left sidebar. The tab bar across the top switches between Cap Rate, Sale Commission, Lease Commission and Net Effective Rent; each one recalculates on every keystroke, so there is no Calculate button.","Nothing here is saved to a deal. Use it to sanity-check a price, quote a fee or explain a concession on a call — then use Print on the commission, lease and rent tabs for a branded one-pager."],"feats":{"caprate":{"lead":"The Cap Rate tab works the Value = NOI ÷ Cap Rate formula in whichever direction you need.","body":["Pick what you want to solve with the three buttons: <b>Solve Value</b>, <b>Solve NOI</b> or <b>Solve Cap Rate</b>. The inputs change to the other two fields, and the answer shows in the result box with a one-line read-back (for example \"$175,000 NOI at a 7% cap\").","<b>Solve NOI</b> tells you the income a property has to throw off to justify a given price at a given cap. <b>Solve Cap Rate</b> gives the implied cap on an asking price — useful when a seller quotes a number with no context."],"steps":["Open the <b>Cap Rate</b> tab.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Solve Value</span>, <span class=\"kbd\">Solve NOI</span> or <span class=\"kbd\">Solve Cap Rate</span>.","Type the two known figures — the answer updates as you type."],"tip":"Enter cap rate as a plain percent — 7 or 7.25, not 0.07 — and dollar amounts without commas (175000, not 175,000)."},"sensitivity":{"lead":"When solving for value, a table shows what the same NOI is worth across a range of cap rates.","body":["The <b>Value sensitivity by cap rate</b> card appears under the calculator in <b>Solve Value</b> mode. It runs your NOI at cap rates from 1.0% below to 1.0% above your input, in 0.25% steps, and shows the value at each plus the difference from your base (in dollars and percent). Your own cap rate is highlighted.","Use it to show a seller what a quarter-point of cap compression or expansion does to price. The table hides when you switch to Solve NOI or Solve Cap Rate."]},"salecomm":{"lead":"The Sale Commission tab (the Commission Forecaster) works a business-sale fee the marginal, tiered way.","body":["Enter the <b>Sale Price</b> and the forecaster runs it through the <b>Tiered Rate Schedule</b>: each tranche of the price earns that tier's rate and the tiers are added up. If that comes in under the firm's minimum fee, the gross is raised to the minimum and the card is labelled <b>Firm minimum fee</b>. The summary cards show <b>Gross Commission</b>, the <b>Blended Rate</b> (gross ÷ price), <b>Agent Net</b>, <b>House Net</b> and any <b>Referral Fee</b>.","The schedule loads from the firm's rate card (set by an admin under Settings → Commission Structure), so every rep quotes from the same numbers. Tiers the price doesn't reach are dimmed so you can see where the deal lands.","<b>Quick Scenarios</b> under the schedule shows gross commission and blended rate at $1M, $2.5M, $5M, $15M and $40M using the same card — useful when a seller asks \"what if we get more?\""],"steps":["Open the <b>Sale Commission</b> tab.","Optionally type the <b>Deal / Business</b> name — it becomes the sheet title.","Type the <b>Sale Price</b>; the tier table and summary fill in."]},"overrides":{"lead":"Every rate and break point in the tier table is editable for the deal in front of you.","body":["Click into any rate or upper threshold in the <b>Tiered Rate Schedule</b> and type a new number. Gross commission, the split and Quick Scenarios recalculate immediately. This changes only what's on your screen — the firm rate card is untouched.","Click <b>↺ Reset rate card</b> to go back to the firm schedule, or <b>Reset</b> in the toolbar to clear the whole sheet."]},"firmcard":{"lead":"Set the firm's sale-commission schedule right from the forecaster.","body":["Signed in as an admin, the <b>Sale Commission</b> tab shows <b>💾 Save as firm rate card</b>. Edit the tier rates and thresholds, then click it and the schedule is saved as the firm rate card (the same one under Settings → Commission Structure).","Reps never see the button — they quote from whatever card is saved."],"tip":"Check a few prices in Quick Scenarios before saving — every rep's quotes change the moment you do."},"split":{"lead":"The sale and lease commission tabs both carry the deal through to agent net and house net.","body":["On <b>Sale Commission</b>, a <b>Referral Fee</b> % comes off the gross first (enter who it's owed to under <b>Referred by</b> and it shows on the Referral Fee card). The rest is split by <b>Agent Share</b>: for reps it's fixed at the firm's agent split from Settings → Commission Structure, or your personal split if an admin set one; admins can type a different share for a deal.","On <b>Lease Commission</b>, <b>Side Share</b> handles co-brokes (100% sole, 50% co-broke), the referral comes off the firm's gross, and <b>Agent Split</b> divides the rest (blank = 50%)."],"tip":"On a co-broke lease, set Side Share first — the referral and split then work off your side only."},"leasecomm":{"lead":"The Lease Commission tab figures a restaurant lease fee on total lease consideration across the term.","body":["Enter <b>Square Feet</b>, <b>Base Rent ($/SF/Yr)</b>, <b>Annual Escalation</b>, <b>Lease Term (Years)</b> and <b>Commission Rate</b>. The <b>Rent & Commission Schedule</b> lays out every year — rent per SF, annual base rent, the rate applied and the commission — and the summary shows <b>Gross Commission</b>, <b>Lease Consideration</b>, <b>Effective Rate</b> and <b>Commission / SF</b>.","For a declining schedule, set <b>Step-Down After Year</b> and a <b>Reduced Rate</b>; years after the step are highlighted. Percentage rent, free rent and TI are not included in the consideration.","The tab opens with a sample loaded (2,500 SF at $36, 3% bumps, 10 years, 4%). <b>Load sample</b> brings it back; <b>Reset</b> clears it."]},"ner":{"lead":"The Net Effective Rent tab shows what a lease really costs once the concessions are backed out.","body":["Enter the lease terms (<b>Square Feet</b>, <b>Base Rent</b>, <b>Annual Escalation</b>, <b>Lease Term</b>), then the concessions: <b>Free Rent (Months)</b> and <b>TI Allowance ($/SF)</b>. You get <b>Net Effective Rent</b> per SF, <b>Face Rent (Avg)</b>, the <b>Effective Discount</b> and <b>NER / Month</b>, plus total base rent, free rent value and TI value.","Add <b>NNN / Op-Ex</b> and a <b>Gross Occupancy Cost</b> row appears with effective gross rent per SF, per month and the total annual occupancy figure.","Free rent is taken from lease start, and TI is treated as a straight rent offset (not amortized with interest). The <b>Rent Schedule</b> marks the abated years."],"tip":"Use this when comparing two LOIs — a lower face rent with less TI can cost the tenant more."},"print":{"lead":"The Sale Commission, Lease Commission and Net Effective Rent tabs each print as a branded sheet.","body":["Type the deal name in <b>Deal / Business</b> or <b>Property / Deal</b> — it becomes the sheet's headline, and the subtitle summarizes the key numbers. Then click <b>Print</b> in that calculator's toolbar and choose \"Save as PDF\" for a file.","The Cap Rate tab has no print button; use your browser's print if you need it."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Why can't I change Agent Share on the sale commission tab?","a":"Reps quote at the firm's agent split (Settings → Commission Structure), or their own split if an admin set one on their user record. Only admins can type a different share for a deal."},{"q":"Do these numbers save to my deal?","a":"No. The calculators are scratch math. Print or save a PDF if you need to keep a copy."},{"q":"Where does the tiered rate card come from?","a":"From the firm's commission structure, set by an admin under Settings → Commission Structure. You can override it on screen for one deal."}]},"email_finder":{"intro":"Email Finder is a prospecting tool. It pulls restaurants for an area and cuisine from Google, then reads each restaurant's own website for a public contact email.","overview":["Email Finder lives under Tools and is admin-only. Results go into their own saved lists, separate from your CRM contacts and from Campaigns subscribers, so a raw prospect list never pollutes either one.","It uses the firm's Google Maps API key from Admin, and the Places API (New) must be turned on for that key's Google Cloud project with billing enabled. If the key is missing, a yellow note at the top says so."],"feats":{"search":{"lead":"Build a prospect list for any market in seconds.","body":["Under <b>Search</b>, leave the toggle on <b>City & State</b>, enter the <b>City</b> and <b>State</b>, pick a <b>Restaurant type</b> and choose <b>Max results</b> (20, 40 or 60). Click <span class=\"kbd\">Find restaurants</span> or press Enter.","<b>Restaurant type</b> uses Google's own categories, so the match is tight. You can pick a cuisine (Mexican, Italian, Sushi…), a style (Barbecue, Steakhouse, Fine dining, Fast food…), bars and cafés, or bakery and sweets. <b>Any restaurant</b> gives the widest net.","Each row shows the restaurant name and address, phone, a website link and the Google rating with its review count."],"steps":["Enter the City and State","Pick a Restaurant type and Max results","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Find restaurants</span>"],"tip":"Google caps one search at 60. To cover a whole metro, run several types or neighborhoods and let them <a href=\"#stack\">stack</a>."},"zip":{"lead":"Work a trade area, not a whole city.","body":["Switch the toggle to <b>ZIP code</b> and enter one or more ZIPs separated by commas, like <i>78701, 78702</i>. Each ZIP is searched on its own and then filtered to addresses really in that ZIP. Google's results spill into neighboring ZIPs, and those are thrown out.","Up to 12 ZIPs run per search. Any beyond that are skipped, and the message tells you how many to run separately. In ZIP mode, <b>Max results</b> caps the total across all the ZIPs; if it's reached before every ZIP is searched, the message says how far it got so you can run the rest or raise Max results."],"tip":"Searching by ZIP is the cleanest way to work a trade area around a listing."},"region":{"lead":"Skip looking up ZIP codes for the areas you work most.","body":["The <b>Region preset</b> dropdown has San Antonio areas: <b>North San Antonio</b>, <b>Stone Oak / Far North</b>, <b>Medical Center / Northwest</b>, <b>Alamo Heights / Central</b> and <b>Downtown San Antonio</b>.","Picking one switches to ZIP mode, fills in that area's ZIPs and puts the area name in the list-name box. Then click <span class=\"kbd\">Find restaurants</span>."],"tip":"Change the ZIP box after loading a preset to trim or extend the area."},"stack":{"lead":"Build one big list from several small searches.","body":["A new search doesn't replace what's on screen. It adds to it, and restaurants already listed are skipped. The message shows how many were added, how many were already listed and the running total.","<span class=\"kbd\">Clear list</span> empties the working list so the next search starts fresh. Saved lists aren't affected."],"tip":"Run Barbecue, then Steakhouse, then Bar & Grill for the same ZIPs to build a full-service list."},"emails":{"lead":"Google doesn't give out emails. This finds the ones restaurants publish on their own sites.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">✉ Find emails</span>. A progress bar fills as websites are read in small batches, and emails drop into the table as they're found. Rows still being checked say <i>searching…</i>.","For each site it checks the home page, then /contact, /contact-us, /about, /about-us and /reservations, and stops at the first page with an address. When there are several, it prefers mailto links, addresses on the restaurant's own domain, and inboxes like info@, reservations@, events@ or catering@. Image file names and tracking junk are filtered out.","Only rows with a website and no email yet are checked, so you can run it again after adding more searches. Restaurants that only use a contact form won't return an email, so expect a partial hit rate."],"tip":"Tick <b>Only rows with an email</b> to see just the hits."},"save":{"lead":"Keep the lists you build.","body":["Type a name (it's suggested from the type and area) and click <span class=\"kbd\">Save as list</span>. The confirmation shows how many restaurants and how many with email.","Under <b>Saved lists</b>, each list shows its type, area, who made it and a count like <i>12 / 40 emails</i>. <b>Open</b> loads it back into the table so you can keep searching or finding emails. <b>CSV</b> downloads it. <b>Delete</b> removes it for good.","Saved lists are kept apart from CRM contacts and Campaigns subscribers."],"tip":"After you <b>Open</b> a saved list, the button reads <b>Save changes</b> and updates that list in place; <b>Save as new</b> keeps the original and saves a separate copy."},"export":{"lead":"Turn a prospect list into a mailing audience the right way.","body":["<span class=\"kbd\">Export CSV</span> downloads the working list with <b>Name</b>, <b>Email</b>, <b>Phone</b>, <b>Website</b>, <b>Address</b>, <b>Rating</b> and <b>Reviews</b>. A saved list's <b>CSV</b> link does the same.","Nothing goes to Campaigns automatically. To mail these restaurants, import the CSV on <a href=\"rrg_mail_subscribers.html\">Subscribers</a>, typed as Restaurants, and send from <a href=\"rrg_mass_studio.html\">Campaigns</a>. Every email then carries your firm's identity and a one-click unsubscribe."],"tip":"These are published business addresses. Send B2B outreach through Campaigns so you stay CAN-SPAM clean."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why did my search say Google denied the request?","a":"The Places API (New) isn't turned on for the Google Cloud project behind your Maps key, or billing is off. Fix it in Google Cloud. The key itself is set in Admin."},{"q":"Why do so few restaurants have an email?","a":"Many restaurants only publish a contact form or a phone number. The finder only returns addresses that are posted on the site."},{"q":"Does this add anyone to my CRM or subscriber list?","a":"No. Lists stay here until you export them and import them yourself."}]},"tracked_emails":{"intro":"Tracked Emails is one log of every email sent to your contacts, from a contact's page, by an automation, or from your own Gmail, with open counts on the ones the app sent.","overview":["Tracked Emails lives under Tools. Emails are matched to contacts by email address, and each row links back to the contact. Reps see the emails they sent, plus automation emails to their own contacts or from automations they enrolled. Admins and anyone with the see-all permission see the whole team's.","Opens can only be tracked on emails sent from inside the app. Emails sent straight from Gmail carry no tracking, so they're hidden unless you choose to show them."],"feats":{"log":{"lead":"Stop digging through a contact's history to see what went out.","body":["Each row shows <b>Sent</b>, <b>Subject</b>, <b>Contact</b> (click it to open their record), <b>Company</b>, <b>Opens</b>, <b>Last opened</b> and <b>Sent by</b>. Emails from an automation are tagged <b>automation</b> and Gmail ones are tagged <b>gmail</b>. A hidden <b>To</b> column can be turned on under Columns.","Newest first by default; sort by any column. The newest 500 emails are loaded.","Reps see the emails they sent, plus automation emails to their own contacts or from automations they enrolled. Admins and users with the see-all permission see everyone's."],"tip":"Sort by <b>Opens</b> to find the buyers who keep coming back to your email."},"opens":{"lead":"An open count that isn't padded by your own clicks.","body":["Emails sent from the app carry an invisible tracking image. Each time the recipient's mail app loads it, the open is counted and <b>Last opened</b> updates (like <i>2h ago</i>).","Opens from the same IP address a contact-page email was sent from, and opens in the first 20 seconds after sending, aren't counted. That keeps your own preview and the recipient's mail scanner from looking like a real read.","<b>Opens</b> shows <i>N opens</i>, <i>unopened</i>, or <i>not tracked</i> for Gmail sends."],"tip":"Recipients who block images won't register opens, so treat <i>unopened</i> as \"maybe\", not \"no\"."},"detail":{"lead":"See when, and on what, they read it.","body":["Hover over a green <b>N opens</b> pill, or click it to pin it, for a card listing each counted open, newest first, with the date and time and <b>Mobile</b> or <b>Desktop</b>. Up to the last 25 opens are shown.","Click anywhere else to close a pinned card."],"tip":"Three opens on a phone in one evening means the buyer is showing it around. Call them in the morning."},"read":{"lead":"Check exactly what was said before you follow up.","body":["Click any <b>Subject</b> to open the email. The header shows <b>To</b>, <b>Cc</b> if any, <b>Sent</b> with sender and source, <b>Opens</b> (like <i>Opened 3× · last …</i> or <i>Not opened yet</i>) and a <b>Contact</b> link. The message text is shown below.","Gmail emails only show a short preview. Open them in Gmail for the full message.","Press Esc or click outside the window to close it."],"tip":"Read your last email before you call so you don't repeat yourself."},"gmail":{"lead":"Get the emails you sent outside the app into the contact's history too.","body":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">⟳ Sync sent from Gmail</span>. It reads your Gmail Sent folder for the last 90 days and adds every email sent to an address that matches a contact. The message tells you how many emails were added, to how many contacts, and how many were scanned. Emails already added are skipped.","Your Gmail has to be connected first (Account → Gmail). An admin can also have connected reps' sent mail pulled in automatically on a schedule, under Integrations.","Gmail emails can't be tracked for opens, so they're hidden by default. Tick <b>Show Gmail (untracked) sends</b> to see the full log. The page remembers your choice."],"tip":"Sync once after connecting Gmail to backfill your history. The automatic sync keeps it current after that."},"stats":{"lead":"A quick read on whether your emails are landing.","body":["The tiles show <b>Emails sent</b> (everything in the log), <b>Tracked · app sends</b>, <b>Opened</b> and <b>Open rate · of tracked</b>. Gmail sends are left out of the open rate because they can't be tracked."],"tip":"If your open rate drops, check your subject lines and whether you're emailing stale contacts."}},"faq":[{"q":"Why does an email say not tracked?","a":"It was sent from Gmail, not the app. Gmail sends can't carry a tracking image. Send from the contact's page to track opens."},{"q":"I opened my own email to check it. Did that count?","a":"Not if you opened it from the same IP address you sent it from (contact-page sends) or within 20 seconds of sending — those opens aren't counted."},{"q":"Why can't I see a teammate's emails?","a":"Reps see their own sends, plus automation emails to their own contacts or from automations they enrolled. Admins and users with the see-all permission see everyone's."}]},"feedback":{"intro":"The Wish List is where the team asks for changes to the platform — new features, fixes, questions — and where admins decide what gets built and track it through to shipped.","overview":["Open it from Admin in the left sidebar. The count tiles across the top (All, Open, and one per status) double as filters; under them, the Requests list shows each request's title, detail, who asked, type, priority, status and votes.","Anyone signed in can add a request and vote (one vote each). Admins set type, priority and status, approve requests, send the approved batch to Claude for building, and paste Claude's status replies back in."],"feats":{"new":{"lead":"+ New request opens a short form for a feature, bug, improvement or question.","body":["Click <b>+ New request</b>. A <b>Title</b> is required; pick a <b>Type</b> (Feature request, Bug, Improvement, Question, Other) and a <b>Priority</b> (Critical, High, Medium, Low — Medium by default), then explain it under <b>Details</b>: what you want, why, or the steps to reproduce a bug.","Click <b>Submit request</b>. It appears in the list as <b>New</b>, with your name and the date, and gets an id chip (fb_…) used to track it."],"tip":"One request per item — it's easier to approve, build and mark shipped."},"filter":{"lead":"The tiles at the top count requests by status and filter the list when clicked.","body":["<b>All</b> shows everything; <b>Open</b> hides Shipped and Declined; the rest show one status each — New, Approved, Sent to Claude, In progress, Shipped, Declined.","Next to the buttons, the <b>All types</b> drop-down narrows to one type, and the search box matches title, detail, the person who asked and the type."]},"vote":{"lead":"Each request carries a vote count — one vote per person.","body":["Click the <b>▲</b> in the Votes column to add your vote; click it again to take it back. Sort by <b>Votes</b> to rank requests by demand.","Everyone gets one vote per request, and the ▲ stays highlighted on the ones you've voted for."]},"inline":{"lead":"Admins can change the Type, Priority and Status cells right in the list.","body":["Click the cell, pick a new value from the drop-down, and it saves as soon as you leave the field (Enter saves, Esc cancels). Sorting by <b>Priority</b> or <b>Status</b> follows the real order — Critical first, New through Declined — not the alphabet.","Type, priority and status are admin decisions — for everyone else these cells are read-only."]},"replies":{"lead":"Each request shows its history in the list.","body":["Under the title and detail you'll see a <b>↩ Claude:</b> line with the latest note on the work, plus when it was approved and sent. Filter to <b>Shipped</b> to see what's been delivered."]},"approve":{"lead":"Approval is the gate between a wish and a build.","body":["On a <b>New</b> request, admins see an <b>Approve</b> button; on an <b>Approved</b> one, <b>Unapprove</b> returns it to New. Approving records who approved it and when."]},"send":{"lead":"✈ Send to Claude packages all approved requests into one brief.","body":["Click <b>✈ Send to Claude</b>. The box lists every Approved request with its id, type, priority, title and detail, and ends with instructions for Claude to reply one line per item as <i>fb_id | status | note</i>.","Click <b>Copy</b>, paste it into Claude, then click <b>Mark as sent</b> — those requests move to <b>Sent to Claude</b> with today's date."],"steps":["Approve the requests you want built.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">✈ Send to Claude</span> and copy the brief.","Paste it into Claude.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Mark as sent</span>."]},"paste":{"lead":"⇤ Paste Claude update closes the loop without editing rows one by one.","body":["Click <b>⇤ Paste Claude update</b> and paste the lines Claude replied with, one per request: <i>fb_abc123 | Shipped | Built the expense tracker</i>. Click <b>Apply updates</b> and each matching request takes the new status, and the note becomes its Claude reply.","Status words are matched loosely (\"done\" counts as Shipped). Ids that don't match a request are reported back so you can check them."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Who can see the Wish List?","a":"It sits under Admin in the sidebar, so admins reach it from there. Anyone signed in can add a request and vote; only admins change type, priority or status, approve, send and paste updates."},{"q":"Can I delete a request?","a":"If your role has delete permission — Delete on the row, or tick several and use the bulk Delete."}]},"tickets":{"intro":"Office Requests is the help desk between reps and the brokerage office. Open a request for signage, marketing collateral, photography, a listing action, a compliance question, IT or supplies; an AI office assistant answers immediately and the right department is notified.","overview":["Open it from Admin in the left sidebar. The counts across the top show Open, Overdue, Answered and Closed requests; the filter chips under Your requests narrow the list, and clicking a request's number or subject opens its thread.","Every request is routed to a department (set up by an admin under Settings → Departments). Members of that department see it, get an email when it comes in, can reply as the office and keep private notes. You always see your own requests, and admins see every department's."],"feats":{"new":{"lead":"+ New request sends a numbered request to the brokerage office.","body":["Click <b>+ New request</b>, fill in a <b>Subject</b>, choose the <b>Department</b> (the <b>Category</b> list follows the department you pick) and a <b>Priority</b> — Normal, High or Urgent — then describe it under <b>What do you need?</b>. Subject and details are both required. Click <b>Send to office</b>.","The request gets a number (#1001 and up) and the department's members — or the services desk if no one is assigned — get an email with the details and a link."],"steps":["Click <span class=\"kbd\">+ New request</span>.","Fill Subject, Department, Category, Priority and the details.","Click <span class=\"kbd\">Send to office</span>."],"tip":"Put the listing or address, sizes, quantities, deadline and where to send it in the details — the office acts on what's written."},"ai":{"lead":"There's no waiting for someone to pick up a request — the office assistant works it immediately.","body":["As soon as you send a request, the assistant replies in the thread and sets the status: <b>Answered</b> when it can resolve it with guidance, a template, a drafted message or steps; <b>Action Needed</b> when someone at the office has to do something physical (order a sign, book a photographer, cut a check); or <b>Info Needed</b> when it needs specifics only you have, listed exactly.","It won't claim to have done anything it can't do, and won't invent vendors, prices or staff names. It can also explain and troubleshoot this platform itself.","If the assistant is unavailable, the request stays <b>Open</b> with a note that a person will review it."]},"thread":{"lead":"Each request is a conversation between you and the office.","body":["Open a request and type in the reply box, then click <b>Send reply</b>. When you reply to your own request it goes back to <b>Open</b> and the assistant responds again with the full thread in view.","When someone from the office replies, their message is posted as the office, the status moves to <b>Answered</b>, and you get an email with their reply and a link back."]},"due":{"lead":"Requests carry a target date so nothing sits.","body":["A new request is due 1 day out if Urgent, 2 if High and 5 if Normal. Anyone on the request can change it with the <b>Due</b> date box in the thread header.","Past the due date and not Closed, the request shows ⚠ in the list, an <b>Overdue</b> badge on the thread, counts in the <b>Overdue</b> tile and chip, and adds to the red count on Office Requests in the sidebar."]},"filters":{"lead":"The top of the page is built for triage.","body":["The tiles count <b>Open</b>, <b>Overdue</b>, <b>Answered</b> and <b>Closed</b>. The chips filter the list: <b>All</b>, <b>Needs you</b> (Open, Action Needed and Info Needed), <b>Overdue</b>, <b>Answered</b> and <b>Closed</b>.","The list shows number, subject (with a 📎 if files are attached), department, category, priority, status, due date and how many days it's been open."]},"files":{"lead":"Anyone on a request — you or the office — can attach files to it.","body":["In the thread, click <b>📎 Attach a file</b>. PDF, Word, Excel, CSV, PowerPoint, images, text and ZIP are accepted, up to 20 MB each. Files list under <b>Attachments</b> with type and size, open in a new tab, and can be removed."]},"close":{"lead":"Closing keeps the history; deleting erases it.","body":["Click <b>Close request</b> under the reply box when you're done. A closed request can't take replies until you click <b>Reopen it</b>.","<b>🗑 Delete this request</b> (requester or admin) removes the request and its whole thread for good — use Close unless it was opened by mistake."]},"internal":{"lead":"Internal office notes are for the team working the request, not the rep who opened it.","body":["When you're a member of the department a request routed to (or an admin), the thread shows an <b>Internal office notes · office only</b> box. Add a note with <b>Add internal note</b>; delete with the ✕ on the note.","Notes never appear to the requester. Department membership is set per person under Settings → Users."]},"admin":{"lead":"Admins get extra controls for running the desk.","body":["An <b>All users</b> drop-down next to the filter chips narrows the list to one requester, the <b>Requested by</b> column is shown, the thread header shows who it's from, and a status drop-down lets an admin set Open, Answered, Action Needed, Info Needed or Closed directly. Admins can also delete any request from the ✕ on its row.","Admins see every request, whichever department it routed to."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Who sees my request?","a":"You, the members of the department it routed to, and the firm's admins."},{"q":"The assistant said Action Needed. Is it done?","a":"No — that means a person at the office has to act. It's logged and the department was emailed; watch the thread for their reply."},{"q":"How do I set up departments?","a":"An admin creates them under Settings → Departments and assigns people to them under Settings → Users. Requests in a department's categories route to its members."}]},"team":{"intro":"Team is the firm roster: each member's active listings, deals, commission earned, commission still owed and pipeline, with a click-through to their employee record.","overview":["Open Team from Admin in the left sidebar. Admins land on the roster — firm totals across the top, one row per member below. A rep who opens Team is taken straight to their own record and never sees anyone else's numbers.","Every figure is computed live from the listings and deals each person owns, so nothing is typed in by hand. Admins also add and deactivate team members here; full account settings live under Settings → Users."],"feats":{"roster":{"lead":"The Team members list puts the whole firm's book side by side.","body":["Each row shows <b>Title</b>, <b>Listings</b>, <b>Deals</b> (with how many closed), <b>Didn't close</b>, <b>Commission GCI</b>, <b>GCI YTD</b>, <b>Received</b>, <b>Waiting on</b>, <b>Net</b> and <b>Pipeline</b>. The list opens with the highest Commission GCI at the top; click any column header to re-sort — biggest pipelines, most still owed. Your sort is remembered next time.","The card header shows the head count and the firm's agent split."]},"kpis":{"lead":"The tiles above the roster add up every member's numbers.","body":["<b>Active Listings</b>, <b>Deals</b> (closed all-time and this year), <b>Commission GCI · YTD</b> (with all-time underneath), <b>Didn't close</b>, <b>Received</b>, <b>Waiting on</b> and <b>Pipeline</b>.","All of it is calculated from the listings and deals each person owns — if a deal isn't showing, check who owns the listing."]},"owed":{"lead":"Waiting on is commission earned on closed deals that hasn't been paid yet.","body":["<b>Received</b> is what's been paid to date; <b>Waiting on</b> is the gap between commission due and commission paid on closed deals. Sort the roster by Waiting on to see who has the most outstanding.","It drops to zero once commission paid on the deal covers what's due. Payments recorded on the deal's invoices update Commission paid on their own; money that didn't come through an invoice can be entered on the Deal card."]},"record":{"lead":"Every team member has an employee record that reads like a contact record.","body":["Click a name on the roster to open it: a header with photo and role, the same production and commission figures, editable employee details, payroll and tax for admins, and their <b>Listings</b>, <b>Deals</b> and <b>Agreements</b>.","A rep who opens Team lands on their own record."]},"details":{"lead":"The employee record holds the contact and HR basics.","body":["Home address, personal email and phone, LinkedIn, territory, start and end dates, emergency contact and HR notes. A rep can update their own contact details; start date, end date and HR notes are admin-only."]},"commission":{"lead":"Net is the rep's share of closed-deal commission.","body":["Net is worked from GCI through the firm's commission setup — the agent split, plus any referral or co-broke share on the deal. The firm split is set under Settings → Commission Structure (default 50%) and shown in the roster header; change it there and every Net figure updates — except for a rep with a personal split set under Settings → Users, which wins."]},"private":{"lead":"Reps never see each other's numbers.","body":["The server only returns the roster to admins. Anyone else who opens Team is sent to their own employee record, and payroll and tax are hidden from them entirely."]},"add":{"lead":"+ Add member creates a new user account from the roster.","body":["Click <b>+ Add member</b> in the Team members header. Enter first and last name, a <b>Username</b>, <b>Role</b> (defaults to Associate), email, title, phone and a <b>Temporary password</b> of at least 6 characters, then <b>Add member</b>.","They sign in with that username and password and change it under Account. For passwords, permission overrides and book hand-offs, use <b>Manage users →</b> (Settings → Users)."]},"remove":{"lead":"Deactivating someone doesn't delete their work.","body":["Click the 🗑 at the end of a member's row and confirm. Their login is blocked — the same as <b>Disable</b> under Settings → Users — and they drop off the roster. Their listings, deals, records and history stay in the system. You can't deactivate yourself or the last active admin.","To bring them back, <b>Enable</b> them under Settings → Users; to delete the account for good, use <b>Remove</b> there. To hand their contacts and companies to another rep, use <b>Reassign</b> there first."]},"payroll":{"lead":"The payroll card on each employee record is admin-only.","body":["It holds payment method, W-9 on file with the date received, tax classification, the tax ID used for 1099 reporting and payment notes. It is never shown to the employee or to peers."]}},"faq":[{"q":"A rep's listing or deal isn't on their row.","a":"Figures follow the listing's owner. Set the owner to that team member and the roster picks it up."},{"q":"Why is Waiting on zero when a deal closed?","a":"It's zero once the commission paid on the deal matches what's due. Record payments on the deal's invoices and Commission paid follows — or enter installments on the Deal card as they arrive."},{"q":"Can reps see each other's commission?","a":"No. The roster is admin-only; reps only see their own record."}]},"settings":{"intro":"Settings is the admin hub for the whole platform: who's on the team and what they can do, how deals move, the paperwork rules, the firm's brand, the data and its backups, the email and API connections, and the AI.","overview":["Open Settings from Admin in the left sidebar. The left rail groups every admin tool — Users & Access, Workflow, Deal Documents, Brand & Public, Accounting, Data, Integrations and Artificial Intelligence — and the panel on the right loads the one you pick. The address bar keeps the open tool (for example ?tab=backup), so you can bookmark or send a link straight to it.","Most panels save as you go or have their own Save button. Changes apply firm-wide, so make them deliberately — and check Activity & Logs to see who's signed in and which tools they've used."],"feats":{"hub":{"lead":"Settings is a single page with a rail of admin tools on the left and the selected tool on the right.","body":["The top of the rail holds <b>Brokerage Info</b> and <b>General Settings</b>; below are the groups <b>Users & Access</b>, <b>Workflow</b>, <b>Deal Documents</b>, <b>Brand & Public</b>, <b>Accounting</b>, <b>Data</b>, <b>Integrations</b> and <b>Artificial Intelligence</b>. Click an item and it loads in the panel; General Settings opens by default.","The page address changes to <b>?tab=</b> plus the tool's name (for example <i>rrg_settings.html?tab=commission</i>), so a link or bookmark opens that tool directly."]},"users":{"lead":"Users & Access controls who signs in and what they can touch.","body":["<b>Users</b> creates sign-ins, resets passwords, and assigns each person a role and their departments. <b>Disable</b> blocks the sign-in but keeps the account and history; <b>Remove</b> deletes the account. <b>Reassign</b> moves every contact and company a person owns to another rep. Per-person overrides beat the role's defaults once permission enforcement is on.","<b>Roles & Permissions</b> defines each role's access, which sidebar sections it sees (<b>Menu visibility</b>), and a blanket <b>Admin-only tools</b> lock. Enforcement has an on/off switch: set roles up with it off — everyone keeps seeing everything — then turn it on, and off again instantly if something looks wrong.","<b>Departments</b> groups people so office requests route to the right team, and <b>Activity & Logs</b> shows sign-ins (including failed ones) and tool usage by person."],"tip":"Give reps the narrowest role that lets them do the job; you can always widen it."},"pipelines":{"lead":"Workflow → Pipelines & Stages and Automations shape how deals flow.","body":["<b>Pipelines & Stages</b> holds each pipeline's stages and their close likelihood — <b>↗ Auto-fill likelihood</b> spreads it from low to near-certain for you to adjust. <b>Deal-type routing</b> picks which pipeline each kind of deal enters; for example, a new seller screening call drops into the Business Sales pipeline you choose. Routing changes apply to new deals; existing ones stay put.","<b>Automations</b> builds follow-up plans — a list of email, task, notification and other steps spaced by days — that you apply to a contact or set on a listing so every buyer lead for it is worked automatically. Emails personalize with {{first_name}} and replies go to the enrolling rep. A stage's <b>On enter</b> / <b>On leave</b> automation in Pipelines & Stages runs when a listing or a buyer moves into or out of it."]},"commission":{"lead":"The Accounting group holds the numbers every fee and invoice is built from.","body":["<b>Commission Structure</b> sets both fee methods — the tiered (Modified Lehman) rate card, where each tranche earns its own rate, and a flat rate — plus a <b>minimum fee per location</b> for multi-unit deals and the <b>Agent share of company dollar</b> (the agent's cut of GCI after any referral fee and the co-broke side's share). Each agreement picks a method and its projected GCI flows to the deal. The tiered card is the one the Commission Forecaster loads, and the agent share drives Net on the Team roster and Commissions — except for a rep with a personal split set under Users.","<b>Accounting & Invoicing</b> holds payment terms, tax, payment methods and your products and services list, which you can paste in from a QuickBooks export."]},"paperwork":{"lead":"Deal Documents holds the templates and rules behind every document the platform generates.","body":["<b>Agreement Templates</b> are the tokenized documents your listing agreements, NDAs and other agreements are built from. <b>LOI Builder Setup</b> sets the deal defaults pre-filled on every new LOI (reps can override), the key terms, and the clause sections and boilerplate. <b>Valuation Rules</b> set the factors and defaults the valuation engine uses.","<b>Questionnaires & Interviews</b> builds the call questionnaires — or uploads one — and assigns each to a call type, so every rep runs the same questions in the same order; changes apply on the next call. The seller interview, data room access and booking emails each have their own template panel too."]},"roomsecurity":{"lead":"Deal Documents → Data Room sets firm-wide protection for every buyer data room.","body":["Turn on buyer verification to require a one-time code emailed to the buyer on top of their access code, so only someone with the code and the inbox gets in — rooms can override this on their own <b>Buyers & access</b> page.","Key-document alerts email the room owner the first time a buyer opens a P&L, financials, lease or tax return (once per buyer per document). Watermarking stamps every page of every PDF download with the buyer's name, email, the time and your firm, so a leaked copy traces back. You can edit the alert email here and reset it to default."]},"calendar":{"lead":"Workflow → Calendar sets the firm's clock and turns calendar capabilities on or off for the whole team.","body":["Switches cover the two-way sync button, dated tasks on the calendar and contact cards, Google Meet links on meetings, working hours and location, meeting attachments, self-scheduling booking links and sharing a calendar with specific teammates. Each saves the moment you flip it.","Turning a feature off hides its controls everywhere but keeps the data — synced events and booking links come back if you turn it on again. The meeting invitation email template is picked here too.","<b>Firm time zone</b> (America/Chicago unless you change it) is the clock for meeting times, task due dates and reminders: it decides when reminders fire, what counts as today or overdue, and the times on calendar invites guests receive."]},"branding":{"lead":"Branding & Appearance puts the firm's look on the app and everything it generates.","body":["Set the <b>App Name</b> shown in the browser tab, a <b>Header Announcement</b> across everyone's dashboard, a <b>Toolbar Logo</b> for the dark sidebar, a <b>Document Logo</b> for BOVs, LOIs, Marketing Packs, emails and the login screen, a <b>Favicon</b> and <b>Brand Colors</b>. The toolbar logo and favicon can be pulled straight from your website.","<b>Brokerage Info</b> holds the firm’s legal name, address, phone, license and EIN used on documents and email; <b>General Settings</b> holds renamed tool labels, type lists and currency."]},"integrations":{"lead":"The Integrations group is where outside services plug in.","body":["<b>API Keys</b> holds the Google Maps key (storefront and Places photos, with a <b>Test key</b> button), the Regrid token that pulls county parcel facts into property listings, outbound email (SMTP, with <b>Send test</b>) and Twilio for text reminders. Keys stay on the server and are never sent to browsers.","It also controls the background Gmail sent-mail sync that logs reps' sent emails onto matching contacts, and the BizBuySell lead-scan look-back windows. <b>Email / SMTP</b> and the <b>Integrations</b> gallery round out the group."]},"ai":{"lead":"The Artificial Intelligence group controls the model behind valuations, marketing packs, attack plans, offer analysis, the location finder and office requests.","body":["<b>AI Engine</b> sets the model (pick a tier or type an exact model id) and an API key that overrides the server's; it takes effect on the next AI run, and <b>Reset to default</b> recovers if a saved model id is wrong.","<b>AI Prompts</b> tunes how the assistant behaves, and <b>AI Usage</b> shows what's been run, how long it took and estimated cost, by time range."]},"data":{"lead":"The Data group keeps the book clean and complete.","body":["<b>Import Data</b> brings contacts and companies in from another CRM. <b>Data Enrichment</b> fills operating status, ratings, logos and cuisine, cleans and standardizes, finds duplicates and links people to companies by email domain. <b>Record Counts</b> shows what's in the system and <b>Export Data</b> pulls it to a spreadsheet.","There's a separate Data help guide for the full enrichment workflow."]},"backup":{"lead":"Data → Data Backup zips the entire book — every store, uploaded document and data-room file.","body":["A snapshot is taken daily and the last several are kept on the server (seven by default). From <b>Data Backup</b> you can download any snapshot, take a fresh one now, or download a live backup. An automation can also pull copies off-site with a secret token.","Restore replaces everything from a saved snapshot or an uploaded backup zip, and you must type RESTORE to confirm. It writes a safety snapshot of the current data first so the restore can be rolled back; if that snapshot can't be written, the restore stops and nothing is changed.","Behind the scenes every record is also mirrored to a database replica and files to object storage when those are configured, and the server restores itself from them if its disk is lost."],"tip":"Download a snapshot now and then and keep it somewhere outside the platform."}},"faq":[{"q":"A rep can see things they shouldn't.","a":"Check their role under Roles & Permissions, make sure permission enforcement is on, and look for a per-person override under Users."},{"q":"How do I send someone straight to one settings panel?","a":"Open the panel and copy the address — it ends in ?tab= and the panel's name."},{"q":"Where do I change the commission split?","a":"Accounting → Commission Structure, in <b>Agent share of company dollar</b> (the agent's cut after referral and co-broke). Net on the Team roster updates from it, except for a rep with a personal split set under Users."}]},"reports":{"intro":"Firm-wide analytics: where leads come from, what each rep is doing, which buyers are digging into data rooms, and who's using the system.","overview":["<b>Reports</b> (Admin group, admins only) has fourteen tabs in four groups. <b>Money</b>: GCI &amp; Closings, Forecast, Receivables. <b>Pipeline</b>: Pipeline &amp; Aging, Funnel, Inventory, Buyer Activity. <b>People</b>: Rep Scorecard, Lead Sources, Campaigns. <b>Activity</b>: New Leads, Rep Activity, Data Room, Team &amp; Usage.","Money, pipeline and people reports share one control bar — period, rep, market and type — plus <b>Export CSV</b> and <b>Print / PDF</b>. The activity tabs keep their 7 / 30 / 90-day and 12-month window. Every number is computed from the records in the app: deals, listings, invoices, agreements, BOVs, LOIs, tours, the activity log, data rooms and the Email Studio."],"feats":{"period":{"lead":"One control bar drives every money and pipeline report.","body":["<b>Period</b>: This month, Last month, This quarter, Last quarter, Year to date (the default), Last 12 months, Last year or Custom dates. <b>Rep</b>, <b>Market</b> and <b>Type</b> (Business sale, Asset sale, Tenant rep, Landlord rep) narrow the report.","Forecast, Receivables, Pipeline & Aging and Inventory are <b>as of today</b>, so the period is greyed out on those tabs; the filters still apply. The page address carries the tab, period and filters, so a bookmarked or shared link opens the same view."]},"gci":{"lead":"What the firm earned on deals that closed in the period.","body":["The top row shows <b>Gross commission (GCI)</b> with the change against the same dates last year, <b>Deals closed</b>, <b>Company dollar</b> (GCI after referral fees and the co-broke’s share) and <b>House net</b> (after each rep’s split). The chart puts each month next to the same month last year.","<b>By rep</b> credits GCI to the listing’s rep; <b>By deal type</b> splits business sales, asset sales and leases. The table lists every closed deal with price, GCI, referral/co-broke, company dollar, rep net, house net and commission status. The math is the same waterfall the Commissions page uses."]},"forecast":{"lead":"What’s likely to close, and when.","body":["Every open listing or search with a fee is in the pipeline. The fee is the deal’s commission when there’s a deal, else the expected fee on the listing, else an estimate from the asking price or valuation. Each is weighted by its stage’s <b>Likelihood %</b>; the chart shows weighted next to unweighted by expected close month, with <b>Past due</b> and <b>No date</b> at the ends.","Expected close is the deal’s expected close date, else the listing’s target close (“Q1 2027”, “Mar 2027”). If a stage has no likelihood % set, a yellow note names it — set it in Settings → Pipelines &amp; Stages so those deals count in the weighted number."]},"receivables":{"lead":"Money owed to the firm, and money the firm owes its reps.","body":["Every issued invoice with a balance, bucketed by days past its due date (or issue date when there’s no due date) and listed oldest first. Drafts and voids are left out.","<b>Commissions by rep</b> covers all closed deals to date: GCI, the rep’s net, what’s been paid to them, what they’re still owed (their share of what the firm has collected) and what the firm hasn’t collected yet."]},"pipeline":{"lead":"Where deals are sitting, and which ones are stuck.","body":["One table per pipeline: open count, fees, average days in stage, the stage’s target days, how many are stalled and its likelihood %. Stages marked as leads show a <b>lead</b> tag.","<b>Stalled</b> lists every deal that has sat in its stage at least the stage’s target days, longest over first. Days in stage count from when the card last moved."]},"funnel":{"lead":"How far listings get, and how long each step takes.","body":["Takes every business or asset sale opened in the period and shows how many reached each step — <b>Valuation (BOV)</b>, <b>Listing agreement signed</b>, <b>On market</b>, <b>LOI / under contract</b>, <b>Closed</b> — as a share of those opened, with the average days between steps.","Each step comes from the records themselves: the first BOV, the executed listing agreement, the listing start / live date or marketplace publish, and the deal opened on the listing. <b>Lost</b> shows listings marked Lost or No Deal, or whose deal died. The table shows each listing’s dates."]},"inventory":{"lead":"Everything you have on the market today.","body":["Each active sell-side listing with its asking price, price cuts (from the price history), days on market, when its agreement expires and whether it’s published to the marketplace. Longest on market first.","A yellow note flags expired listing agreements and active listings that aren’t published. Days on market count from the listing start / live date, else the signed agreement, else when the listing was opened."]},"buyers":{"lead":"The story you tell each seller.","body":["For each active sell-side listing: inquiries and buyers added (marketplace inquiries called out), NDAs (and how many signed), data-room visitors with views and downloads, buyer tours and offers/IOIs with the best offer.","Everything is counted by when it happened, inside the period."]},"scorecard":{"lead":"Every rep’s production, side by side.","body":["New listings and searches, closings, GCI, rep net and open pipeline go to the listing’s rep. BOVs, signed agreements, LOIs, tours, calls and emails go to whoever logged them.","Calls and emails aren’t tied to a market or deal type, so they show “n/a” while those filters are on. Bars below compare GCI and open pipeline by rep."]},"dollars":{"lead":"Where the business that pays comes from.","body":["Each listing or search is credited to its client contact’s <b>Lead source</b> — Referral when a referrer is set on the listing, BizBuySell when it came from BizBuySell. The table shows listings opened in the period, active now, deals closed, closed GCI, average GCI per closing and open pipeline.","“Unknown” means the client has no lead source — set it on the contact to sharpen the report."]},"campaigns":{"lead":"How your email marketing performed.","body":["Every Email Studio campaign sent in the period, broker blasts included: emails sent, delivered (sent minus bounces), unique opens and clicks with their rates, unsubscribes and bounces. Click a campaign to open it in the Studio.","Rep, market and type filters don’t apply here. If the Email Studio isn’t connected, the tab says so."]},"export":{"lead":"Take any report out of the app.","body":["<b>⬇ Export CSV</b> saves the report’s main table — closed deals, forecast deals, unpaid invoices, stalled deals, funnel listings, inventory, buyer activity, the scorecard, lead sources or campaigns.","<b>⎙ Print / PDF</b> prints the report with your logo, firm name and address, the report title, period and filters at the top — choose “Save as PDF” to keep a file."]},"leads":{"lead":"Is the top of the funnel growing?","body":["<b>New Leads</b> shows <b>New leads (contacts)</b>, <b>New companies</b>, <b>Activities logged</b> and <b>Total contacts</b>, then <b>Leads generated over time</b> — new contacts per day (grouped into wider bars on long windows). Hover a bar for the date and count.","<b>Full lists</b> links jump to All contacts, All companies and Agreements to filter and export from there.","The four activity tabs use their own window — <b>7 days</b>, <b>30 days</b>, <b>90 days</b> or <b>12 months</b>."]},"activity":{"lead":"Who's working the phones and the book.","body":["The <b>Activity by user</b> card has a bar per rep, split by activity type with a color legend, then a table with each rep's count per type and total.","It counts activities logged on contacts in the window, credited to the rep who logged them.","<b>Leads by source</b> groups new contacts by their <b>Lead source</b> (blank shows as Unknown). <b>New leads by rep</b> credits each new contact to the user who created it.","Both are on the <b>Rep Activity</b> tab."]},"docs":{"lead":"See which documents buyers actually read.","body":["The <b>Data-room document activity</b> card shows <b>Active rooms</b>, <b>Document views</b>, <b>Downloads</b> and <b>Active buyers</b>, then the top documents with room, views, downloads, buyers and <b>Read time</b>.","A ★ marks key documents — P&Ls, financials, tax returns, leases, rent rolls, the CIM. Views, downloads, buyers and read time all follow the window.","The <b>Buyer engagement</b> card on the <b>Data Room</b> tab lists active buyers across all rooms with docs opened, views, downloads and last seen, most recent first.","A buyer who touched a key document (financials, tax return, lease, CIM …) within the last 7 days gets a <b>🔥 HOT</b> badge, and the card counts them at the top."]},"usage":{"lead":"Adoption and access at a glance.","body":["The <b>Login & tool usage</b> card shows <b>Sign-ins</b>, <b>Active users</b>, <b>Failed logins</b> and <b>Tools used</b>, then <b>Sign-ins by user</b>, <b>Most-used tools</b> and a <b>Sign-ins over time</b> chart.","A spike in failed logins is worth a look."]}},"faq":[{"q":"Can reps see Reports?","a":"No — Reports is admin-only. Reps see their own numbers on their portal and in Commissions."},{"q":"Why does a document show views but little or no read time?","a":"Read time follows the window like everything else, but reading time recorded before this update wasn't dated, so it isn't counted in any window. Views, downloads and buyer counts were always dated, so they're unaffected."}]}};
